      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>Peter Peacock Challenge Cup. FOURTH ROUND.</head>
        <p>Peter Peacock Challenge Cup. FOURTH ROUND.</p>
        <p>This competition continues to arouse great interest. The fourth round was completed en Saturday at the Bank Quay green. The results are appended :- BEIVSEY C. TOWN HALL A. A Clare 1 II Webb l3 E Morris F Dickinson 6 J Archer W Richmond T Carroll 5 J Collins J Lennon ll A McEvoy l3 S Woodsy 6 E A Sanders l3 J Wilkinson rapt) 8 J Groark F Plurnpton 7 A Lawton l3 J Flynn ll W H Dutton l3 J Coyne l3 T Hughes 7 T H Yates 146</p>
        <p>93 Town Hall A won by 53 points,</p>
        <p>HOWLEY H. ST JOHN'S D. Yir Morgan W Yuile l2 W Critchley IS 3 Maokon lO J Woolley.. W Madden 3 Mercer G A Jenkins 0 J Senior 'l' Rigby 5 J Bennett l3 V 'Nilson J Kirkham l3 E Hill M Llewellyn (capt.) 3 Wrench l3 W Alcoca (capt) l3 T Niullaney</p>
        <p>90 St John's D won by 50 points.</p>
        <p>ST. AkEsTIN'S B. WHITEROSS Tf. J Wilkes A (Heave - J E Jefferson (capt) J Howard l3 H Prinsep H Lindley lO H Deakin l3 Alderman Tinnion W Massey 9 A Middleton ............ 13 J Middleton ........... F Rigby l3 J Howard . J Woods W Randles Seth Hunt</p>
        <p>118 St Austin's B won by 12 points.</p>
        <p>EIFFEL TOWER LEMONADE.</p>
        <p>- -- A glid.• bottlet makes 2 gallons of delicious Home-made Lemonade, produced solely from Lemons and Sugar. The Safest Summer Drink.</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER UNITY FRIENDLY SOCIETY, WARRINGTON DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>A Bowling handicap in connection with the above district will take place at the Brooklands Hotel, Lovely-lane, Warrington, to-day Saturday. The draw for this handicap took place on Tuesday evening at the Brookands Hotel and resulted as under :—Competitora will be allowed to use their own bowls. Commence 2 o'clock prompt. Games 13 up. Referee, Mr Robert Straw ; measurers, Messrs Jcseph Lowe and William Butterworth ; secretary, Mr Wm E Clare.</p>
        <p>PRELIMINARY ROUND. Wm Paskin, i v Joseph Lowe, 5 M Wilcock, 3 v Henry IN iddop, 3 Wilfred Taylor, 3 v J W Pemberton, scratch Hy Langley, 1 v Douglas Barker, 6 T J Hopkins, 3 v Robert Straw, 2 Arthur Dutton, 6 v James Burrows, 5 FIRST ROUND. Henry Langley or D Barker v J H Shelton, 4 Israel Dutton, jun, 3 v Henry Taylor, 3 Herbert Glover, 2 v George Higham, 6 W Borland, 6 v Charles Taylor, 2 Alfred Gleave, act v W Hopkins, 3 James Wrench, 3 v John Broadhurft, 3 John Hankinson, 5 v John °leave, scr Peter Lester, 6 v Joe Daintith, 3 ft H Holford, 2 v Charles Whittaker, 4 N J Clarke, 3 v H 31101?8,, 3 W A Warburton, 2 v W Webster, 6 Frederick Barnes, sen, 4 v Samuel Wray, 3 Thomas Smalley, 5 v S Dale, 3 W Dennet, 4 v James Wright 3 William Bibby, 2 v Walter Langley 1 A E Hare, 6 v 13 Ferguson 2 Frank Webster,4 v Wilfred Taylor or J W Pemberton W Challoner, 4 v James E Broadhurst 3 George F Freeman. 3 v T J Hopkins or Bob Straw A Lawton, 2 v J Finch 2 M Wilcock or Henry Widdop v George Carman Joseph Weave, 1 v William Paskin or Joseph Lowe A E Burdett, 2 v A Perry 3 Jchn Sankey, 6, v W Holbrook, 6 Joseph Riley, 4, v John Parsons, 3 Fred Owen, 1, v Henry Jones, 4 James Langley, 1, • Wilfred Dutton, 4 John E Robin, 3, v Henry Farrar, 6 Herbert Whitelegge, 6. v F Lawless, 5 A Cooper, 3, v Fred Clare, 3 J H Hurst, 4, v A Dutton or J Burrows W E Clare, 4, v Ernest Llewellyn, 4 TRAMWAY MEN AT BOWLS.</p>
        <p>On Thursday of last week the No.l turn of the employees of the Warrington Corporstiou Tramway Department held their third annual bowling main when they proceeded by char-a-banc to the Stamford Arms Hotel, Boilington which was reached about 230 p.m. They were welcomed by the proprietor, Mr, T. F. Jeffer- son, who placed his green at their disposal and bowling was commenced at once. Some very fine bowling was witnessed and at six o'clock a break was made for tee and afterwards bowling was renewed until it was nearly dark when an adjournment was made to the concert room where the prizes were given to the winners. Hearty thanks were awarder] to the donors of such valu- able prizes and the greatest credit must be given to Motormen W Warburton and E Kenwright for the way In which they had carried out their part of the pro- gramme. After the usual votes of thanks to the host and hostess the party once more embarked for home which was reached about 11 p.m., one and all claiming they had had a very enjoyable outing.</p>
        <p>2ND ROUND. H Bowyer l3 G Eastwood Job Jones l3 D Cooper G Seddon l3 W Woods 7 W Goulden Geof. Bowyer l3 R Barlow lO R Acomb R Graham l3 W Ryder W Hardman l3 J Hardmau Joe Bowyer J Hill, jun l3 J Watkin A Price l3 T Hill, sen W Nicholson W Thompson T Gregson E Dumbell J Broadhurst, sen 1</p>
        <p>TH IRD ROUND. T Oregon R Graham l3 G Seddon 8 E Dumbell G Bowyer l3 H Bowyer 7 Joe Bowyer l3 R Acomb W Thomason J Broadhurst, Jun W Goulden l3 M Hardman l2</p>
        <p>FOURTII ROUND. Joe Bowyer l3 R Graham W Thomason 'l' °reason l3 Geof Bowyer ll W Goulden SEM I-FINAL. W Goulden W Thomason FINIAL</p>
