      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>LANCASHIRE MERIT COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE MERIT COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Warrington Player in the Final.</p>
        <p>Warrington players have been vary prominent in the Lancashire County Individual Alerit Competition, the concluding stages of which took place on the green of the Roebuck Hotel, Flixten, on 6aturday. There was a large attendance of spectators, amongst whom were many Warrington enthusiasts including Alderman John Evans of the Bull's Head Hotel, SVorr.ngton. The officials drew a preliminaty round so as to obviate the necessity of giving any byes in the later rounds. E Gleave (Warrington), played a gocd game against A Elowell (Irlarrs), and although he led at 13-8, Howell peeled with 17 and at the fast end bowled a block- toucher to win 21-19.</p>
        <p>In the first round prorer .1 Gleave (Wnrrington 13.11.) bowled in excellent style Rgainst F Hasten). (Irlams) playing a short length round peg mark with grand judgment, end giving his opponent very little I chance, whilst J Jolley (Warrington B H.) gave G Johnson (Angel Hotel) ft warm time to win finally by 21-n. H Eyes (ltylands'). giined a fine win over P R Baxter (Blackpool), by 21-12 only to fall in the second round to Ci W Corner (Roebuck), the champion, by the odd couple of points. This game was a very exciting one and the local trunc ler had excep- tionally hard lines. In the second rmnd .1 Weave aain howled in great style, and in disrosing of H Holloway of Leigh, he got rid of a very doughty opponent. Holloway looked like having a chance at 18-14, but Weave stuck to his work well, and witti a run of seven put himself into the last eight. J W Jolley had all his work cut out to oust A Follows (Chorlton) for after a hart-fought game he only won by a single. In the third round the two Wsrrington players left were again successful. J W Jolly, the winner of the Warrington section. cptal.fied for the last four at the expense of J Grundy (I.lan and Scythe), after a tip-top game. They were level et 6, 12, 14, and 11, hut front this point Jolly went to 21 without. Grundy again troubling the markers The two ' yourgest players in the competition, .1 W Platt and J Gleave (Warrington 8.H.) also had o cling-dong tussle,</p>
      </div>
