      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>TEA. ON THE LAWN.</head>
        <p>TEA. ON THE LAWN.</p>
        <p>The annual ladies' bowling main in connection with the Parish Church Men's bible Class took place on Saturday afternoon, on the Iteciory green, under idea conditions and proved to be the most successful ytt held. During the interval the party numbering about 80 adjourned for tea on the lawn, which was admirably catered for by Miss Hankinson and Miss Reeves. Tea over, the tarty again entered into keen competition for the prizes, "jack touchers " now becoming the rule. The following ladies were successful :-1, Miss L Lloyd ; 2, Mrs J Carman ;3, Mies G Lawrence ; 4, Mrs J Crusham ; 5, Mrs T Hughes. Consolation prizes : 1, Miss E Rigby ; 2, Miss A Lidiard ; .3, Miss M Hankinson ; 4, Miss AI Hughes. The Rector kindly presented the prizes and in a humorous</p>
        <p>Photos Burrell.</p>
        <p>speech congratulated the winners. Mr. J. Hill pro- posed a vote of thanks, and Mr. John Ctaitr in second- ing the vote coupled with it the names of Mrs. Willis and Mr. Evelyn Willis. The vote was heartily carried. Mr. It. W. Antsis proposed a vote of thanks to the donors of priv-s, and Mr., Walter Dennett seconded. Mr. Sam Tilling proposed a vote of thanks to Miss Hankinson and Miss Reeves, and Mr. William Dennett seconded, both being carried unanimously. Mr. Evelyn Willis, on behalf of the ladies, proposed that the best thanks be given to the committee and members of the Bowling Club for providing the after- noon's enjoyment, which was heartily carried. Mr. T. Watson, hon. sec , responded on behalf of the Bowling Club.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>LADIES' BOWLING.</head>
        <p>LADIES' BOWLING.</p>
        <p>OUGHTRINGTON V' ST. CIEaRGB'S ALTRINCHAM.— A match was played at Oughtrington on rhursday between Oughtrington and St. George Altrincham, resulting in a win tor St. George's in the singles by 17 and in the doubles by 8. Scores :</p>
        <p>, OUGHTRINGTON. ST. GEORGE'S. Mrs Brown 6 Mrs Critchley 6l " Mrs McUal ... ...... 15f Mns Dale Mrs Sbelton 15) Mrs Rowbotham 1:} Mrs Priestner 11 Mrs Yarwood 1154j11 86 AallrrsaliChositnt-1 ... r. ................ 15 1 11 Mrs Cook 111055 I::: , ‘s. :1 MrsirBoßckraeireirrio ............................. 1457 111 11: Mrs Copeland Mnl Robinson Mrs Cballoner... Mrs T Travis IMss Mills Hu " Mrs Ackerley ...... 15 ) Miss Simpson 3) „ Mrs Gant..• ........ 15 1 II Mrs J Travis Mrs Shaw lll ii Mrs Latham .. . ...... 15 1 4 Miss Cl Brown lll Mrs Pritchard ...... 15 1</p>
        <p>135 63</p>
        <p>21.,3 76</p>
        <p>the former leading 11.8 when ()leave got in for a run of eight which put him in a winning position, and, playing close to the jack until the finish, he made himself a winner of a rare good guns by the narrow margin of two points.</p>
        <p>THE SEMI-FINALS. In the semi-finals Corner and Jolly met, and of course this left Joe Gleave to face W Mendesley (Rainhill), who had entered the semi-finals at the expense of J Ward, his townsman. Corner bowled in great style across the centre of the green on a mark which needed very careful watching and soon estabished a big lead, no fewer than 12 points separating the pair at the 21st end, when the card read Is-7. Jolly here got in for a run of 6, but this was his last effort, as the Roebuck player, with two good ends, put himself in the final for the first time in his career.</p>
        <p>The heat between Gleave and Mendesley caused much excitement. The young player had been showing good form during the earlier rounds, but it was thought generally that the older bowler would just about succeed in reaching the final. The expectations of the knowing ones, however, received a rude shock when, after the bowling had proceeded for nine ends, Mandesley was in arrears to the extent of 2-11, and this leeway he could never make up, Glesve securing the honour to meet Corner in the float by 7 points.</p>
        <p>Eats &amp; Woe extermthated by DANYSZ VIRUS. Non-poisonous end harmless to Human Beings, DOmestie and other Animals. Single Tube as; Tubes 11/-•' post free from:— . DANYSZ VIRUS LIMITED, (Box 216 ). Loodoeiall Street, Loudon, and of all chemists</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>FINAL.</head>
        <p>THE FINAL.</p>
        <p>When the finalists took the footers the crowd had increased to an enormous extent and we understand last year's figures were by far exceeded. Corner delivered the jack, scored a single at each of the first three ends, and these he followed up with two doubles and another single before (Heave secured a couple, the cird at the seventh end therefore reading : Corner S. Cleave 2. From now until the thirteenth end matters were evenly contested, and then Corner tacked on another half-dozen, and from 16-6 the Roebuck player I obtained the required 5 to mAke himself a very popular winner, 21-6, at the twenty-second end. It would have taken a very good man in his very best form to have made anything like alight with Corner on the game he played against Cleave. End after end he was up to the jack oftner than not with both woods,and he was heartily congratulated. The Lancashire eight eligible to travel into Yorkshire to compete for the British Crown Green Individual Championship, there- fore, are : G W Corner, .1 Cleave and.) Wi Jolly (War- rington B HI. W Alaudesley, J Grundy, J W Ward, J W Platt, and J Valentine.</p>
        <p>When Parliament acjourns Mr. and Mrs. Lloyd George will take up their residence at Printon-on•Sse, where they have taken a furnished house fora month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>LIBERAL CLUB PRESENTATION.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL CLUB PRESENTATION.</p>
        <p>The members of the Liberal Club showed their appreciation of the work done by Mr. Fred Walker, as Secretaty of the Bowling Club, by presenting him with a marble cicck and bronze ornaments, on Tues- day evening last There were present Councillor Wilkinson, Councillor F Lindley, Messrs T Pemberton, J.P., J Wilkinson, J Critchley, W Hayes and others. Councillor John Wilkinson made the presentation on behalf of the members, and eulogized the good work which Mr Walker had done and hoped that his married life would be a happy and prosperous one.</p>
        <p>Mr Walker feelingly replied, and said how happy he had been as a member of the Club, and especially since taking up the duties of Secretary the last two years. He bad made many f fiends, and if health per- mitted he %would continue to do the best he could, wherever his services were requited. He thanked 11r Joseph Wilkinson and 11. Ir W Hayes for the able manner they had assisted him during his term of (Alice. They had certainly made his work much lighter, and more enjoyahle. A very pleasant cere- mony was brought to a close by a vote of thanks to Councillor Wilkinson.</p>
      </div>
