      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>BOWLING NOTES.</head>
        <p>BOWLING NOTES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Works Association. The closing games in the third round of this c()een3; petition were played on the Horse and Jockey Gr fionaeSaatuarddamy ernes which were of a very exciting character. . aafte ahirantosornes. t The weather s wsehaowear wiaasthgeloir,aiortlibl tightest game was the one in which Whitecross won over t. Helens Cable No. Iby five points 'c',l•s. the most sweeping win was that of FletobeTvert., Moulders over Castle Rubber by 51 points. Par are some good draws for the fourth round anu can hardly speculate with any amount cf cer‘ which teams will be likely to figure in the although the chances of Fletcher's Moulders are rosy. 2. oID laYe bl</p>
        <p>.1301 yerl</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS CABLE NO 1 V WHITECROSS NO</p>
        <p>The Whitecross men were handicapped twenty I behind their opponents but nevertheless they Pi' hard game and as a result won although only narrow majority.</p>
        <p>T Stagg (Whitecross) was always the winner C Fellows whom he defeated 15-2 in 15 ends.</p>
        <p>la</p>
        <p>A similar win was recorded in favour of S (Whitecross) over J Alcock whom he defeat(' ends by 13 points. 4 t</p>
        <p>Lag od</p>
        <p>The individual games were not so very excit on six occasions the Whitecross men gained mat victories. w 0.11</p>
        <p>The best game of the match was the one bet North (Cable) and F Mills. They kept together the first to the 16th end, when the Whitecross cal a lead of 10-9. However, in a few ends he inc that to one of 13.10. North, however, got on 3 length and added his five in the four ends, thus by a couple of points after a very tight game.</p>
        <p>The other games were of a mild character. a' Whitecross men well deserve their victory, f( have played well all through the competition.</p>
        <p>FLETCHERS MOULDERS V CASTLE RUBM There is hardly another team in the whole tion which is as well situated as the Mouldel have a good handicap of 70 points, and the te well composed of able trundlers, there beiog Lit end."</p>
        <p>NEL°</p>
        <p>Fi</p>
        <p>They bad a ten point gain in the handle Castle Rubber on Saturday and only losing 0' men they gained a noteworthy 51 point victory • PO' of the Castle men hadn't a look in at all for 0°0;0 of them only scored eight points. The other g tot were not lacking in interest and one of them oissoBl4', in which W Barton (Fletcher's) was bested by A goof Up to the 17th end they ran even lengths and the 000; was 11-10 in favour of the Castle man. In tucc'd oo!'d ends Barton had added two good doubles Old wanted a point to run to game. On the 20th e0,00114' took the block and gained two double scores r" out the winner by the odd chalk. A similar game was fought between 11 (Fletcher's) and G H Darton but in this gs,,a3t, Castle man had the game well in hand at the 1%6003! when he led by 10-4 but before he could add 8-0 t'eea chalk Whitley had peeled with 10, this being „10„ 18th end. Two doubles on the next two ends 700; Darton one oil game but the Fletner's man g4lseo3'' doubles and a stogie from the 21st end and 1013 00 og f A Bandies (captain Fletcher's) had no easy t-hi (!„ with H James, although he finally won by a ctt points. James was ahead from the first end anr to orl 15th end be had a lead of 11-7 which was reducer' 400 of 13-12 at the 19th end. Randles took the JOG added his necessary three points in two ends. i 0 0 A E Burdett (Castle Rubber) had hard lifls game with Isherwood to whom he only lo 7900, points after 23 ends had been contested. 151100,, had the advantage of 13-7 at the 17th end but 4-'l3 0:1 got the jack and ran his woods well to peel wit"„e pa' the 21st end. Isherwood got his final tso on two lengths. t CPO The Moulders are very hopeful, and with .1!100.70 for the future and indeed they are deserving 0, ! for each player is a trier.</p>
        <p>HOwLEY TANNERY V. RYLANDS NO. .9 One hardly expected to see the wireworkers out of the competition in the third round but a„tte,,,,oB,r'fr the case for on Saturday they lost to Howie .170, ult by 22 points. The handicap was a very lare.,°„-t Hots leyites being in receipt of 60 points vib"-'re opponents were on the scratch mark. There we,ci three of the vvireworkers who failed to reu°l,4o amongst these being T Humphreys the caPtel lost to J Dickinson 6-15 in 14 ends. ~•ote,t-e Some of the Rylands men gained splendid istb' but the mojority of the games were evenly cunt° aro, • gir . W Ditchfleld (Howley) had to play exceedin Fob to dispose of A Stringer by 15-14 in 24 ends. 2ot e;el had a slight advantage from the first and at that onto he only wanted one point whereas his opPnuen„eo e° four. Ditchfleld got his points on the next to• Stringer being out of it by the odd one. With a lead of 14-8 at the 16th end W „Ouglvd4 (Howley) was indeed the favourite against Wr-to to but from this point of advantage he fella; 0 shoo another chalk and with three good double I an“ with in the next four ends the wireworker ran out odd chalk in his favour. A Itithevf W Langley was in his usual good ford a et" t 3 P h d 19t he won over T Middleton (Howley) he —a the ad exceedingly hard, for after having led 13-11 ntlra sot end he only scored onanother end np to the Ilihn 0.0; when Middleton peeled with 14. A single on 0 nsvi length was all the wireworker won by, the get° been a very tight one all through. st in their 9' The Howleyites have a good handicap and should go strong in future encounters.</p>
        <p>The Soacpße th* 00Cmt hnvroroeeses.rtefiDoeoAlffdfi isttt ohehwrei e ba sioso: ohackepen rani Orr men had a 5 vga. gain of itofK.:oop• 011,,ntileinroe oil tandicap and the P. and K. men played ePI win if I wbaustveh°o:ll:: 0 will he seen from the fact that the unwa re illjrcie the foundry men. J Archer was one of the successful tirie tueciryillifiliitt score to 14-11 at the 21st end i'contelit„Tt tr. been e ml hr° 'ed* foundry man got away with the jack a (lOW. Ott ryd( ygspab. mmhya aene edandi gibi:iee.th: nor) go ri en dtt eus c. r i an el: dv hi rvi eni on . it ge rg i eeS 131 0 r hi gleq IP onward career and Archer landed four .4 ods, remaining ends and won by the odd one in 4 mole . •fl hie egioe Floyd (Crosflelds) had very hard 11.1c1(10$111 die with P Glesve who showed good form a'?" vet tc'horcl. 15-14. Floyd was always a winner from Teed verf„"ole 19th end when he led 14.13. It Was imlnd he 9",0t * lines that after having led on all but eee °leave ' have been defeated on the 20th end wile° neat pair in and won by a single point. P Cowell (P and K) made no miettl,4oo ph" opposing captain, A Overend, whom he '' k r 11 ends. --- fiplde) " l''ve In his game with Woodward (Cr°s , the foer et' "0-1, tlia had matters practically all his own waY rd woodw lith 00 end he led 14-7. At this point t thee wood ill block and ran his score to 12-14 a i Whittaker jest managed to get his *villen° the next length. sell ditienA 00 J Caddick (P. a: K.) wadefeated F Howard 16-10 in 20 ends after lisi' r" every end. al of °CI was In ii7J-be best of eht'sttopg le'</p>
