        <p>iLocal trundlers are ever willing to aid any charitable "nlect and to-day (Saturday) the grand charity match ..!Zr the season will take place on the " Horse and green between Warrington Works and the ~y,t,l.edonians (assisted). The games are to commence at fo4° and the names of those chosen to take part will he 1.,1!d ill another column and it will be I 111" Y prominent gentlmen are down to 'VS' including Mr A H Crosfield and the .2isor (Councilbr Dr Joseph) Mr Harold Smith Zan was to play for the works will unfortunately be an bosentee. Ladies are especially invited and it is to be Ped a large number of ladies and gentlemen will t;end and thus help a deserving object. Crosflelds' prize BAIA will play selections during the afternoon. -- ~'t.The tenth annual main in connection with the Asso- station will be commenced (weather permitting) on ill."laY August 100. Anyone working at a works affiliated to the Association, may enter, but the °lntnittee reserve the right to, refuse any entry. The ~itrnes will be l 5 up with a limit of six. The prizes are !r9 valuable ones ;and the entries close on Monday Ight the 29th inst, names to be handed in to any men- of the of the committee or the secretary Mr. W. Patrick. !.'e draw will be published in the "Examiner" on ^,ugnSt 3rd also the time when the games will be laSed.</p>
        <p>Peter Peacock Challenge Cup. tb Th is competition which has been so interesting all r°ll/4h is rapidly approaching completion for on i vaturday four nteresting games in the fourth round el'e decided. The semi-final will take place to-day `alurday and the final will be played on August 3rd ahen the cup and watches will be presented to the tiers and runners up on the green to be followed by ife and fork tea in the pavilion. The handsome ,Pll3 and watches are on view in the window of Mr. ataeS Criicbley, Tailor, Sankey-street. The games °llSaturclizy were well contested but the best game of four was the one which St Ausiins B defeated itecross Hby 12 points. ' BEWSEY C V TOWN HALL A. - Town Hall men had little difficulty in disposing thSir opponents to the tune of 33 points. There 're only two of their men who were not successful in unaiug winning cards, and these were T Hughes and lennes...y, who were defeated by J Coyne and T H 13.7 and 13-9 respectively. PlYnn played a good game against W H Dutton /jca Hall), and after having peeled on various ends 64 10-9 at the 14th end. In two more ends the was 13-11 in favour of the Town Hall player, who good single on the next length and won by two -- ,similar game was played between J Lennon and A 'SOY (Town Hall), tor the Bewseyite only lost by Points in 17 ends, after having kept close to toy all through the game. Th tightest game of the match was the one in which 'lords (Bewsey) lost to It H Holford by the odd 14 in 16 ends. The Bewseyite led from the first to 10th end, when he had an advantage of 10-6. Half ord got, the jack and ran his score to 10-12 at the 14th .4 A single and a double on the next two ends put 4'Ord amongst the winners. l'he Town Flail men have fought hard during the r,iaitietition but they will Lave to play exceedingly It tc)-dav against Whitecross who are the favourites the final. HOWLEY D. V. ST. JOHN'S D. ,-Ina of the Howleyites were in poor form on Satur- fur it took five or their players to score 19 points klihe St. John's men enter the semi-final with a win POints to their credit. There were only three of the Howley men who won r individual games these being J Kirkham, M ellYn (cart), and J Wrench who defeated E Hill, tee attiock and W Alcock (capt), 13-3, 13-S, and 13-12 Dectively.</p>
        <p>-- The game between Wrench and Alcock was a very Iliar one for from the Tat to the 15th end Alcock t,he game well in hand and at this point of the game them 11-9. Wrench peeled on the next end with 11 &gt;et a good double. The St. Joba's man gained a e (orl the next end but on the 14th length Wrench the couple and won by the odd paint. C,etimr (St John's D) as at his best and soon put ..enkins to the right-about, for be defeated him la eight ends. -- elk (Howley) lost to W" Critchley by the odd ' alter 18 ends had been contested fairly evenly. %hley had a lead of 10-6 at the 13th end but after laote ends Tulle led by 12-11. The St. John's man a double on the next end and won 13-12. 'Other games were fairly'interesting and St. Johns "re deserving of the victory. sr. AUSTIN'S B V WHITECROSS H. St. Austins team did very well to dir,pose of the I'OES men by 12 points for the wireworkers are a team and one hardly expected them to go under turday. There were some sweeping wins in of either side hut the majority of the games ly contested. -- Hunt (St. Austins) was in good form hut after led 10-6 at the 13th end over J Rigby' he Aly lost by one point on the 21st end. Rigby ~- exceedingly well towards the finish of the game 410 his woods well on the block. a lead of 11-5 at the 11th end. I Mather was a Ite against Aldermen Tinnion (St. Austins), but. 4:1" the worthy alderman added two doubles and 7,41;1es in the next five ends thus being only 11-12 "u his opponent. Mather was indeed surprised he failed to land one in the next end and the won by one point.</p>
        <p>gßtne was one of the best which has been .Ett`"ed (luring the competition and feeling is strong It the A ct Austins men will partner Whitecross B in M. Time will tell.</p>
        <p>ST JoIIN'S B. V WIIITEChROSS tki,,,lniug on all but three games t rossmsen B. noteworthy victory over Johns by 2 were not many games which can be called keen 'este but a few are worthy of mention. b lbs °ll9 (St John's) kept even with W Bibby up to, Pro,:aat few ends when the wirework er got goint, and won by two points. t;. eschin was one of tbe few St John's who won 1,3-1,11,8 victory was over F Cleaton whom he defeated ,t,tei1.44,. 21 ends. Cleaton led 10-7 at the 15th end but gained five of the next seven points and at the. itto '4l the score was 12 each. Meechn got his one tile next end hut only by a few inches. h(3l -- Ive (St. John's) put up a good fight against +kit --Tnrt, but after having led 10-9 at the ISth end he 1,111;4 to record another chalk and the Whitecross " tan out the winner by three points. 't pp ••tq ppe''o3' (Whitecross) ought to have made his win ' aa , Lingley far more decisive, for at the 11th end aaen la,Y wanted three points to game whilst Langley "1319 scored five points. Langley got the best of lens ends and at the 16th end the game wes Woo',l'2-10 in favour of Welsby who got his winning 041 the next length. .„„ attendance is expected on the Bank Quay. to-day when the teams to meet in the semi-final ttecrOsS B v Town Hall A. "°tites D v St Austin's B. toliection will be made for the Infirmary Fund. linlaltanty games s 7.-h—e Tn—dividual merit competi- ' be played-after the semi-final games this afternoon. The games and times of starting . 51vtin in last Saturday's "Examiner." ,-rrington and District Association. •I•I2IIVIDCAL MERIT COMPETITION. • Progress was made in the Individual atr;`uulPetition promoted by the Warrington and l'et Association on the Cemetery Green on pi Satur- eTh nun_players engaged in the County tourney °7:13'04.4 were excused and the first round will be „0.•-'ad to-day. The surprise packet of the after- ' the defeat of Albert Gleave the well known 'hitietc)sa bowler by J Melbourne, Cemetery. One .I,he