      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, July, 27, 1912.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, July, 27, 1912.</p>
        <p>favour of the Conservative. From this to the 26th, end Horrabin only scored a double and with two singles and a double in the ssme ends Allison gained a lead of 18-17. At the 29t1i end Horrabin landed a single and led by 20-19. The Cemetery man got two singles in on the next ends to win 21-21.</p>
        <p>A good'ea.nre was witnesied between H Hughes Caen Conservatives) and Walter Langley (liylands). linghes was very prominent in the early stages of the game when he seemed to be able to get doubles on almost every end. Langley played a steady game to gain a lead of 14-12 at the 20th end. On a good length he ran the woods well but Hughes got a double now and then and at the :.7th end the wireworker bad a lead of IS 17. A single On the next length was Langley's final sctore and with four succet sive single FCOTPf, Hughes ran out the winner by toe odd chalk, the game having been most exciting.</p>
        <p>T Cooper (Sen. Conservatives) was succssaful in his game with G Monks (Rylands) by three points. After 17 ends, which had heen very evenly contested,the score was 11-10 in favour of Cooper. A good run on the next eight ends put the Conservative 9 points ahead of his opponent, and placed him only two points off game. Monks stuck to his work and with three donhlea and two E ingl es ran 1-111 at the 29th end. Cooper howled a good length and landed the couple thus winning by a trio of chalks.</p>
        <p>T Bridge (Cemetery) had a diffitmlty task before him to bent J Newton (Rylands) and tie only achieved his object in 31 ends by the odi one. He left. Newton well behind in the first 13 ends when he led 12-6. Newtmi took the jack in baud and at the 27th end had run his. score to 19-18. Bridge gained two singles and led 20-19. Newton caused a large amount of excitement when he peeled with 20 and taking the jack he threw ii good length which he failed to reach with his woods and Bridge got one close ot. %loci won by 21-20.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>CRICKET NOTES.</head>
        <p>CRICKET NOTES.</p>
        <p>• The recording of defeats is becoming monotonous, and it is time Warrington showed signs of extricating themselves from the rut in whicti they have found themselves of late. On Saturday they visited Ashton- under-Lyne, at d found the eleven of that town too hard a nut to crack. The batting of the Warring- tonians was very indifferent, and realized only 92. Lambe's bowling proved almost unplayable to many of the visitors, and he captured 7 wickets for 43 runs. F J Cooper made a fine attempt to place a better com- plexion on Warrington's prospects, and he batted in classy style for Iris 20. 'Fisher, the professions], put in a useful 16 and .T Callon and J S Broome each bad 11 to his credit. Warrington lacked the service of the Bev H Gray and this was to some extent accountable for the weakness of the attack. Lambe had matters all his own way and hit out well fur 52, his wickets being still undisturbed when the end of the match came. The Only Warrington howler to make anything like an impression was B S.lcock who took four alckets fur 43 runs.</p>
        <p>Warrington second gave a more exhilarat- ing display agamst Ashton second but the draw was in favour of the visitors. Batting first, Ashton declared at 220 for nine wickets, the notable scores being forthcoming from P Ileswick (53), Bolton (50 not out) and Cheetham (42). Clearly the bowling of the Warringtoniqns was far from f,.rnaidable. Warringtou put togellv-r 161 fr seven wiz•kets, J W Pearson distinguishing hian,eif with a capital innings for 40. W. V 'rurton was also in fine form and carried out his hat with 27 to his account. T Dixon</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>KING'S PRIZEMAN.</head>
        <p>THE KING'S PRIZEMAN.</p>
        <p>The King's Prize was won at Bisley on Saturday by Private A. G. Fulton, of the Queen's Westminsters. He has on four pre- vious occasions been in the final, but has never before won the prize. His success is unique in one particular, and that is he is the son of a Queen's prizeraan, his father having</p>
        <p>won the Gold Medal in 1888. Private Fulton is a Londoner, and twenty-four years of age. Young as be is, lie has won many prizes, and shot as a member of many teams. He was one of the British riflemen at the Olympic games at Stockholm.</p>
