: ... •:... .i*...3.:::iii.:. _-..:',/ . . .. . .. ......-.... • .. ...... :.....: 0</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <p>knocked up 22; and G Woodcock, the Botelerian who promises to do great things in the cricket world, made 21.</p>
        <p>The most decisive win in the Warrington and District League tourney wav secured by Cairo-street Unitarians ho trounced the C.ble Co in unmerciful fashion. The Cable men could make no headway against the bowling of Plinston aud Hodgson and were all sent to the right about for 14. The Unitarians replied with 129 for the loss of only five wickets, G H Hodgson heading the acmes with a brilliant not out innings of 59. W Mehlor was also in great form with the bat and compiled 44.</p>
        <p>Warrington Conservatives had an easy tisk in dis- posing ot Orford. \V Bell and B Green were the destructive agents and the villagers were dismissed for 15. The Conservatives put together 47 for eight wickets It Hewitt being the most successful batsman with 20 to his credit.</p>
        <p>Sankey added another to their list of victories, Walker's Brewery, who have on only one match this season, being the latest to succumb to the prowess of the leaders. There was nothing very notate in the scoring on either side, the chief contributors to Sankey's total of 63 being Gregson (13) and ltylance.(l2).</p>
        <p>Wire opposed wire in the match between Rylands Recs and Whitecross, the Church-street men winning with six wickets in hand. The Bank Quay contingent were all out for 50 and ltyland's passed this total by four runs, with six wickets still to fall. A Renwick did conspicuous services for the winners, carrying out his bat with 21 to his account. J Hallam] was the main- stay of Whitecross but he had the misfortune to be run out when his snore had reached 20. Rylands record for the season now stands at : Played 8, won 5, lost 2, drawn I, runs for 500, against 272.</p>
        <p>The League table to date is as follows : P. W. L. D. Pts. Sankey ll . 8 ... 2 ... 1 ...17 Warrington Contervatives 10 7 ... 2 ... 1 ...15 Whitecross Cairo-street ll ... 6 ... ... 1 ...13 Rylands St Helens eible lO ... 5 ... 5 ..-. 0 _lO Orford Wilderspool Walkers -- The match between Bylands' Recreation and Wilders- pool Brewery is not included in the above table.</p>
        <p>In the Warrington and District Sunday School League, Orford St. :John's created another record by dismissing Bold-street for 5, thus going one better than Workingmen's Mission, who got the same opponents out for 6. Orford have now placed two of the bigbest scores in the League to their credit-133 and 140, the latter being the record. McGarr continues to prove his superior powers as a bowler, having captured over fifty wickets for Orford this season. J Reardon (30) cc as the highest scorer in Saturday's match.</p>
        <p>Workingmen's Mission were triumphant over Walton Juniors, who found the boeling of Corlett and Lloyd o much for them. There were no double figures on either side.</p>
        <p>A friendly encounter between Emmanuel Institute and Latchford Park resulted invictory for the Institute by 30. S Hughes howled and batted spendidly, taking four wickets for 0%0 runs, and compiling a not out innings of 22.</p>
        <p>St. Mary's disposed of Itylands' 2nd, M Kay taking six wickets for 15 runs. For ltylands Barton bowled well for 17.</p>
        <p>There was a keen struggle between Golborne-street Baptists and Bank-street Mission, the Baptists having to admit defeat by the narrow margin or 4. Wright was the top scorer for Bank-street with:2B,Freemau (12) batting well for the losers.</p>
        <p>Winwick Asylum defeated Patricroft Methodists by 14 runs ; due to the fit e bowlin,, of C Dawson, who accomplished the bat trick and took five wickets for 16 Tune; and to J Walker who scored 10. The top scorer for Patricroft was W Stafford with 33 not out.</p>
        <p>CRICKET ANECDOTE. G Barlow tells a good tale of a man coming into his shop and asking, " Do you keep a full supply of cricket rt q tes ?" " Certainly," said Barlow. Then let me have a bottle of arnica, threepennyworth of court plaster, two yards of bandaging lint, and an arm sling. I'm p 153 log against Jack Crossland this after- noon."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <p>FOUND. THE GLAD EYE—and it was Mother's. No wonder she was pleased. She had just proved what a blessing Steedman's Powders are to Parents. Nothing else keeps children so fit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>CYCLIST'S NOTE-BOOK.</head>
        <p>THE CYCLIST'S NOTE-BOOK.</p>
        <p>[By " Silver Street.']</p>
        <p>ALDERLEY PARK.</p>
        <p>What fortunate folks Wanington cyclists are ! A mile of toc%n tiding and there we are in the cyclist's Paradise—Cheshire ! For although several of the English counties can boast more varied scenery, of hill and valley, of rocky coast or boundless moorland, ye: no c,uniy authority has more definitely and delibei ately planned to bttract visitrrs by the road. Ever since the old velocipede's days the roads of Cheshire, level as a rule, have had special attention from the road surveyor, until it is the modile de luxe of cycling counties, and has been more visited, and written about, and praised, and con.e(ineatly dotted with inns and rest-hous(s than any other county without excep- tion.</p>
        <p>ALd one of the chief attractions is its endless succes- sion of gentlemen's parks, some thrown open to the public, and some—not In the course of a good deal of correspondence regarding the granting of one society or another permission to spend afternoon or evening in such and such a park, the character of its owner has been revealed very emphatically : " I ben to inform you that -- Park is not open to the public on such a date.</p>
        <p>Yours -- steward."</p>
        <p>Such notes are not the Jule. As a rule gentle- men instruct the stewards to grant permission at once. What must be said, then, of the Duke of Westminster, whose splendid domain we, especially cyclists, may all share, all the time ; of Mr. Egerton Warburton, whose lovely park at Arley was open to cyclists until some hare-brained selfish bounders insulted him, and even now we have almost the full run of the park if we die- msunt and leave the cycles where we are going to have tea; and of Lord Sheffield whose noble park at Alderley he throws open to the public every Saturday afternoon, employs a band to play on the lawn in front of the house, and tells off one of the park keepers to see to the safety of the cycles left at the entrauce to ele gardens, while the ovvne/s go and watch or join in the dancing. It was to this scene that the Wesleyan Cycling Club were introduced on Saturday afternoon. Sixteen r 4 de from Latchford Swing Bridge at 2.45 via Knutsford to Mobberley where an excellent tea was served by Mrs Pollit at Knolls Green, the caterer for the C.T.C., whose sign of the winged wheel is dis- played in the garden. The road from Kt,utsford all the way to inbberley i 3 at present very faulty, but the way forward to Alderley Edge is excellent. Tne park was reached at 5 o'clock and one or two of the party joined the dancers set on the well shaved lawn. The whole scene was charming, but the most pleasing was a little mite of seven or eight who had come with her father and mother and others in a motor. She had evidently been trained on the sta,le, for her movements were such as only an experienced dancer could execute. The back ground of it all was the rose garden, just now in full beauty, and tie pergolas bung with rambler and brier roses of a score of different shades or forms put the finishing touch on one of the prettiest scenes it has ever been my lot to enjoy. At 7.30 the National Anthem gave the signhl for the closing of the park,and the parry made its way to the Eagle Gate at Monk's Heath 011 the Macclesfield-road, from whence a quick run of 19 miles through Knutsford was made home- wards. Other Warrington Clubs please copy. THE PEDER,EN 3-EPEED GEAR.— I was asked some time ago now if I knew of a 3-speed gear that gave a better choice of gears than the Sturmey Archer or the Armstrong. lam glad to say I do, for lam riding an All Black B.S.A. machine that was fitted at the B.S.A. factory with the above mechanism. As the cranks are eight inches long the normal (middle) gear is 64, the upper gear is .50 per cent, higher, that is 126, and the lower 50, a decrease of 33 per cent. ; the Pedersen people make an increase of 50 per cent, from the low Ito the middle, and again a :50 per cent, from that to the high gear. Thus the normal gear is to the crank length as 10} to 1 ; on the 56 gear with such a leverage as the Sin. cranks give,,, you can climb practically any hill there is, whilst on a long down bill, or on a level with the wind behind, you experience the joys of cycling. The protruding case that revolves around the back I hub enables the gear wheels to be made of a size and strength that cannot be doubted ; it is perhaps a little ! unusual in aspect, though I cannot allow it is unsightly, and its revolutions are absolutely imperceptible to the rider. In fact, the advantages are so great that it is the only 3-speed gear that I shall look at, of all those that are made at present. This is the second machine that I hare had fitted with it, and have recommended it to several friends, all of whom are impressed with its superior design and workmanship. The Hub is made at parsley in Gloucestershire, so that the foreign name of the invetor need not terrify even the most intense patriot.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>BEETLES AND THEIR SIX LEGS.</head>
        <p>BEETLES AND THEIR SIX LEGS.</p>
        <p>As a change of subject may I suggest a topic for five minutes' close study during these hot months crowded with flower and insect life. Everyone knows, since they have read "The Bishop and the caterpillar," that such creatures are blessed with six legs. t o has a beetle, and all other insects. In fact, no creature is properly regarded as an insect unless it has this magic number of six. But how many of you cyclists (males only, for in these days of hobble skirts, ladies are not supposed to know any- thing about subjects of this kind) can tell in what order a beetle uses those six legs? It will perhaps impress you with the beauty and gracefulness of these lower animals if you find out for yourself, and especi- ally when you remember how hard it is to dispose of only four limbs, sometimes, especially when you run over a collie.</p>
        <p>Lamp time to-night 9.15 ; next Saturday, 9 1. STOCKTON HEATH ST. THOMAS'S CYCLE CLUB.</p>
        <p>On Saturday last the above club, by the kind per- mission of Mr. Warburton, held their annual picnic at Arley Hall. A party of 40 friends, in waggonettes, together with GO cyclists, left the Square at 230. and duly arrived at Arley about 4 o'clock. They immediately adjourned to the spacious and beautiful gardens, and expreesed their keen delight at all they saw. An excellent tea was then served by several of the lady friends, and during the progress of same, on the motion of Mrs Hallows, a hearty vote of thanks wa4 proposed by Mr. W. Cleaton, and seconded by Mr. P. Maddock, to Mr. Warburton for his great kind- nesses and the privileges he had extended ; also to all those who had worked so hard and willingly for the outing.—This motion was heartily carried by all present, who afterwards adjourned to amuse them- selves in the various games contained in the grounds. The return journey was commenced at 7.39 via Great Budworth, and Stockton Heath was safely reached at 9 o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>WARRINGTON CHILDREN'S CAMP.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CHILDREN'S CAMP.</p>
        <p>The most exciting event of the year for the children who ale able to go, is the visit to the camp at Prestatyn. One hundred and twenty-seven left War- rington on Saturday morning in charge of Mrs. Hill, Misses Emson, Jefferson, Morley and Wainwright, and arrived at camp about 12.30. After each child had been shown her sleeping quarters and given a good meal, all went away to the shore. The streams of delight at the sight 'of the beautiful sea would have cheered anyone who has helped to send the children. The afternoon was spent in piddling and making sand castles. Sunday was rather showery, so singing and reclining under the verandah of the charming bungalow given by Lady Greenall, with occasional short walks, was the order of the day. Monday and the remainder of the days have seen visits to the pierrots, with paddling on the shore, &amp;c. One day the bigger girls had a lovely walk through the woods to llyserth. The parties never failed to return ready for the good meal provided by Mrs. Quinn. The end of the week came all too soon but when the children arrived at War- rington last evening it was evident Iron their bronzed and happy appearance that they had derived much good from their brief sojourn by the sea.</p>
        <p>CIIRISTIAN ENDEAVOURERS' SOCIAL.—On Monday night last there was an excellent attendance at the Primitive Methodist Church when the members of the Senior Christian Endeavour Society held a social even- ing. Owing to the inclemency of the weather, the function, which should have been held in the wood at the back of the church took place in the schoolroom, where the party enjoyed games which included fortune telling and a new stile of arms to the musical chairs. Light refreshments were also participated in. Miss Greta Welford kindly sang " The rose garden, '' and in response to the many demands for an encore, the song "Because." The Rev RW B Whitecvay closed the meeting.</p>
        <p>Wharton Presbyterian Church near Walkden is cele- brating the 250th anniversary of its formation. The church was one of the churches in Lancashire estals- lished as the result of the Five Mile Act.</p>
