        <p>A HISTORY OE WARRINGTON NONCONFORMITY.</p>
        <p>Forthcoming Articles by Mr. A. Mountield.</p>
        <p>Next month Warrington Noncon- formity will reach its 250th anniversary. In recognition of this date, Mr. Arthur Mounfield has prepared an account of the early years of local Nonconformity,which will appear in due course in our columns. The anniversary falls upon St. Bartholo- mew's Day, August 24th, and we have arranged to publish the articles during the month of September. An account will be given of Robert Yates, the ejected Rector of Warrington, who became the first Nonconformist minister. Many interesting documents will be quoted and the history of local churches will be traced. As no history of Warrington Noncon- formity has yet been attempted we think these articles will have more than ordinary interest to a large number of " Examiner " readers. The first article will appear on Satur- day, August 31st.</p>
        <p>The Mauretania and the Adriatio, which arrived at New York on Friday, both encountered icebergs during their passage, and the former steamed south Out of her course to avoid danger,</p>
        <p>The momentous Bp, THE NAVY AND delivered by the Fii THE FUTURE. Lord of the Admirah in the House of Cony mons this week is the subject of world-wide interest and of grave thought on the part' every one in this country. It proves, 0' think it may be claimed, that although theret are differences of opinion as to the e/tac standard of naval strength required guard our own shores, and to protect our Dominions and commerce, there is no differ' ence of opinion between the two great partiee`. in the State as to the need for the mainterl",! ance of our undoubted supremacy, if arlr one ever had any doubt about that the' speeches in Parliament this week, and Par ticularly the pronouncement of gr. CHURCHILL, must have swept away that doubt. The facts which have to be faced are grave facts, facts calling for a still increasing expenditure upon the instruments of war, which is regrettable, but which is essential both for the protection of our own interests and to ensure the peace of the world' About one other thing there is general agree- ment. Mr. CHURCHILL has faced the situation in a spirit of frankness which is greatly to be admired, and which must bale the best results. He talked to GermanY as„. he spoke, but he talked neither in arrogant' nor aggressive terms. Germany, it is nil clear than ever, is the dominating factor the circumstances which guide our nas policy, and must continue to guide it, and ii is well that the mission of intimating tl should have fallen to Mr. CHURCHILL, sic was able to intimate it in terms which one strong nation talking to another si, nation about strenuous preparations for sol object which is open to mistrust. Perhi his words convey more than the deeds wl are to be accomplished under the Sir , mentary Estimate it was his mission explain. For let it be confessed there '5 nothing very startling in what is to be CO ' passed by the proposed additional expenditnr: i The FIRST LORD'S speech was very ninel more interesting as an outline of our naTs, policy as a whole than as an exposition how £990,000 islto be spent. For once in the history of the Admiral 4 the invariable rule of never revealing the - building programme of a succeeding year we . departed from. Mr. CHURCHILL framed 311 elementary Navy Law for this country in the matter of armoured construction. He di.,5,- closed the annual proposals down to 19164" making it quite clear that these proposals were a provisional response to the presuroPi , tive normal continuity of the German Navaf ! Amendment Act. The 60 per cent. margin .° Dreadnought superiority laid down by hitn,lll • March last is to form the standard of calcula; tion. Our naval position in the Mediterranea , is to be recognised on a footing of great' strength than it has enjoyed since the Palinif days when this was the premier station the British Fleet. Gibraltar,with its snaeln4e _ docking accommodation and impregnan,e defences, is to form more completely tu • ht ' key to the Middle Sea than ever before. of the most effective pre-Dreadnought battli,e; ships will supplant the six vessels ef tt' ' t, sed, erstwhile Atlantic Fleet, formerly alternatively upon the Rock. The strategl full " advantages of Malta are to be utilised in ad' measure, and the presence of a mobile scln . ron of battle-cruisers, of immense stringa power, coupled with the establishment an_ strong torpedo base at Alexandria, will re of der our position in the Mediterranean one e potentiality. Five more Dreadnoughts 91. tie to be added to the fully-commissioned bat o : divisions within the next two years,w-ithllt, the correspondingly reducing the strength 0' nucleus squadrons. The eight light armour cruisers, a type new to the Navy, are ,''' _a _. brought forward as rapidly ' as possible, an__ - great developments are foreshadowed in cell , the nection with naval aviation to make ll'.o t leeway which Great Britain may have by following the doctrine of looking beNre leaping—or, rather, before flying. So much for the material side 0 as a CHURCHILL'S statement, and the nation iencY. whole,will rest well assured of its suffie the t Coming to the matters which affect personnel, there is rather more sooPe fßcer" , mixed criticism. Fifteen hundred 0 ent in" ba' , and men are to be added to the establish _ In view of the heavy demands which d trairr' , been imposed upon the recruiting an ~,, few ing services by the expansion of the 1)307 ,tit rears, this is perhaps far enough to g whether it is far enough for the needs ()lope' h. Navy is quite another matter. It is an ~ r tleet secret that a very large number ot-Lturo Reservemen are to be invited to e</p>
