        <p>1° continuous regular service. If the 11')es13°11se to the favourable terms offered way of inducement is satisfactory .L'ue manning difficulty will be met, but met by an expedient that will leave .°lr first line of reserve correspondingly ,Weaker, and 1-ius find us in no better position 'or Meeting the human wastage of a great War. With regard to the burning question of iraProving .the pay and prospects of the ',Ovver-deck, the FIRST LORD is somewhat i'llsalMointing. That he is quite sympathetic undoubted. That he would do very much ,Inore than he has done if he could is also °eYond question. But good intentions are a 11°°r Vbstitute for disappointed expectations, alld Mr. CHURCHILL will be judged in this ,41atter not by what he might have wished 1;0. do, but rather by the negative degree of : actual fulfilment. In short, we must ait and see, resting satisfied with a definite ar°lllise of a proposal upon the subject in the t.ilturan. Here, at any rate, is something :tagible upon the one subject which, of all hers embraced by the FIRST LORD in his 4'64,sterly speech, probably excited most 'erest in the Navy itself.</p>
