        <p>Our gentle chaff of the lIAROLD'S BAD Member for Warrington TEMPER. anent his non-appear- ance at the Old English I seems to have touched Mr. Smith's tibilities in a rather tender place, by the masterly exhibition of invec- Which appeared over his signature in „columns of our contemporary last Satur- We did but suggest, in a vein which iondly imagined as humorous, that there Possibly some confusion between the Court and Earl's Court, as the ,cement was made on the opening day e Fair that Mr. Smith's absence was o attendance in the High Court,whereas Manchester Guardian" in a report of old English tourney at Earl's !Court the day, alluded to Mr. F. E. Smith's place taken by his brother. The circum- • or coincidence, seemed to us 'iently odd to require an explanation tto afford scope for the gentle chaff to 'en Mr. Smith has taken exception in ,violent terms. We are glad to learn Smith's absence from the fete was the reason which was publicly announced for no other." The fact too, that tP,resence at the tourney was not required 10.30 p.m. lends colour to his proud 411,1? that "he hoped to assist two big cavit,;es, although this involved nine hours t}3lo:",lng the same day." We presume ill'tga Mr. Smith is not definite upon the ) that he was not compelled to under- nine hours travelling, because his 10 in the High Court prevented a journey arring ton. The explanation is one which aFe happy to accept and one which we id have been pleased to place before ,iner " readers if Mr. Smith had been .'ons enough to send his letter to us for bileation. urtesY was not foremost in Mr. Smith's , however, and we are beginning to that he has not yet outlived an early ition to be rude, and even vulgar, when ted to criticism. When he fought ersfield in 1909 Mr. Smith got cross a newspaper there called " The !liner" largely because he was chaffed by 3ournal about his speech on Tariff Reform nnier than his entertainment farce to,4rch Hare." Mr. Smith's retort to the • Was characteristically refined and Le• ." Fill your tiny squirt with venom," e? and" make all the money you can nkling it where it pays best." And this, to think that we are charged rowing mud! I imagine that when we bury the tElaminer shall still find the corpse throvaing mud." lat a lurid idea this is. Somehow it :lot lead us to think that Mr. Smith's ;Dtion of our far-distant obsequies is as a*.te as is his knowledge of mud- g. Of the latter he seems to possess hand experience, but in regard to the he indulges in vain illusions. If Mr. does not add to the gaiety of nations, e, ertainly contrives to brilliantly 'nate the personal side of politics, and gre t to note his disposition to lose a not eqUable temper. , Strike and strike and MINIMUM yet again strike is in WAGE. the news. Everywhere the workers complain cost of living with which wage does not keep pace at all. The strikes 'SS or threatened are of such pro- that only national governments can them. They can't deal with them repression, Government must try else. There is but one thing to told. That is the establishment irnum wage. It may have to be ice so many people cannot think any than that device; but the minimum 11 fail. The minimum wage is only a liev,,,ttry stop-gap, which will temporarily iitions only to make them worse "allY. There cannot be successful egulation by law, while conditions the law cannot touch operate bIY to lower wages. The best thing lent can do is to make opportunity for every man. It can do this only Jking the land for the use of the Without their paying tribute to That once done, the Government • a ,othing better than let wages alone. eve_i°b Open to everybody, wages will go ti..'Yyqthere. Wages will go up because Will be in demand, and they will not ,ty t the expense of capital, for with cre", free labour at work they do nothing e capital. The minimum wage ,ti !lo Lwlll not work in a world where there .14914"1111rilrnization of monopoly of the only Irectl„P°n which labour can be exercised or indirectly—the land.</p>
