        <p>V 0.., The decision of the t,REmPTORY British Medical Associ- bEOISION• ation at Liverpool to INt boycott the National tAlle 111-11ce scheme will be deeply regretted by :4s 0 friends of the profession. The ',vea i`b,lou is a powerful Trade Union, but It has the power—and some of the 'ssile "est members believe it has not—to 9141 4ri ultimatum to the Government with iss'oi ng grave injury to its owninterests, such : hoilo deDlorable that a profession Moirable tradition and record o should t" methods of the highwayman \'vh• °Ur rnone our life "—to a scheme origin t, y. or i y rav-.;" Its gin n the desire to mitigate g.ges of sickness which are one of the hwas -teoliive for considerable adjustment room of unemployment. There I.l,eroom :01.1,-the demands of the Jissociation and t°etOrr Ws of the Government. But the c)rils,. for Trade Union has voted peremp- achih m°Nvei,``erlr for adjustment. No body of men, the breaking-down of the useful and whatever their prestige, 11Q1 art's. to alienate public sympathy by '.rarY conduct.</p>
