        <p>Avery practical 'and A CONDITION OF crushing comment upon VOTE-GETTING. Mr. Austen Chamber- lain's at tempt to convict the Government of" log-rolling " in order to pass the Home Rule Bill comes from Crewe. Mr. Austen Chamberlain declares that" the condition of getting Welsh votes for Irish Home Rule, which Welshmen loathe, is that Irish votes should be given for the Disestab- lishment of the Welsh Church." But the " Unionist " candidate at Crewe has been careful to explain that "he is not fighting the election on the Home Rule question." Appar- ently the condition of getting Crewe votes for a Unionist candidate is that he should not attack Home Rule!</p>
        <p>THE MANDATE FOR Mr. Asquith last week THE PARLIAMENT had the happy experi- ACT. ence of being the first</p>
        <p>British Premier to carry to Ireland a message of reconciliation between two democracies which, while separated by the sea, have been far more seriously separated by artificial barriers: To the round of deputations which met him in Cheshire on his return, he made a declaration which, while it was after all only a lucid and cogent summing-up of the actual situation, evoked no less enthusiasm than his mission of pacification to Ireland. As Mr. Asquith told his hearers, "we did not spend two years of strenuous fighting, we did not go through two General Elections, at which twice we got the emphatic approval of the electors of the country, for nothing. When you, twice in twelve months, declared your- selves in favour of that great measure of political reform, the Parliament Act, you did so with the knowledge and the intention that it should be used to carry into effect those great measures of self-government for Ireland the establishment of religious equality in Wales, and the simplification of the franchise law, on which the energies of the House of Commons are now being concentrated."</p>
