      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>NOTES AND NO7ITqI</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NO7ITqI</p>
        <p>"Is is hard to see how anyone can doubt that Free Trade will ultimately be the policy and practice of this country, and of all other enlightened nations."—The Secretary to the Treasury, U.S.A, * * "A Nation in Arms "—that blood-curdling melodrama, by Mr. B. S. Townroe—was produced at a matinee at the Savoy Theatre in London, on Thursday. The Standard, the Tory organ, states: "A plain story of invasion by a determined enemy would have suited better the playwright's end. Such incidents as serve to bring down the curtain on" A Nation in Arms" belong more properly to the old Adelphi melodrama." * * *</p>
        <p>We hear that the -.Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee have granted permission to the Million Penny Fund Committee to use Victoria Park as an alighting ground for an aviator who is to visit Warrington at an early date This will compensate for the disappointment occasioned by the „non- appearance of Mr Ewen at the recent Old English Fair. A small charge will be made for admisSion and the proceeds will go to the Infirmary.</p>
        <p>Last Saturday, at Sir William Lever's invitation,Mr A H Crosfield went to Thornton Hough to join in the welcome given to the Prime Minister. Last evening he was dining at the Palmerston Club under Sir John Simon's presidency, and afterwards attending the reception given by the, Liberal Party to the Irish Party at Surrey House. This latter is, we believe, the first public social gathering between Liberal and Nation- alist M.P.'s.</p>
        <p>The presence of M. A. IL Crosfield and Mr. G. Caradoc Rees at the open-air meeting on Monday evening should prove a big attrac- tion. Mr. Rees, a Liverpool barrister, is a first-class speaker, and is well remembered for the gallant fight he made at the last election to win bacli Denbigh Boroughs. It is a matter of history that he only failed by nine votes in a particularly hot contest. Rumour is now busy with his name as the possible Liberal candidate for East Car- marthenshire, in the vacancy caused by the death of Mr. Abel Thomas, M.P. * *</p>
        <p>Sir James Barr, the President of the British Medical Association, at Liverpool on Thurs- day said that speaking as a non-abstainer, he did not know of any disease where the patient would not get on better without alcohol than with it. His experience was that the less alcohol everybody took the better. Professor Mott, Charing Cross Hospital, endorsed the view of Sir James, and yet members of the Warrington Board of Guardians object to scientific temperance lectures on alcohol being delivered to the children of Padgate Industrial schools! * * *</p>
        <p>Much to the gratification of the people living or employed near the Mersey opposite Victoria Park, the Town Hall, Parks and Baths ICommittee have 'just provided three swans for the ornamentation of the river. Since the untimely end of "Peter" one swan had been left in solitary possession of the water, but she will now have a mate, an adult bird having been purchased to take the place of the lamented "Peter." The other two are young birds. It is the intention of the committee ultimately to transfer the swans to the lake, which is to be constructed in Victoria Park. * *</p>
        <p>Immingham, which- the King and Queen visited on Monday, though our newest British port, is a very ancient parish, and mentioned in Domesday. It was a seaport of some significance during the days of King John, who came to grief in this neighbour- hood; and it was from the derelict harbour that the Pilgrim Fathers, when foiled at Boston, began their flight to America by way of Holland. Discovery of a forgotten deep- water channel that comes close in shore has lent fresh life to the long defunct East Anglian port. The new dock, for which King George has performed the opening ceremony, is the largest on the East Coast, and capable of admitting the largest vessels at any state of the .tide, an advantage not n ' enjoyed by Hull or Gmsby.</p>
        <p>A correspondent vouches for the authen- ticity of the following incident—Scene : Open space by Farmer's Stores. "Workman Jim" addressing about 50 of us on Tariff Reform. He proposes a vote of censure on the Liberal Government in connection (I believe) with the purchasing of some leather for Navy use from Germany. "Those in favour, say Aye" One man does so, and immediately looks as though he wishes he hadn't. " Against ". No one troubles to speak. Workman Jim : "The Ayes have it. I shall ask the secre- tary to see that the vote of this meeting is sent on to the Member for Warrington." Old man in audience indignantly shouts "One Aye! One Aye !". A budding Tory, just arrived on the scene, thinks the old man is passing remarks on the speaker's personal appearance, and waxes indignant accord- ingly. " What a game it is," as Mr. B ottemley would say.</p>
        <p>Members of the anatomical section of the British Medical Association (which has been meeting in Liverpool this week), were much interested in a young man, introduced to the assembly on Thursday by Dr. Manson, or Warrington, the organs of whose body were transposed. His heart, liver, and stomach are on the opposite side of the body to that on which they are in normal persons. A peculiar circumstance of the young man's case is that he is not left-handed. He is quite healthy, is in work at the present time, and left his employment in order to attend the meeting of the section. The young man is a member of the, Territorial Force, and he is physically well-developed. Before he left, a number of X-ray photo- graphs of his body were taken. *</p>
        <p>- There is every probability that the Warrington Corporation's sanatorium at Great Sankey will be used for the treatment of consumptives under the National Insurance Act. The other day the members of the local Insurance Committee accom- panied the Health Committee on a visit of 'inspection and were much impressed with the suitablity of the Institution for the pur- pth-es of the Act. Terms have been discussed but not yet settled, as to the contribution to be paid by the Insurance Committee for the use of the building. It is a source of gratification that Warrington is one of the comparatively few towns already pre- pared to administer the Act in its entirety and the sanatorium benefits will be forth- coming immediately they are required. * * *</p>
        <p>How times change is illustrated in a very interesting way by an extract which the " Times " gave on Thursday from the "Times" of July 25th, 1812, just one hundred years ago. The extract says : Our Navy, that pride and boast of every true Englishman, might admit ( f great reduction in the present annihilation of the em-my's fleet. What a position! Britannia 100 years ago did, indeed, rule the waves; she ruled them alone and undisputed. To-day our supremacy iv still maintained, it is true, but at what an awful cost. And instead of being in a position to study proposals for reducing our fleet, because there is no opposing fleet, we are about to enter upon a programme of ship- building the like of which has never been known before, in order to maintain our advantage. At the beginning of the last century Trafalgar brought the crisis to an end. What is to be the end of the present crisis asks the "Evening News."</p>
        <p>* - Amid all the other changes which they have adopted the Japanese have preserved one tradition intact—reverence for their Emperor. The devotion of the Japanese to their Monarch is of a kind unknown in the Western world. Even the mostisuperstitious subjects of the Czar scarcely hold their Emperor in quite such awe and reverence. Down to 1867, and even later, no subject might look upon his Majesty, who lived his life apart, in the seclusion of his palace. All that was seen of him by those who waited upon his commands was his back. When the rule was first modified to the extent of leav- ing the palace all shutters had to be put up, all blinds drawn, and crevices covered over with paper, and no one was allowed in the streets. Changes have been introduced since then, but it is still not considered quite proper by the masses of the people to look at his Majesty as he is driving through the streets. *</p>
        <p>It is interesting to note the different lines adopted by successive speakers on such occasions as the Grammar School Speech Day. For homely, practical advice which will stand the boys in good stead in their after-school careers, the address delivered by the Bishop of Liverpool on Thursday has probably never been excelled in the long history of these functions. It had none of the oratorical trimmings with which it is the custom of many public men to embellish their utterances, but was couched in the simplest of terms. Every sentence contained home truths, and the direct- ness and Cogency of the address appeared to call forth a real response from the scholars. The Boteler Grammar School is worthily maintaining its proud traditions, and the past year has produced another fine record of successes on the part of present pupils and old Botelerians. The usefulness of the Willcocks Memorial Fund has just found striking demonstration, the trustees having granted £35 to the head boy, G. Woodcock, who is about to enter upon a University career, having obtained a mathematical exhibition at Cambridge. Woodcock, who is a very promising cricketer as well as student, received his elementary education at St. Barnabas' School and gained admission to.the Grammar School by winning a scholarship.</p>
        <p>LOCAL PARS.</p>
        <p>Warrington .and District Billiard League.</p>
        <p>Clubs wishing to join the above League are requested to send in their applications to the secretary T L Crompton, 23, Orford-road, Warrington, not later than Wednesday, August 7th, 1912.</p>
        <p>The Citizens' Guild of Help. The workers connected with the Guild of Help are making an excursion to Pickmere to-day. Brakes will leave Arpley Cannons at 2.30 promptly, driving via Lymm and returning via Great Budworth.</p>
        <p>Four New Police Sergeants. The Watch Committee at their meeting on Thursday, on the recommendation of the Chief Constable, made the following promotions, viz:—Constable TR Dennett, Detective H A Woollecott and Constable J lienwright, to be uniform sergeants, Detective D C Holland, to be detective-sergeant, Detective J A Metcalfe to be war- rant officer, with rank of acting sergeant, and Con- stables Newey, Whitby and Hewitt to be acting sergeants. FIRE GRATES.—If you want a new grate our large and varied stock will interest you.—J. S. GARLAND, Tronmonger, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Church. The preacher at Wycliffe Congregational Church to-morrow will be Mr. F. Chalmers Rogers, MA., senior student of the Yorkshire United College. Mr. Rogers is the son of the Rev. Stanley Rogers, of Liverpool, and grandson 'Of Dr. Guiness Rogers, who preached the opening sermons when Wycliffe Church was first opened. He is looked upon as a most promising son of a distinguished family. - - -</p>
        <p>County Council and Sanatoria. At the Lancashire County Council meeting next Thursday the chairman of the Public Health Com- mittee will move "That this committee be authorised to negotiatiate with authorities having the manage- ment of sanatoria with a view of making provisional arrangements for the curative treatment of Lanca- shire cases of pulmonary tuberculosis." It is proposed, as areas for the tuberculosis dispensaries, to divide the administrative county into eleven districts with populations ranging from 115,576 to 214,542. Women's Suffrage Garden Party. On Thursday afternoon, at Miss Massy's, Greenbank, Latchford, a garden fete was held under the auspices of the Warrington Branch of the National Union of 'W omen's Suffrage Societies. The weather was beautiful, and about 50 guests were entertained in the charming garden with croquet, clock golf and other lawn games, for which prizes were given. During the afternoon Miss C. Leadley Brown, of Liverpool, who had just come from the Crewe bye-election, spoke on the need of the vote as a protection to women and the effect it would have on women's wages. Miss Crosfield and Miss L. Broadbent also spoke, and several peop'e became members of the Warrington Society.</p>
        <p>Music in the Park. The Warrington Borough Prize Brass Band will play in the Park on Sunday. Conductor, Mr. T. Griffin. Progressive Labour League. Special attention is directed to the open-air meeting to be held at Bridge Foot on Monday evening, under the auspices of this League. The principal speakers will be Mr. A. H. Crosfiela and Mr. Caradoc Bets (late candidate for Denbigh Boroughs). If wet, the meeting will be held in the Co-operative Hall. Musical Succsses. Vera M Gonad!, Barbara W Jeans, and Frances M Rowse, pupi:s of Miss Mary Gomall, passed the Primary Division Local School Examination of the Associated Board of the Royal Academy of Music and Royal College of Music at the examination held this month in Liverpool. Art Students' Successes.</p>
        <p>The following ale the successes gained by students from the School of Art in the national competition, 1912: Bronze medal, Arthur Hayward, design for mosaic tablet. Commended, Margaret E. Clare, design in outline with tinted ground ; Richard A Collinge, study of drapery arranged on an antii:,-ue figure; Reginald C Essenhigh, modelled study of flowers and foliage from nature;. Reginald C Essenhigh, modelled designs based on a flowering plant; Richard E I'oulton, studies in lettering.</p>
        <p>BABY CARRIAGES, special Valile at 395. 6c/. Go-carts from OR Gd.—J. 6. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The Territorials. The following is taken from the current Territorial orders :—" The following es.tract from the London Gazette,' dated 12th July, is puhli•hed for inf. rma- tion '4th Battalion Prioce of Wales's Volunteers (South Lance. Itegirneut), 2nd Lieut. Arnold Seymour Jones, forthe unattached bat for the Territorial Force (serving with the Leeds Univertay contingent :senior Division, to be 2nd Lieutenant.—Dated July 18th, 1912."'</p>
        <p>stockton Heath Garden Society. There will he a special Bank Holi.tay attraction at Wilderspool Park on Monday, August .Ih, in the Eha pe of the Stockton Heath Garden Society's 11th annual show, sports and fete. An excellent round of amusements wril be provided, including sports, old English fair, juvenile fancy dancing in costurne,open- air concert, conjuring, &amp;c. The Warrinwod Pioneer Band will be in attendance. Lady Greenall will open the flower show at 2.30. Entries close on July 30th. Territorial inspection. On Saturday last the 4th Battallion (P.W.V.) Territorials marched to Appleton Park where an inspection of .100 members was wade by the Brigade Colonel who expressed his great satisfaction with the conduct and appearance of the men. The local Territorials will on Sunday week journey to Kirkham to undergo their annual training. To-morrow (Sun- day) the battalion will parade at 9-30 a.m, and pro- ceed to Sr. Paula' Church for divine service.</p>
        <p>DRFSS HASK ETS, 19. Gd., 21s. 6d., 245. 6d., specia value in Hand Bags, frunks, Suit cases, etc.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Manchester Unity of Oddfellows. The passing of the Insurance Act has drawn attention to the special advantage offered by the Loyal Queen Mary Female Lodge of the Warrington Disttict of this Order. The next meeting of the lodge will be held at the Adult School, Academy- street, on Thutsday next at 7.30 p.m. Forms of application fr membeiship may be obtained from Mr. Frank Webster (secretary), 86, Manchester-road Women Liberals' Picnic.</p>
        <p>The annual picnic in counecti m with the Warring- ton Wcmen's Liberal Association will be held on Tuesday next, when Eastham Gardens will be visited. The party will leave Latchford Locks by the steamer Claughton at 10 a.m.; returning at 6 p.m. The day's programme includes variety entertainments, admission to 50 acres of pleasure gardens, knife and fork tea, ,kci. Mr. and Mrs. A. H. Crosfield and Miss Crosfield will accompany the excursion. Trades and Labour Council. The monthly meeting of this Council was held in the I.L.P. Rooms, Ai tingstall's Buildings, on Tuesday evening, Mr. Charles Dukes presiding.—A letter was read from the National Transport Workers' Federa- tion asking for finvicial support to enable them fo carry on their fight.—A discussion ensued on the election policy of the Council, and it was decided that various miters broached at the meeting be left in the hands of the election committee.—Reference was made to the National Insurance Act with regard to the contributions at present being paid by young persons in receipt of ,low wages.—No resolution on the subject was passed. TENNIS, Croquet, Cricket and other outdoor games.— J. S. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. Motor Cars in Collision. Norcott Brook, Whitley, was the scene of a violent collision between two motor cars on Monday. Shortly after neon a car, belonging to a Blackpool gentleman, was proceeding towards Warrington from the direc- tion of Tarporley, and when turning the corner at Norcott Brook it collided with a car owned by a Hartford gentleman, which was travelling in the opposite direction. Both cars were damaged, the Blackpool car being completely wrecked. A gentle- man who was in the Blackpool car received a severe scalp wound, and a lady received a fractured shoulder blade. They were attended by Dr. Bower' of Stretton, and were able later to proceed home. The occupants cf the Hartford car fortunately escaped with a shaking. Voluntary Aid Detachment Inspection. In the pleasant grounds of Garswood Park on Satur- day, Major Gregson, Warrington Barracks, inspected, on behalf of the War Office the Warrington St. John Voluntary Aid Detachment and the Ashton and New- ton Voluntary Aid Detachment. There was a good I muster of both bodies. The Warrington contingent was ' under the command of Sergt. Baldry, Dr. Law being unable to be present. First Officer Williams, Second , Officer Spann and Sergt. Fell were also on parade. Lady Mary Gerard, who is the commandant of the Newton section, was present. and was accompanied by Drs. Valentine and Watkins (Newton), and Jones (Ashton), Sergt-Maj or Rimmer, and M .ssrs. F. Wagg S. Lamb. G. Beaton and A. E. Bennet, under-officers. At the conclusion, Lady Gerard stated that the In specting Officer had expressed his pleasure and astis- faction at all he had seen, especially the stretcher drill.</p>
        <p>Warrington Corporal's Bravery. A Warrington stAdier —Corporal Hughes, of the let Lifeguards—was concerned in a plucky rescue from drowning on Saturday. It appears that while boating on the Thames at Windsor, Miss Nellie Wilson, aged twenty, and a friend, Miss C. Harvey, were exchang- ing seats when the boat capsized and they were thrown into deep water. Mr. A. D. Bridge, an Eton boy plunged into the water, followed by the Corpural, who is said to be the best swimmer in the Guards. Miss Wilson was unconscious when brought to the shore, but recovered afterwards. Her friend was unable to swim to the bank unaided. Corporal Hughes is a member of a well-known Warrington family living at the Railway Inn, Milner-street. His brother, Mr. James Hughes, was a member of the Widnes and Leeds football teams. The Corporal went to South Africa with the first active service contingent of the Warring- ton Volunteers and soon after his return from the war he joined the Army. Death of Mrs. G. J. Wallace.</p>
        <p>We regret to announce the death of Mrs. Wallace, the widow of the latn Mr. George John Wallace, of Wilderspool. The deceased had been in rather indifferent health for some time, and she passed away on Tuesday last week at the age of 57 years. The body was interred on Saturday at 3 p.m. in St. Thomas' churchyard, Stockton Heath The mourners were : —Mr Percy Wallace and Mr Bruce Wallace, Sons; Messrs Edward Westbrolk, H J Westbrook, Thomas Westbrook, brothers ; Messrs William C Page and H J Page, nephews. Floral tributes were sent by : " Her sorrowing children," Grandson Victor, Tom and Marie, Willie and May, Nellie and Plorrie, Nellie (Southport), Edith, Mr ad Mrs F Howard, Mr J F Dutton, Mrs Bolton and family, Mr and • Mrs Sayer, A Friend, Mrs Hill and MIS Durleston. Mr and Mrs John Gillies, Edith Bolton, Mr and Mrs Bell, Mr G Proffit and B Bullen. The funeral arrangements were in the hands of Messrs J and W Dutton, San key-st reet.</p>
        <p>HOWLEY BRIDGE. To be Opened Next Week. The new footbridge over the river at Howley has now been completed and will be formally opened by the Mayor at 330 next Friday afternoon. The members of the Council and invited guests will assemble on the Howley bank of the river and, after the Mayor has performed the opening ceremony, they will walk over the bridge into Victoria Park.</p>
        <p>DON'T Loom OLD—kep the avages of Time at Bay by Darkening the Grey Streaks with LOCKYER'S SULPHUR HAIR RESTORER Which deepensto a natural colour in a few days. Lockyer' s maintains a youthful appearance. Large bots e erywhere. Post I ree 1/6. Bedford Laboratory, Loudon, SE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>SMOKE.</head>
        <p>SMOKE.</p>
        <p>CANON STEVENSON'S SUGGESTIONS.</p>
        <p>THE SMOKE AND TRADE.</p>
        <p>I [To THE EDITOR OF THE "EXAMINER."] Sir,—May I make a small contribution to the discussion on this subject ? Smoke, by which I mean all that comes out of chimneys, surely aught to be abated as much as possible. It cannot be as good for us to breathe polluted air, as pure air. It makes it almost impossible to keep our honses clean. It lessens our amount of bright sun- shine. It destroys our trees and spoils or vegetation. When I first came to Warring- ton an avenue of limes led from the Man- chester road to the College. Not one is alive now and no new ones will grow. On the other hand, nothing must be done to injure the trade of a town that lives by its trade: If the trade cannot be carried on without the smoke, the smoke must be endured.</p>
        <p>Nor do I think the action of Mrs.Hopwood of Middleton is to be admired or imitated. We want to live on friendly terms with our neighbours and fellow-citizens and the idea of perpetual prosecutions would B`tily mean perpetual strife and Better smoke than this.</p>
        <p>It seems to me that the first thing we want is reliable information. May I put our wants in the form of questions? I._ Is there any means by which smoke can be abolished or considerably abated? 2. Does this means apply to all chimneys. If not, to which? 3. If there is such a means, what is its cost?</p>
        <p>4. What amount of saving would be effected by the introduction of smoke abate- ment?</p>
        <p>If any expert can answer these questions and give good proof of his statements then we shall have something definite to lay before manufacturers, who, I am sure will consider sympathetically any reasonable businesslike scheme placed before them.—Faithfully yours, MORLEY STEVENSON. _ AN ENGINEER'S POINT OF VIEW.</p>
        <p>[To THE EDITOR OF THE "ExAmiNER."I Sir,—ln connection with the correspon- dence that has been going on in your paper about the smoke trouble, it may be of interest to your readers to have brought to their notice by an engineer some of the facts upon which the emission of smoke black or otherwise depends. It should be clearly realized that the emission of black smoke from any chimney is an evidence of imperfect and therefore wasteful combustion. It is practically impossible to avoid the production of smoke altogether, but there is no doubt that for power production, apart from smelt- ing and like operations, it is possible and practicable to reduce smoke emission much more than it is done in many of the power producing plants in Warrington. It woull be impossible to estimate the waste of fuel going on in our manufacturing processes in town without detailed know- ledge of the working of individual plants, but Ido not think anyone who has know- ledge of the question will accuse me of exaggeration in estimating that we waste 2,000 tons of coal per week in Warrington which could be saved if all our manufacturers employed right plant, and intelligence were used in the working.</p>
        <p>What then are the main causes resulting in waste, and in the emission of the black smoke of which your correspondents com- plain? In imperfect combustion not only is there a loss occasioned by a partial instead of a complete utilization of the volatile constituents of the fuel, but also that due to incomplete oxidation of the fixed carbon of which the fuel mainly consists. The importance of this in regard to heat produc- tion is apparent by a glance at the figures supplied by Mr G B Stone, M.I.M.E. British Thermal</p>
        <p>• Heat Units. 11b. Carbon consumed to Carbon Dioxide gives off 14,650. 11b. Carbon consumed to Carbon Monoxide gives off 4,400</p>
        <p>These two figures suggest the most important item in economical power produc- tion. It should be remembered that all combustion is effected by help of the oxygen that is taken from the air. •In view of the figures given the all important point is to ensure that the right amount of air at the proper temperature is brought into contact with the carbon in combustion to convert this carbon into carbon dioxide gas. If insufficient air is supplied there is a deficiency of oxygen available and carbon monoxide is formed which, as the figures indicate, reduces the heat to one-third. It is, however, worthy of note that a deficiency of air supply is not the only cause of the production of C.O. The air may be badly distributed, and so combustion may be complete at one point and incomplete at another. A sufficiently high and uniform temperature of the boiler furnace is also an important factor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>STOKERS.</head>
        <p>THE STOKERS.</p>
        <p>The above point, important though it be, is only one of many, but it illustrates how unwise it is for manufacturers to leave this question, as is often done, to men of com- paratively low intelligence. 1, irst there is the stoker. There is no doubt a great deal could be done, by more intelligent stoking, especially in band stoking—probably far more than manufacturers realize. The mis- take many employers make is the paying of low wages which generally means an unin- telligent stoker, who by carelessness or ignorance wastes ten times more in coal than is saved by the few shillings in wages. Of course it does not follow that higher wages of itself will make a man intelligent, but by paying a good wage you have a call on a higher class of mind.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>ANALYSIS OF GASES.</head>
        <p>ANALYSIS OF GASES.</p>
        <p>But the stoker, whatever his care may be, must have his knowledge supplemented and guided by scientific control, and this suggests the importance of an analysis of the flue gases without which the stoker and all con- cerned with the boilers are very much in the dark. This is comparatively a simple opera- tion and the sampling apparatus only costs a few pounds. But this is often rendered useless by the absence of knowledge on the part of those who work it to realize its importance and to apply the remedies. It can hardly be. expected that in ordinarily sized manufactories men should be kept who, have specialized along these lines. The best plan in such cases is to call in the services of a competent engineer, who before his services are dispensed with can put those in charge on proper lines.</p>
        <p>THE MISTAKES OF MANUFACTURERS.</p>
        <p>But when all has been said about stoking, and efficient scientific control, you are bound to have in the case of an overtaxed boiler, a smoky chimney. Unfortunately few mann-</p>
        <p>facturers are able to give time and intelligent attention to thrs department. They know the details of their own manufacturing processes, the trading and financial parts, but these as a rule have no relation to power production It would pay manufacturers over and over again if they would call in some independent expert who had given lifelong practical study to the subject. The mistake many make is to depend on the recommendation of those interested in pushing some particular appli- ance for the saving of coal. They select the one that has been most highly recommended. This often proves a failure,is scrapped and the manufacturer in disgust loses interest in the subject and turns down every new sugges- tion. The appliance tried may be a good thing under certain conditions, hut not applicable to the conditions of the works in question. What the manufacturer ought to have done, before adopting the appliance,was to consult someone who from an independent view point could take all the circumstances into account and say whether the appliance was likely to be of value with the conditions existing in particular works. A TEST OF EFFICIENCY. A rough test to ascertain if a boiler is 'doing good work is to find the amount of water evaporated by 1 lb. of coal of average calorific value. When a boiler is doing good work 1 lb. of coal ought to evaporate (say unc'er 100 lbs. pressure) 11 to 12 lbs. of water. If the combustion is bad the evaporation will sink to perhaps 8 to 9 lbs. of water to the pound of coal. If it so does there is a loss of 25 per cent. in the fuel. Not only is the manu- facturer's coal bill very much higher than it ought to be, but he becomes a nuisance to his neighbours by the fuel which ought to be consumed in the furnace being sent into the atmosphere by the chimney in the form of soot, tar and gas. In the above test, if com- parisons are to be made, it is essential that. the conditions of the plants compared are practically similar,and there are many points to watch in what appears to be a simple test. I his test will indicate if the boiler is doing well, but will not aid the manufacturer in locating the fault or faults if the boiler is doing badly. —Yours &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>ENGINEER.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF AN OLD WARRINGTONIAN.</p>
