      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>LATE MRS. HOLT.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MRS. HOLT.</p>
        <p>One of Warrington's oldest inhabitants has been removed by the death of Mrs Holt, widow of the late Mr Mark Holt, who passed away at her home in Knutsford-road on Sunday night. Mrs Holt was a very familiar figure in the town, the whole of her long life having been spent in Warrington and district. She was born in the same year as the late Queen Victoria and would have been 93 years of age in August. Her native place was Penketh where her father kept a farm at Penketh-lane ends. Mrs Holt lemembered the days when racing took place on Penketh Common, tbe sport ultimately being stopped through the intervention of the Quakers who owned land in the locality. Notwith- standing her great age, the deceased lady had a wonderful memory and was fond of recalling incidents that happened in the Warrington of far bygone years. She retained all her faculties and was able to read without the aid of spectacles. Up to the vely last she was surprisingly active and had hardly ever experienced an illness. On Sunday, there was nothing to indicate that the end was so near, for Mrs Holt appeared to be in her usual good spirits and partook of a hearty tea. Sae retired to rest in the evening and passed away peacefully in her sleep. Mrs Holt had lived in the same house in Knutsford-road for about forty years and was highly respected in the neighbour- hood. Her eldest surviving son is Mr. Edward Holt, boot and shoe maker, St. Austin's-lane, who is 08 years of age, and was 28 years in the service of the late Nir Joseph Davies, of the Old Warps, Latchford, a former Mayor of the borough. Mrs bolt's descendants are 7 children, .58 grandchildren, and CJ 0 great grandchildren. _ . _</p>
        <p>The funeral took place in the quiet churchyard at Aston (Cheshire) on Thursday. Mrs Holt lived at Sutton Weaver for about twenty years and during that time she was a worshipper at Aston Church. Since coming to Lttchford she had attended St. James' Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>OPEN-AIR TEMPERANCE CAMPAIGN.</head>
        <p>OPEN-AIR TEMPERANCE CAMPAIGN.</p>
        <p>A Plea for Local Option. The open•air temperance campaign was resimed on Thursday when another meeting was held at Bridge Foot..</p>
        <p>The Rev H Ogden, who presided, said that whatever social legislation was placed on the statute book, it must tumble to pieces or be largely abortive unless there was morality to back it up and give it life. The slum problem was a great menace but he maintained that it was impossible to deal efficiently with the housing question and the general uplifting of the people unless the fearful drink curse were removed. The very areas which were most congested were usually the nests and hot beds of public houses. He noticed with gratitude that one of the leading representatives of Labour, Mr Philip Snowden,M.P., in a speech on the U.K.A. platform in Mahchester, recognizeo that it was time all social, labour and temperance workers recog- nised that the problems of intemperance and poverty were inter-related. Poverty was undoubtedly one of the causes of drink, but it was equally certain that drink was the cause of poverty. He should be glad if those who were actively associsted with open-air Labour propaganda locally would support, that temperance campaign, because the Labour members of the House of Commons and the leaders of the Labour world were agreed that it was impossible to get the workingman to think about social problems so long as he was content to sit in the public-house, droWning his senses and ruin- ing his capacity. Mr George Neville, of Liverpool, said that the great responsibility toward child life was with the State and it was because they recognised the danger of the drink trade to the well being of the child that they called upon the State to protect the children. The drink business was not a trade but a traffic with human flash and blood. Probably the greatest clanger of the drink problem was the tied-house system with its awful consequences of increared fscil hies for drink- ing. The workingmen of Britiin spent £100,000,009 in intoxicating liquor—almost one quarter of the total wages earned by the workers. The unemployment problem was linked with the problem of intemperance. Until they got a clear- thinking people they would never reach their goal of a. free, happy people. He believed he could make no higher appeal to Englishmen than an appeal on behalf of England's homes. (Applause.) In every big city men and women were going down into the dregs of society on the tide of strong drink and it was dehumanizing little children and robbing them of the light of home. They demanded that the State should step in to help them in protecting their countrymen from the dangers of theidrink trade. They demanded local option, that was the right of the people to say for themselves whether they would have the public).- houses in their midst or not. (Applause.) The brewers said it would . not be effective. Then why were they fighting it so strenuously? It was because they knew it would be operative. He urged them to work for losal option and so give to certain areas where they so demanded it, the right to banish drink from their midst. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>ISAS 166 ) eANIA NA lIS 101111•1111MINIMMIlli 40111111</head>
        <p>ISAS 166 ) eANIA NA lIS 101111•1111MINIMMIlli 40111111</p>
        <p>THE PREMIER IN CHESHIRE.</p>
        <p>Mr Asquith returned to England from his Irish visit on Saturday. He was cheered by a large crowd on landing at Holyhead, and proceeded by train to Chester, Where lae received an address from the local Liberals in front of the Town Hall, in the presence of an enthosiastic crowd. Accompanied by the distinguished party of Liberals who had attended him in Dublin, he journeyed by motor car to Thointon Manor, where he was the chief guest at the garden party given by Sir William Lever to the Liberals of Liverpool and Wirral, stopping on the way at Little Sutton and Port Sunlight to receive other addresses. At the garden party the guests were five thousand in number. Mr Asquith, in a speech of about twenty minutes' duration, claimed that the Liberal and progressive forces in +.e past seven years had improved every department of administration, and by old-age pensions and national insurance had made a greater advance in social reform than had been made by any other country in the same space of time. He spofte wits gratification of the enthusiasm of his reception in Ireland,and predicted that with the passing of Home Rule estrangement would give way to reconciliation, affection, and loyalty.</p>
