      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Mr S Wright and Dr Fox. A FATHER'S NEGLECT.—Jchn William Jackson was charged with leaving his two children chargeable to the Warrington Union. Mr. Shearer stated that the children had been in the Workhouse &amp;nee April, 1909 and that their maintenance had cost £l4O. During that time Jackson had been in and out of work, but he had spent every penny he had earned. When the children were admitted to the institution there was not a single stick of furniture in the house and nothing for them to lie upon. The Guardians decided to adopt the children to keep them away from the parents. Jackson was required to contriubte something to their maintenance, but nothing was forth- coming. It was found that the man was earning on an average £1 7s 31,d per week. When he discovered that inquiries were Ceiteb made Jackson left the town. He was apprehended at Bolton. It was hardly possible to describe how the man had ill-treated his family for years. The Chief Constable proved 37 previous convic- tions against the prisonee. Jackson was sent to gaol for three months with bard labour. ltiES DAY.— Before Councillor Henry Roberts, presiding, Colonel Edelston, Dr Fox, Councillor C Jackson Holmes, Mr J Rymer Young and Mr 'l' Locker. Tin FT OF A CYCLE.—Robert Clayton, of no settled residence, was charged on remand with having stolen a bicycle, £4 10s., the property of Hairy Thomas, amuse- ment caterer, Castle-yard, Stockport, on the 13th inst. Thomas was in the Palmyra Restaurant and bad taken the precaution to take his cycle inside. Whilst he was having tea the prisoner, who was in the same room, left with the machine. He sass apprehended in Liverpool endeavouring to dispose of it at a second-hand shop.— The Chief Constable said that the man bad apparently been roaming about the country tor some months. No less than seven bicycles were stolen in the town last week.—Prisoner, who had nothing to say, was sent to gaol for a months' hard labour. WIFE MAINTENANCE AI:REARS : £l7l IRRECoVER- ABLE.—During the hearing of a charge against James Hackney, of Widnes, for owing arrears of maintenance to his wife under it separation order, Mr Browne men- tioned that the prisoner actually owed £lB7 4s. However, by an order in the King's Bench Division, not more than six months' arrears could be recovered. The warrant therefore charged the prisoner with owing £l5 12a. Since June, 1895, Hackney had not paid a single penny. He was a moulder at Forshaw's Works, but for some reason resided at Widnes. Until recently Mrs Hackney's circumstances had not necessitated the enforcing of the order. Prisoner said that when the order was made he was a young man, but now his earn- capacity was not so good and he could not pay 12s per week. The Chairman said the prisoner would have to pay the money, or go to gaol for two months. ALLEGED THEFT OF A POSTAL PACKET.—A youth named James Alfred Silcock, 154, Forster-street, was charged with having stolen a postal packet containing a fountain pen, on March th.—The Chief Constable said that the accused was only arrested the previous day. For some time past complaints with regard to packages being missed from the Post Office had been received, with the result that a member of the Investigation Department, London visited Warrington. lie made inquiries and traced the theft of the fountain pen to the prisoner. It was impossible to proceed with the case that morning, and he asked for a remand for a weeks o objection WOS offered to bail.—Mr. Steel, who , appeared for the prisoner, said Silcock had been in the employ of the post office for four years, and belonged. , to a very respectable family. He had no father.— Silcock was remanded and admitted to bail.</p>
        <p>THURDAY.—Before Mr J Rymer Young (in the chair), Drs Fox and Naden and Messrs A J Jolley,W Roberts and C S Burgess. A WONIAN'S CRUEL NEGLECT.—Bridget 11I'Coy, 20, Stamford-street, was charged with neglecting her three children.—Mr Browne related a sordid story of a woman who was addicted to heavy drinking. The father of the children died about three months ago. Since his death the wife had been continually drinking,and the children in consequence had been deprived of food'and clothir g. On Tuesday last the local inspector of the N.S.P.C.C. called at the house its the morning and found the children left in the house alone. Late at night the woman came home staggering drunk. The children had not had a bit of food the whole day. The home was in a filthy condition,and upon examination of the children, Dr Peacock came to the conclusion that they could not have been washed for weeks.—Prisoner was sent to gaol for three month. FRlDAY—Before Mr S Wright (in the chair), Aldermen Bolton and Pierpoint, Dr Fox, 'Messrs A Jolley, T Locker and W Furness.</p>
        <p>THEFT FROM A TELL —William Millar, 4, Hart's- place, was charged with stealing from a till at the bar of the Royal Oak Hotel, Bridge-street, 2s 6d,the monies of Thomas Sangster.—Prisoner pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable stated that at 3.40 the previous day the prisoner was in the httel with two other men. They were served in the vaults by the barmaid, who left them to go to another part of tee hotel. Whilst she was away she heard the bell of the cash register ring, and on running back found the prisoner leaning across the counter with his band in the till. He then left the premises, but was brought back and banded over to the police. When charged by Detective-Sergeant Holland, prisoner gave as his excuse the fact that be had been drinking rather heavily. The police were of the opinion that the man had beets led away.—Prisoner was ordered to pay the costs of the case (i 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>The weekly meeting of the above society was held on Sunday last when Mr A Healey occupied the chair and spoke of the benefits and blessings derived from attend- ance at the men's meetings. Mr J Ball was the speaker for the day and gave a thoughtful address upon the subject of' Motive Power" as applied to the christian life, through the Lord Jesus Christ. Mr Payton read the lesson and Mr Healey led the prayer. Mr W H Payton, Mus , Bac., gave an organ solo in an efficient manner. There was a fair attendanoe.</p>
        <p>The Bermondsey Settlement, In connection with the Wesleyan Methodist Confer- ence which has been held in Liverpool this week a meeting in support of the Bermondsey Settlement took place in Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church on Tuesday even lug. Mr It H Posnett presided and the speakers included Dr Scott Lidgett (warden of the Settlement), Miss Alice Barlow and Mr. C. E. Parker (hon treasurers). In the course of his address Dr Lidgett showed how the work which he initiated in South-East London twenty- one years ago was helping to solve some of the social problems. He sp.cially emphasised the value of voluntary service based upon practinal experience and sympathy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>St. John's Presbyterian Church.</head>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church.</p>
        <p>Preaching on Sunday evening from the words" Show me Thy glory," the Rev W Reid suggested that it was the spirit of sacrifice seen in the intercession of Moses which opened up the possibility aud the cpportunity for the vision. But, proceeded the preacher, just as in the realm of colonr there is tut a small range within which the eye effectively operates. so the glory of God is possible to the human soul only within a certain narrowed range. Spoken o' as a cleft in the rock, and the shadow of His hand over all, it faithfully portrays the experience of the average life. To see things only after they have gone by is oft4n one of our chief com- plaints; and yet to see everything as God sees it would tend to paralyse life instead of strengthening it. What we have to remember is that we are not here to explain, nor even to understand fully the meaning of lifa. but to be educated by it. Afterwards we snail. see His face and be satisflA when we awake in His likeness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>Brick-street Mission.</head>
        <p>Brick-street Mission.</p>
        <p>The above mission, after being closed for a short time for renovation purposes, fixed Sugday last for the re-opening services, when Mr George Hunter, of Leigh. was the preacher for the morning. The afternoon was devoted to a sacred cantata entitled "The day of rest " by the mission choir. Mr B Hall occupied the chair and gave a few pleasing remaras on seeping the Sabbath as the day of rest. The mission choir under the leadership of Mr W Barton, senior, assisted on the organ by Mr Tornkinson, and Mr A Trow on the piano, went through their performances in a praiseworthy manner. Their singing was greatly appreciated and admired by all present. Solos and quartets were given in excellent style by the following members of the choir:—Mr W Barton, Mr H Lightfoot, Miss H Howell, Miss A Harding ; Mr Jas. at:t in, Miss Eva Shaw, Miss E Davies and Mr A Morris. The evening service was preached by Mr Will Price., of Wigan, president of the Connexion, when a large congregation attended. The choir rendered a special anthem in grand style, and Miss H Howell greatly pleased the congregation with her splendid rendering of the solo. " 0 corn., unto Mo." Tne collections for the day amounted to alittle over £5.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>Latchford Baptist Boys' Brigade.</head>
        <p>Latchford Baptist Boys' Brigade.</p>
        <p>A concert and demonstration in aid of the Summer Camp Fund, was given in the schoolroom on Thursday evening last week, before a large and enthusiastic audience. The Chaplain, Rey J 8 Chedburn, 000upied the chair, and referred to the remarkable progress made by the institution since its foundation last November and to the excellent work it had already accomplished. The programme cmasisted mainly of items contributed by the members including dumb-bell, bar-bell, and Swedish drill exercises, songs, recitatiums, and a weird and extremely funny perf,,rmance by it band of wild savages in full war-paint, entitled " The wail of the Wallawulloo." Mr Alf Dutton gave a number of recitals which were specially appreciated, and Mr Stanley Mather contributed two humorous songs. A special feature of the programme was an exceedingly graceful display of Indian club drill by Miss Ay Grounds (gold-medallist) and Miss Connie Ward (prize winner) under the direction of Mr Mottrarn, Instructor at the Warrington Gymnasium. A number of prizes including free tickets for the camp which bad been won during the season, were presented, and the proceedings terminated with the singing of the National Anthem. The camp will be held at Whitley Let-ouzo the kind iiivitation of Mr John Wilkinson from 2911 July to 10th August.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>Wycliffe Congregational Church.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe Congregational Church.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Samuel Pear.on, A.T.s., of Tynemouth, preached saorning and evening at Wycliffe Congrega tional Church, Bewsey-street, on Sunday. A portion of the morning service was devoted to the children, and in brief address to them be drew a lesson from the flowers which came year after year to cheer and brighten our surroundings by their ber.uty and fragrance, and said there was a perennial freshness in the word of God which had cheered the hearts of young and old for many generations. The prelcher bei.ei his sermon on the text" Thou seest the sh dow of the mountains as if they were men." (Judges 9c. 3Gv.) He said the text represented a type of character by no means uncommon. There were many people who suffered from imaginary fears—fears which were usually gronndless. He instanced " Mr. Fearing " in Bunyan's " Pilgrims Progress," and the imaginary fea,s which possessed Cowper, the poet. Referring to the lightness and superficiality of life at the present day the breather said there were many like the women in Ibsen's pin 3, who whilst occupied with their dolls paid little heed to the higher purposes of life. What we had to fear was ourselves.</p>
        <p>At the evening service the discourse was based on the words" Who am I?" (11. Samuel, vii., IS). David he said, felt his own unworthiness to build a temple to God, but unlike him, there were some who would occupy the Throne of the Most High if it were offered to them. He disagreed with the theory of the agnostics concerning the origin and destiny of man. Mau might be a reed, but he was a thinking reed, and was able to think, speak, lore and worship. Like the prodigal son we must come to ourselves and claim kinship with " Our Father," for we are the sons of God. On Sunday next the pulpit will be occupied by the Rev F C Rogers, a griindson of the late Dr. J. Guinness Rogers, who is a student at Yorkshire United College</p>
        <p>CANADA'S WORLD-WIDE TRADE.—Carthda's foreign trade which in 1910-11 was 151 millions sterling is now stated to have reached nearly the 200 million mark for the year ending march last. This foreign trade has practically doubled in a decade. Canada does business with 87 countries, and her trade per head of the popu- lation ranks third among the nations. Her empire trade has doubled in 14 years and represents 37 per r'ent. of the whole. It is significant, however, that Cimadian trade with the United States is now 61 per cent, of the whole, and that her trade is increasing 25 times as fast with the United States as with Great Britain. On the other hand Canada's foreign trade for 1910-11 was 107 dollays per head, while that with the United Statcs was 40 dollars per head.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>KING OPENS IMMINGHAM DOCK.</head>
        <p>THE KING OPENS IMMINGHAM DOCK.</p>
        <p>On Monday, the King, accompanied by the Queen, opened the new Great Central Rail- way dock at Immingham, Grimsby. The construction of the new dock is a notable achievement. Built on marshy and waste land, it is one of the best-equipped ports in the kingdom, the water area being forty-five</p>
        <p>acres, while the graving dock is 740 ft. long and 56ft. wide. There is accommodation on the quays for 11,600 waggons. The work has occupied six years, and cost about £3,000,000. Our picture gives a general view of the dock and shows gunboats dressed for the Royal visit.</p>
        <p>• • ; •,tt;,„,s • •*. •&gt;.3.• • (.4,4:, •-•, • • • ' • 'l' : . - •, •-• : • , iggi:4*2.' • - •, • . " PS * • 7v*. • QfT ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>TAXING THE LAND.</head>
        <p>TAXING THE LAND.</p>
        <p>Mr. Onthwalte's Reply to Lord Haldane.</p>
        <p>Tn the House of Lords on Wednesday the question was raised of the" new " Liberal policy in reference to land, Lord Haldane replying that there was DO such policy. There was an independent committee at work —but he could not give the names of the members—wbo would report as to reforms in the land system, for the information of the Goverrinifnt, but be scouted the suggestion that there was a Covernment campaign in favour of the single tax. Mr. Outhwaite, M.P., who is assisting Mr. Murphy's candidature at Crewe, seen by a Press representative on Thursday respecting Lord Haldane's statement in the House of Lords on the subject of a proposed autumnal campaign advocating land taxes, said, " We have the facts stated by Mr. Asquith in the House of Commons that a committee of investigation was being formed with his acquiescence, and it was my own personal deduction that the facts were going to be used tor the purpose of a great campaign, which was stated by the Unionist Press as going to take place." He did not regard Lord Haldane's statement as any repudiation in regard to the single tax. Mr. Outhwaite pointed out that at Hanley and at Crewe they had been fighting on the definite proposals embodied in the memortal presented to the Prime Minister.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>BANK HOLIDAY RAILWAY ARRANGEMENTS.</head>
        <p>BANK HOLIDAY RAILWAY ARRANGEMENTS.</p>
        <p>What a remarkable contrast is to be found in" the splendid excursion facilities of the various rxilway companies for the August Btnk Holidays, in compari- son with the deplorable depleted railway service of only a few mouths ago, consequent upon the great coal strike. From Friday next, for three or four days, the people of Warrington will, in common with the people of the country generally, be more or less on holiday. Many are the tastes and whims of holiday makers, and though many Warrington people will, weather permitting, thoroughly enjoy excursions into the immediate neighbourbood,yet others desire to go further afield for their pleasures. The London and North-Western Rail- way Company are therefore to be congratulated upon the complete manner in which they are catering for the needs of both classes mentioned, more particularly the last mentioned class of holiday-makers. Practically every resort in England, Ireland, Scotland and Wales, including London and the South Coast,West of England, Wales and the English Lakes may be reached by means of excursion ticksts.Without expatiating in any way upon the attractions of London at August,we may mention that the bookings to the Metropolis are both low and varied. Then there are cheap excursions to Douglas 1.0. M. on August Bank Holiday, every Monday and Saturday (fares etc. periods). Then there are special attractions at Manchester on August Bank Holiday. There will also be cheap excursions to the popular resort of Black- pool during these holidays' also to Shrewsbury and district, and many other delightful resorts such as Colwyn Bay, Rtiyl, Llandudno, Chester, Prestatyn, Abergele and all the popular health and pleasure resorts in the.l3ritish Isles, tnat are accessible by the premier I.ne.</p>
        <p>BISHOP'S CRITICISM OF MODERN PARENTS. The increasing irresponsibility on the part of par,nta was the theme of a sermon by the Bishop of Liverpool at the anniversary of the day schools of Emmanuel Church, Earlestown, on Sunday. i He said that at present there were some very ominous signs u the home life of England. Parents were shirking their responsibility. He supposed that at present we .were suffering from a reaction ass result of the severity with which, say a hundred years ago, parents treated their children. But be could not help thinking it would be better for the country and better for the children if there were a little more of that Spartan severity which characterised the upbringing of that race which helped to make , England what she was to-day, a position which it seemed not unlikely we should lose before long uniesa the people were more disciplined and obedient. While parents loved their children, they should show that love not by spoiling them and by indulging there, but by seeking to train them from their earliest years In habits of obedience, reverence, loyalty and affeotion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <p>people. They were land animals and could not live without land. They bad as much Tighr. to the land as they had the right to live. (Hear, hear.) No man had an exclusive right to the land which was the common property of the people who had the privilege to live on it. The land had to support man in one way or another. In the past the landlords bore practically the whole of the taxes of the country but now they hardly bore any. (Hear, hear.) Year by year, generation by generation, and opportunity by opportuuity, the laud owners had 'hi fted from themselves the burdens of the land on to the hacks of the people. When people saw the direot consequences of taxation they showed a keen interest in it. There was a vast difference between direct and indirect totation. A man had to pay more if he was indirectly taxed. (Hear, hear.) It put</p>
        <p>UNFAIR BURDENS on the people. If there was a twopenny tax on the land values of this country they would get £50,( )0,00 by it and that would reduce very materially the various other taxes to which we were subjecterl (Hear hear, and applause). The value of land had been increased through the industrial progress of the country. Every industry that was bringing riches to the country WPI bringing riches to the mau who owned the land because that land was indispensable to the industries and efforts of the workingman. (Hear, hear). lde lend-owner did nothing but wait for the time when he could get his own price for his land—when the industries of the people wanted it and could not do without it, for the good of the whole nation. The land-owner whose values were being raised by the people all over the country and from which be derived a tremendous profit, did not contribute to the local rates. The</p>
        <p>VALUES WHICH WERE MADE BY THE PEOPLE should contribute something to the common needs of the people. (Hear, !hear ) the new valuation would, he hoped, open people's eyes. There were 200 members of Parliament who were in favour of putting a tax on the land values of the country in order to relieve the burdens of the workers, inventors and thinkers of the country. They would put taxation upon the things which ought to be taxed. It should be posiible to get special local expenses from land taxation. He could not help seeing that that was coming to the front, and was likely to be discussed more and more if the Liberal Government remained in office. The people should support the Liberal party which had been so instru- mental in getting the people so many benefits. (Applause.) Ought not the wealth created, to belong to the people who had created it, in order to minister to their common needs ? He knew there were many difficulties in the minds of some people who asked " If yea put a tax on laud won't we have to pay the same as we had before in taxation ?" VVirat did they get the rates from in Warrington ?—not from land values. (Helar, hear.) The tax ought to be put on the man who was</p>
        <p>REAPING GREAT EENEFITS. and not improving things at all and the burden of those who were improving the country ought to be made lighter. That was the direction which the land taxa- tion of the future would take. (Applause.) The rail- way dividends were affected because the companies had to pay such a heavy price for the land, and it was the same with the Manchester Ship Canal. The Liberal party were not going to stop at the land question— (hear, hear)—but they would go On to other important questions for the welfare of the people. (Applause.) They had many good banners but he could see others coming forward at the present time, and one was One man, one vote." He thought the Tories were going to lose that battle as they had lost many a one in the past. There was going to be another record cut on the tombstone, lamenting the departure of the plural voter. (Hear, hear.) He hoped the people would make the best of their opportunities to make the land brighter and better in the future then it had been in the past. (Hear, hear and applause!. Hr. MortroN, in proposing a vote of thanks to the speaker, said they bad listened with great interest to the eloquent remarks of Mr.. Canning and they had beard that the income of the country if equally divided would be sufficient to give each family £2uo per annum. Those were the lines on which reform should go ; to make every man able to pay for his own sickness just like the better class people did.. He believed there was a considerable body of opinion growing in the country that it was in the direction of the organisation of industrialism that the true solution must come. (Hear, hear.) He thought they must deal with the land and every other question which made for social reform and eorutinise every question as it came forward. At the present time, so far as he could see, it was the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, July, 27, 1912.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, July, 27, 1912.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. Pendleton, of 7, Belmont Avenue, Latchford, Warrington, says: —" Probably exposure .to all sorts of weather was, in my case, the cause of a somewhat sharp attack of kidney disorder. There were slight pains in the lower part of the back and other I unmistakable symptoms of kidney affection.</p>
        <p>"I decided to try Doan's B.ackache Kidney Pills, and I was quite satisfied with the result. The first box afforded relief, and by the time I had used three boxes I was completely cured. I have no doubt these pills saved me from what might have developed into a serious illness, and I can therefore recommend them with confidence. (Signed), GEO. PENDLETO.N.</p>
        <p>In 219 boxes only 6 boxes 139; NEVER SOLD LOOSE. Of alt chemists and stores. or front Foster-Mellellan, Co., 8, Wet/a at., Oxford St., London, W.</p>
        <p>If you notice that the water has been out of order for a time, or you have to keep getting up in the night, do not be Surprised if you begin to get the pains and swellings of rheumatism, dropsy and heart trouble, gravel, stone, or lumbago pains in the loins and back, for these are the consequences of neglecting kidney trouble.</p>
        <p>Doan's Backache Kidney Pills over kidney and bladder weakness, correct over-sensitiveness of the bladder, clear the water when it is cloudy or gravelly, and prevent deposits of uric - acid in the system. Doan's Pills have cured many bad cases of dropsy, rheumatism, lumbago, gravel and stork., as well as pains in the back and loins, dizziness, languor, and neuralgic head- aches. They are for the kidneys and bladder only—not for constipation, liver complaint or biliousness. A Certificate of Purity accompanies every box.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>OPEN-AIR POLITICS.</head>
        <p>OPEN-AIR POLITICS.</p>
        <p>SUPPORT FOR BOLD LAND POLICY.</p>
        <p>The land question was the chief topic dealt with by the speaker at the weekly open-air meeting at Bridge Foot, held under the auspices of the Progressive Labour League on Monday. There was a large number present, over which Mr. Richard Osborne presided. The chief speaker was Mr. W. Canning, of Mauchester,whose remarks were attentively listened to. At the outset the CHAIRMAN briefly commented on the Insurance Act and said Mr Lloyd George had made people think that it was their bounden duty to provide tor themselves in times of sickness and distress. (Hear, hear.) As regarded the political situation generally, be thought Tariff Reform was abtolutely worn out. (bear, Lear). Mr. CANNING dealt with his subject in a very able manner aad the frequent introduction of humourous stories made his remarks all the more acceptable. He said he had never realised before a short time ago when he visited a cattle show that Mr Lloyd George was responsible for the cattle plague. (Laughter). There he heard an old farmer blaming the Chancellor for his having been refused permission to show his pigs on account of the embargo. (Laughter.) What- ever people might say, the Insurance Act was going to be a great benefit to the people of this country and would indeed make ours a new nation. (Hear, hear.) It would be e safeguard for all classes. When the Act Lad got into thorough working order, the people would reap innumerable henefi s from it. But there were many things they had ti thank the Libaral Government for, in addition to that great Act of National Insurance. There was an important question at the present time which Was loaming large in tae fore- ground, and that was the question of the taxation of land values. It was not that this was a poor country ; it was not the poverty of the country that made people poor and placed them in a position in which they were not able to keep themselves. The income of the country was £L800,000,000, and that was enough to give £2OO par year to every family, if it were evenly divided. But hawever democratic he might te, he was not going to propose that every man's wage ought to be the same as another's. The best reward would always be given to the worker who did the best work. (Hear, hear.) But the idea o having enough income to give each family £2OO per year showed very clearly that there was quite enough money to keep everyone in comfort if there was a fair distribution ot wealth. The tremendous inequality of the income of the cauntry Was, along with the other things consequent on it, one of the problems they had to face at the present time. Mr Lloyd George had laid his hand upon revenues which were like to serve his stead and the purpose of future Chancellors of the Exchequer for some time. There was a terrible huila- hullo° about the 1909 Budget, for it wakened people up when they had to put their hands in their pockets for taxes. The landlords began to think th&amp;r case was every inrd one indeed when Mr. Lloyd George touched them They had been touched 20 years before by Sir William Harcourt's succession duties, but theylfound a way of wriggling out of those taxes, so the Liberal party passed another Act which prevented! their evading them. (Hear, hear.) His sympathy with the landlords with their burdens was diminished iby the fact that they were perfectly able to pay them. (Hear, tear.) It was still further diminished when he considered facts concerning the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <p>workingmen who were paying the rates and taxes. The Liberal Party had done much for the workers in the past and would do even more in the future. (Hear, hear and applaus.). Mr. SANDERS in seconding said the land question was the solution of all our difficulties and troubles. (Hear, hear.) Anyone desiring a copy of the land ~oak could have it by applyin; at the Liberal Offices in Becrsey-street. (Applaube). Mr. CANNING briefly replied to the vote which was heartily carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>JUNIOR LIBERALS.</head>
        <p>THE JUNIOR LIBERALS.</p>
        <p>An Alternative Policy to Socialism.</p>
        <p>The Junior Liberal Association legan an open-air campaign on Wednesday evening with a meeting on the vacant land off Hardy-street. Mr. E. Lloyd Jones (chairman of the association), presided over a good attendance and the principal speaker was Mr W Ashton, of Wrexham.</p>
        <p>The CH MAI AN said it was the object of the Liberal party to make life better and brighter for the working. classes. They were seeking to adjust the burdens fairly and he claimed that that policy had been followed in the Insurance Act. Many people complained about the measure being compulsory but he believed that in the course of a year or two workingmen would be thankful that they had not a non-contributory scheme, because it would have made paupers of thew. As it was, the employer, the workman and the State all had their parts in the scheme. The Tory Press had organized an opposition with the object of befooling the working classes as to the benefizial result 1 of the Act, but their tactics had failed. For many years the arse majority of the people of this country had allowed 10,000,0(10 to dictate to them, but now the workers had begun to take an intelligent interest in their own affairs. A million and a half of the worke a were receiving wages of less than a week, two millions and a hair were getting less than 255., and four millions less than 30s There were only two ways of improving the condition of the workingman—the first was that a man, should be paid what he was worth and the second was that there should be means of making a man worth good wages. The Insurance Act bad a tendency to improve the physical conditions of the workers and they would therefore be in a more favourable position to demand better pay. (Applause.; The strength of a Dation lay in its health and the health of England would be better looked after under the new Act than ever it had been before. (Applause.) Mr Lloyd George—(applause)—came from a workingman's home and the reason the Tories were so much opposed to him was because he would not bend the knee to " blue blood." Ile remained true to the workingmen and if they would continue to back him up, he would lead them to victory. (Applause). Mr. ASHTON addressed himself chiefly to expounding an alternative policy to Soclalism. He said the Socialist usually pointed out that, the great amount of wealth that was being produced in tnis country was very badly shared and that, although the workingman was able to produce four or five times as rauela as he could a hundred years ago, he did not receive much more than formerly for his own use. The Socialist contended that the remedy was for the country to own the land and the capital, and then the workers would not only get the proportion which at present came to them in wages, but also the money which was swallowed up in rents and interest. The Socialist plan, in short, was to nationalise the instruments of production distribution and exchange. The alternative policy was the continuance of private ownership, with State interference in industry. Taus the State could step in and say to the employer that certain conditions must be fulfilled or he could not be allowed to continue as an employer. (Applause). The State could fix maximum hours of labour and minimum rates of wages. The speaker showed how the Liberal Government had moved along those lines by passing the Miners' Eight Hours' Act and the Shop Hours' Act, by setting up Boards to regulate wages in sweated industries and by passing the Minimum Wage Bill which brought about the settlement of the miners' strike. The State could also insist that a landlord who erected houses should make them thoroughly sanitary. To-day if a person took a house and found that its condition was injurious to health, he need not clear out. He bad only 'to communicate with the medical officer of health who would bend a note to the landlord telling him what repairs he had to carry out, and if he declined to do the work the local authority could do it and charge him with the cost. The Housing and Town Planning Act was also a great step forward, as it would enable local , councils to limit the number ot houses to the acre and ' generally to improve the laying-out of newly develop- ing areas. With regard to the distribution of wealth the Liberal Government was insisting that the man who derived his income from dividends and interest should be taxed on a gratuated scale so that he should contribute a larger share to the revenue than the man who bad to earn his wages by working for them. By land taxation the Government was also helping the people to secure part of the enhanced value which accrued to land through the activities and enterprise of the community.lt might be that Liberalism would stand to lose the support of those capitalists and landowners who adopted a selfish view, and unless the Government had the backing of the working classes they would not be able to move 83 rapidly along t he road of progress as they would desire. The speaker dealt at some length with the housing question and pointed out that the only way to reduce rents was te increase the supply of houses. It was now within the power of local authorities to build houses and they were able to borrow money on such favourable terms that they could let them at a cheaper rate than the private owner who wanted to make a profit. Local authorities had also the power to buy land which they believed would be required for build- ing purposes at some future date. Finally Mr Ashton strongly advocated the taxation of land values which would prevent the locking up of land for the speculative purpose and would be much fairer to the owner of hones than the present system. (Applause.) Mr J Morton and Mr NI Llewellyn also addressed the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>LIBERAL ASSOCIATION'S CAMPAIGN.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL ASSOCIATION'S CAMPAIGN.</p>
        <p>"The Imagination of Harold."</p>
        <p>An open-air meeting, which was well attended, was held under the auspices of the Warrington Liberal Association in Catherine-street on Wednesday evening. Mr Richard Osborne who presided said that, judging by the Tariff Reform League's organ one should have been afraid to hold a meeting in that locality. No working man need be afiaid to speak anywhere providing he respected himself, other's religion and his own. When a workingman living and working in a neighbourhood, got on a public platform he had to be very careful what he said beciiuse he was reminded of it in his daily life afterwards and he had to substantiate what he haa said. Two of the greatest subjects before us at the present time were industrial unrest and the Insur- ance Act. (Hear, hear). They should give the Act a fair chance t 3 prove its own worth. From the trade union and friendly society standpoint there was never an Act of Parliament in connection with which the promoter had raceived more deputations far explanatory purposes. Why was there the tertible industrial unrest at the present time ? The Con- servative party said it was Air Lloyd George's Lime. house speech and the Labour members who had caused it. It was, however, because the people were becoming , more educated and they could see they were not getting as much of this world's goods as they ought to have. I (Hear, hear.) As long as things remained as they were at the present time, there was bound to be unrest. Strikes in any form were most deplorable and if the workers got a fair share of what they helped to produce snch upheavals would, no doubt, be a thing of the past. (hear, hear and applause). Mr. MARK HOWARTH referred to the great difference between the Conservative party and the Liberal party Toe latter were in their seventh sucaessive year of office and had behind them a great past of big achieve- ments of social legislation, which they were not afraid to advertise from every street corner. Compare that record with the last six or seven years under the Con- servative regime. After seven years almost barren of any legislative activity the Conservative party were returned to power by a jingo election during the Boer War. Then they were wiped out of existence on the Tariff Reform election of 190 a. Whatever they might say of the Liberal party they could not say they had pandered to wealth and monopoly. (Hear, hear.) They had incurred tbe wrath of the brewers by the Licensing Bill of 1908. They had dealt with Welsh Disestablish- ment and Education and vexed the Church of England. Then there was the great Insurance Act—a measure which would vindicaate Itself in a few years. (Applause.) Continuing, Mr. Howarth said he had read in a local newspaper the great speech by Mr Harold Smith at Bedford. The admiring Tory Press called that speech a review of the Unionist-Con- servative social reform policy. He was quite prepared to admit that that in itself was remarkable because any individual who tried to define what the policy of the Conservative Party was at the present time—never mind the social reform policy—would he in the same position as a man who tried to describe a bung bole without a barrel. (Laughter.) However, Mr Smith had an imagination which was not upset by difficulties such as that. Laughter.) He made the claim that the Conservative Party were the ideal social reform party on two points. He said that he and some other young men had introduced a Housing Reform Bill in the House of Commons. The Commons refused to pass the Bill and the Government refused to allow those young men to have £500,000 to experiment with. (Hear, bear.) The money would probably have found its way into the pockets of the great landlords of the country. The reason the Government refused the bill ' was that the next great problem the Liberal Party were going to tackle was the taxation or land values and the qetestion of Land Reform and Housing Reform was wrapped up with those great issues and they were not going to allow Ifr Harold Smith and the Conservative Party to scotch and interfere with that question. (Applause.) Then Mr Smith made a remarkable state- ment. After expressing his opinion in his own way which none but his brother could imitate—(laughter) he said that he and some young men in the House of Commons had moved an amendment to try and make , the benefits of the Insurance Act become operative in</p>
        <p>13 weeks instead of 26 weeks, They would notice will. an extraorditary state of mind be roue" be in tor at one moment be abusel the Insurance of, and the next moment he was filled with a volent Bill, calling it every conceivable name he couldthinl‘ . i admiration for it. (Laughter.) So much so that the time could not be too short before the country would enjoy the benefits of this Act. Tho:t. things were merely done to catch votes, but althous° they they might go down with the people of Bedford, would not go down with the people of Warring,,t°,l3t; (Applause) After illustrating the weak way in .f.,'1,1-„,t Mr. Smith argued at Bedford, Mr. Howarth gam Mr. Smith had Letter keep his weak arguments for the people of Bedford, and not for Warrington, Where be would not get sumo a great reception, and not have ht dealt remarks greeted with loud applause. After having 0 •with the Insurance Act, and shown the utter uselessne... of Tariff Reform, the speaker concluded by asking te large gathering to form their own opinions on matter:: and not take the kind of talk Mr. Smith hurled at as gospel truth. They would then come to the concit?.., sion that the Free Trade and social policy of thls Government was best for the people of the coulltrl' (Hear, bear.) + the, Mr. E. A. SANDERS said that no matter wo. appearances of the children in that ward were and 'lilt noise they made, the Liberals were not going to O. 3 them " dirty slatternly children," and they Were going to call the Irishmen cowards. (Applause.) T„b„eid fought in the interests of everyone and they s°"" always do their best for the people. (Applause). ire Councillor WILKINSON, in proposing a compreheas vote of thanks, said he believed the Insurance '4° leo would in time be thought of with as much satisfact as th 3 Old Age Pension. Act, which had brought 11,13 r, People of this ness to thousands of homes. The people country would soon be enjoying the great benefits to ti ern under the Act which in time would be ' lately indispens Able. (Applause).</p>
