      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>imE SURVIVALS OF -1662.</head>
        <p>imE SURVIVALS OF -1662.</p>
        <p>[By R. S. Robson]</p>
        <p>%totter of 1646 Presbyterian govern- and discipline were set up within the shed Church in Lancashire and it was . into nine classis or presbyteries. The of these, known as the Warrington I wi inw, gan classis, included the parish of Aolck, and Winwick includes the village itiotis'aY! five miles N.E. from Warrington. of "'er inclusion was that of Thomasßisley tkle„arlington, gentleman, among the ruling •9 of the classis, the father of the founder 't minister of the Presbyterian as a *lg cause in the Parish. The location of vreabyterian cause derived its name ,e the family of Risley, who inhabited 1,31,11a1l in the olden time. The family and '4.411 have disappeared long ago; the son ' ruling elder mentioned was also the Iv of the owner of Risley Hall. THE FOUNDERS. " NOTITA CASTINENSIS " has :—" It 11 that in the early part of the reign , tard 1., Robert, fourth son of Hugh de who had obtained land from Robert I, married one of the four daughters 'heiress of Gilbert de Culcheth, and t obtained Risley with his wife xl the surname. The estate continued in InlY until the 18th century when it Id by John Risley, gent, and is now John Ireland Blackburn, Esq. • Hall, the residence of the family of which existed in the 16th century, disappeared and the Lordship of ' /used into the family of the Black- ad is now held by Richard Denhurst, Kenyon House, the founder who was ly ordained in 1662, retired to his r resigning his fellowship at Oxford ohed to those in the parish of like 'nom he gathered into a regular con- at the Toleration under his ministry of meeting had been in a field on estate. They connected themselves , 45 Cheshire Association, who in 1702 ordination at Warrington in which eY minister took part. Risley Chapel it in 1760, trust deed has date March a piece of land called Fifty Croft in le, Culcheth,near the dwelling-house ' Risley. THE CHAPEL. it,iPgaie's" Lancashire Nonconformity" the description :—" It stands in aUtiful retired spot with a large Ird in front containing many qrie en 8 of the dead. The building is low, da long side facing the road are two ''?'S• Bell tower on left gable, small built on right. An aisle cuts the in two ; the pews are deep and backed. From the vestry door thei a little to the right, and differs from the old Presbyterian pulpits in an It respect, being quite miniature, Dr. Halley says they were 'high, and grand, and in most if not all had three decks. Some of the pew . date 1706. New railings round ayard and new windows in the are the principal improvements, it is about as ancient and interest- I have seen. It was returned by as having 250 hearers, but 200 it. From the Risley Trust it has lent of £3l, and from the Patten It ran the usual course of such with Arian teaching from its Jening into Unitarians in the early the 19th century. According to • R. Hume Elliott's notes :—ln Josiah and Jonathan Doming and Mr. T. Beaumont filled a the Court of Chancery, and as a - Unitarian Minister and trustees I,oved, The Rev Dr Hugh Relph, 1, Dr Alex Munro, Manchester, and 3 harbour were appointed trustees Others and Risley became part of thodox Presbyterian Church of I. fuitarian section at the same time ted and erected a little chapel at or three miles off. There was a a almost amounting to a recon- hl 1839. THE MINISTRY. I RISLEY M.A. 1662-1716.—80rn1 47th, 1630, near Warrington and, ' to Calany, Educated at the school it to Oxford 1649. Made a fellow, ir,confirmed at the restoration by a A irom the Royal Visitors and the ;xuthorities. He was given a year to r5l. the matter of subscription, and rhY the Bishop of Norwich, Nov- -1662. Resigned fellowship, home, gave himself to study, and visiting in a pastoral way to :lans in the parish, and studied a that he might be of use to their r 8 Well as their souls. When ef conscience was granted at llN'injUtion his neighbours who had auditors formed themselves ' liar congregation and he was called r first minister as well as their ta AMong them was the family of Lthridish whose home was near their old five mile chapel as it was h;lahon Hall of Bristol concludes a 'al 1715 :—" I take great pleasure aicating with my old acquaint- I have not seen for many years Cver like to see again in this world. ir" comfort to think of another world, We ever get there, we shall live r'eWith the Lord. May He prepare us t,.. he`gaval thither. "Calany includes by 11)_revalence of such a spirit among f 4 kept our differences from exvte rem which would have been fiCthaPPY circumstance. Risley died '44 lv„ Year, and is buried in the Chapel tt as th"eral sermon by Charles Owen. .c author of "The cursed family," 7,t'lhatt'llee.wal family prayer. Williams' "Life liehvSepte Penry " has: " At Ordination, /t:;111) PiP4 aloe; 1692, at Knutsford. Mr. R9oe of the six officiating ministers ' taleY, Holland,Aspinwall, Cramp- -41 p 4 oler 6. t. 13. and Baddon. Dictionary of 4 1630_114)graphy " :—Thomas Risley, Whe 16, born Risley in Winwick Um re they had an estate. He took Itelsrithe Presbyterian Board. 14• M A 1716-1742.—Sen of —lsley ' • • who succeeded him on his having previously for some uts father's assistant. Born 1690 at Glasgow. The "Dictionary "aPhy " says he died in Sep- n the 53rd year of his life. , of L.his father in the chapel-yard 444144tt0 th"ela successors affixed a brass stone. Mr. Elliott says he mckttgli 13 Glasgow. 4;n1t0075t,1742-48.—Native of Horwich, 4 Pupil of Dr. Rotherham at his</p>
        <p>Academy,at Kendal in Westmorland. Settled first at Bispham from which he was trans- lated to Risley in 1742 and was minister till his death in 1748, only 12 years after his entry into the Academy in 1726. SAMUEL WATERHOUSES, 1748-62, came of a well-known Presbyterian family some of whose ancestors held Indulgence Licences in 1672. After leaving his Academy was ordained Walmsley near Bolton, translated to Risley 1748, died 1762 and buried along with his three predecessors in the burial ground where the brass on his tombstone reads :—" Sam Waterhouses VDM. Officiated as minister of this Society fourteen years Obit July 5, 1762 aged 54." THOMAS THRELKELD, 1762-78.—Nightin- gale says: He was a remarkable character. Son of Rev. Samuel Threlkeld of Penrith and Halifax born 1739, trained in the Acadamy at Daventry and Warrington. In 1762 settled at Risley among a small and plain but very affectionate society of Protestant Dissenters. Translated in 1778 to Rochdale where he died 1806. He was master of nine or ten languages, not merely without diffi- culty but with critical skill. The " Lanca- shire and Cheshire Province" gives the the preacher at the Warrington meeting 15th June 1779 as Thomas Threlkeld. Mr. Calli- gan says somewhere that his father brought him when a boy from Penrith to Sheffield in one of the panniers of his donkey—he had good precedent. JOHN HANKISON, 1778-82.—Educated at Warrington Academy and ordained at Risley in 1780 after being there two years, as was quite common with the Academy men. Dr. Enfield, the author of the "Speaker," and he with the consent of the two congregations of Warrington and Risley, made an agreement for their alternate preaching at both places. He died from consumption, May 17, 1782 at 25 years of age. "A minister of great ability and character."</p>
        <p>JOHN ASPINWALL, 1782-96, who was minister at Walmsley later of Dundee Chapel, Ramsbottom, and from 1782 at Risley till his death in 1796, was a descendent of a well-known Presbyterian family. His ancestors Peter held an "Indulgence Licence" in 1672 at Ashton and William at Winwick. Peter the father was ejected from Formby and preached in secret "where more people now go regularly than now go to our Par sh Church." The son William was the subject of a petition that he be appointed minister of the Winwick meeting at the time of the Indulgence of 1672. THOMAS SMITH, 1796-18.—" Nightingale has :—Thomas Smith, minister at Duckin field 1794-96. His other ministries were at Risley, Stand, and Park-lane, Wigan. He was the author of an "Essay on avarice' and two volumes of sermons. His predecessor at the old Presbyterian Foundation at Duckinfield, Mr. Buckley was reckoned to be the first Arian minister at the chapel.</p>
        <p>S. LLOYD 1800-09.—Supposed to be a minister who after having been a few years at Risley was called to Dot's-lane, an old Presbyterian foundation, and later who became an eminent banker in the City of Manchester.</p>
        <p>0. W. ELLIOTT 1809-14,—Student of Hox ton Academy and an Independent. Ordained in 1802 at Coventry, translated to Fulwood and from thence in 1809 to Risley. A pressing invitation to Rochdale to the Presbyterian congregations there induced him to leave Risley, and he took up his residence at Oakwood Hall, three miles from Rochdale, according to Nightingale. In 1826</p>
        <p>translated to Prescott. - WILLIAM MORRIS 1814-25.—Regarding whom the only particulars we have are the dates of his coming and going, 1814-25. He was followed in the latter year by</p>
        <p>JAMES MARRYOT 1826-38.--We find in the " Henley Distributor" for May 1830—Risley, Minister James Marryot —Socinian Probably the kinsman of Dr. Zephaniah and Dr. Thomas Marryat, of London (Presby- terian Minister) and of their descendant Captain Marryat, the novelist. There was a? in the "Henley lists" to his entry as a Socinian but there is little doubt of its trut:a, for on the Law Suit brought by the Orthodox section he was deprived along with the trustees who were of like mind. ALEX RENNISON, 1838-44.----A minister who had previously held a charge in Scotland; the first pastor of the Orthodox Section who held the Chapel, the hetrodox leaving to begin a new foundation. He was minister till he received a presentation to a parish in Scot- land in 1844.</p>
        <p>WILLIAM FORSTER, 1845-58, from the old Presbyterian foundation at Stafford, inducted 1845, retired to Bowden in 1858. A very scholarly man, the Presbytery at his death placed on record its appreciation of his many years' service at Stafford from 1839, and Risley, as clerk of his Presbytery and elder at Bowden till his death. THOMAS ROBINSON, 1858-66.—Native of Morpeth. Teacher; missionary, then ordained Salford, translated Risley 1856, and to Newbiggin 1866, from which he retired and lived in Morpeth till his death 1888. Was the author of several volumes of Divinity and at least one of poetry. His was an American degree for one of his contributions to theology Founder of the Newbiggin congregation and the Butler's Green Mission,Morpeth.Travelled as a missionary at large in the near East and around the English watering places at his own charges. Loved best the work of an Evangelist. JAMES CLELAND, 1866-81.—Born Carluke, N.B. Studied St. Andrew's and the Buzber O.S. Divinity Hall, Perth. Ordained steward in Ayrshire in 1830. In 1841 translated to Bolton, later Douglas, 1.0. M., and in 1866 to Risley. He attained his ministerial Jubilee in 1880, resigned 1881, died 1888, the Father of the English Synod at Warrington, in his 85th year. WILLIAM DUNN, 1882-1905.---Licentiate o the Church of E.P. Ordained at North Sunderland in June, 1865, translated to Risley 1882, where he ministered till his retirement as Minister Emeritus in 1905. Died suddenly during a sitting of Presbytery in 1905. J. J. GLOVER, 190!.—Educated Glasgow University aud U.P. College there. Ordained Whalley Range, Blackburn, 1901, and trans- lated Risley, 1905. Like all his predecessors, proud of his historic little church and con- gregation. AUTHORITIES QUOTED.—Among the his- torians: McKelvie, Shaw, Calany, Bate, Nightingale, Evans, Baines, Raine, James, Dictionary of Nat. Biog., files of Local Press, Messenger and Presbyterian, Notes per Rev. J. J. Glover and R. A. Elliott, "Lancashire and Cheshire Provincial" Minutes, &amp;c., &amp;c.</p>
