      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, Suly, 27, 1912.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, Suly, 27, 1912.</p>
        <p>THE SCHOOL OF THE FUTURE.</p>
        <p>As I read the account of Speech Day in connection with the Warrington Municipal Secondary School, I thought of a certain educational contest of a few years , ago, the winner of the first prize in which wriete I among other things :—"The school of to-morrow will have over its door, We conserve the whole boy.'" Those who managed the schools of yesterday thought that children were created for the exemplification of the curriculum. In the schools of to-morrow the c trolling nartive will be that the curriculum is arranged to serve the needs of the pupils. In place of machine operatives in the factory of instruction we shall have teachers who believe that they are rendering the right service when they teach boys and not merely subjects. I The school of to-morrow, standing fer health and joy in labour, will not have its work interrupted by an excess of holidays and vacations. In fact, to educa- tive process will be so wisely distributed between the work done in the school and the home that every day will he both a holiday and a work day, the school simply adapting its occupations to the requirements of I the season. In the school of to-morrow the leacher will be a master craftsman. He will be the artist and not the machine hand. He nil take the human clay and fashion it into its various shapes rather than dump it in a common mould between the levers or tradition and unifaimity. He will have before him as a model the image of the perfect man of to-morrow sound, accomplished, and beantiful in body ; and sympathstic in mind ; reverent in spirit, and pioductively efficient. The teacher and man-to-be- pupil will be ready to place thi se qualities at the service of the social purpose of to-morrow. You ask what is this social purpose ? It is the increase not alone of material, but of human we ilth.</p>
        <p>"HOUSING OF THE PEOPLE." I find in the last issue of the " Todmorden District News" a letter from Mr. B. S. Townroe, wh3 is wooing the Middleton Division with a view 0 Parlia- mentaty honours, and dates his communication from Stansfield Hall in that region. It is headed as above, and strongly commends Mr. Montagu Barlow's Bill, the desigo of which, according to Mr. Townroe, is " to enable each man to buy his own house, and live where be pleases, unfettered by rules arid regulations other than the ordinary ones f eanitary progress." Mr. Townroe writes in the interests of artiz ins. A stranger and sojourner in those parts, he is perhaps unaware that a considerable proportion of the working folk thereabout (I knwr the locality well) own their own dwellings, and that their building and ca-opera- tive sociefes, which are exceedingly strong and exist wholly for the benefit of members, confer advantages with which even a state-aided system is unlikely 0 bear favourable cmparison.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>A "BIT OF A SELL."</p>
        <p>The touch of editorial experience introduced into last eek's reference to the late Mr. Hubert Latham 's memorable flight in a gale at Blackpool three years Ago reminded me of that a an old colleague of my own on the Sa2lo occasion. He went from an inland town to the coast specially to one the flying, but whet, he reached the venue the wind was so boisterous that it seemed unlikely that any of the birdinen would venture into the air with their machines. Assuted that notice of any intention to ascend would be signalled by means of the flag at the summit of the Blackpool Tower, my friend took car for North Shure, and there strolled about the promenade. His atten- tion was attracted by incidents on the sands and thereabout, and the sign by which it had been arranged to notify a favourable change as to the aviation had been nut a while before he iealised it. He went post haste to the ground, and arrived in time to hear delighted admirers enthusiastically sing the praises of the daring Latham—but just too late to witness the latter's marvellous flight ! Of course the grievously disappointed scribe was ready to kick himself for his folly. MOTOR MISHAPS. After a recent serious moor car accident in this district, I ventured upon a few comments which no doubt some readers would resent. Meanwhile, thee have been cther local mishaps of the kind ; and I am free to admit that there is room for difference of opinion on the general qw Bijou.. In the preceding paragraph I had occasion to refer to one of my old colleagues. Another of these, older still, writes thus respecting people's "extreme carelessness "in cro:sing roads in these strenuous times -" The great majority step off the causeway without looking to right or h It, ana innumerable accidents result from this c ireless- ness. In such circumstances the m,st cautious of drivers is often put in as bad a fix as the reckless driver. Caution in crossing the roads is more and more necessary with the rapid growth of motor traction.. The pace is faster on the roads as well as in almost every ether sphere of civilization, and, when all has been said and done in respect to speed limits and cautious driving, the pedestrian has still to make up his mind that new circumstances demand new rules. In the familiar phrase he must adapt himself to modern requirements, and in this cast the simple rule is to look both ways before leaving the security of the causeway."—ln</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>TIIE FOUNDLING</head>
        <p>TIIE FOUNDLING</p>
        <p>The University of London has decided on the Foundling Hospital, Guilford-street, W., for its future home. The hospital was founded 173 years ago by Captain Coram, successful seafaring man, who had noticed in his journeyings in London numbers of half- clad infants deserted by their parents. He obtained a Royal Charter and sufficient funds</p>
        <p>other words, pedestrians must yield to smrehing road- hogs that prior right to the highway which the Courts have always hitherto held to be theirs.</p>
        <p>AWKWARD MISTAKES.</p>
        <p>The daily papers' blunders in refererce to Mr. Frank Harris, of Warrington, and the Wesleyan Con- ference, go to some extent to confirm my contention that many of the reporters for these journals are hope. lesely at sea when they have to do with the affairs of the Free Churches. I noticed that in recording the proceedings at the recent conference of the United Methodist Church, even the " Daily News," which is recognised as a Nonconformist organ, repeatedly blundered in the name of a conspicuous ex-president who was one of the foremost speakers; and, worse still, a contemporary published to a Yorkshire town in which the rev, gentleman in question had spent some years "Jilted "the item bodily without dettcting the error. We wild multiply such cases almost indefinitely. THE PRISONER'S VIEW-POINT.</p>
        <p>In these columns last week was narrated a pretty- story showing how a grand juiy at the Liver- pool Quarter .essions had temporarily lost a true bill which they had agreed to bring in against a Warring- tonian accused of window smashing, and how the prisoner's feelings alternated between hope and fear with successive stages of the incident's development. It somehow brought to my mind the case of the criminal who was asked by the Judge if there was anything he wished to say before sentence was passed upon him, and promptly replied: " Well I'd like to say Good evening' and be off home if it's agreeable to the present company ?" What a lot of stories of the like character one could tell. I observe that Dr. Alexander, of Halifax, has successfully claimed an ancient peerage that had been vacant for hundreds of years. The new Baron's father, the late Dr. Alexander, was a Justice of the Peace for the county, and in extreme old age he became so absent- minded that it was not prudent to allow him to adjudicate single-handed. Occasionally, however, it was unavoidable. Once, when I was present as reporter, a voluble prisoner, against whom there was a long list of previous convictions, was before him on a minor charge and made such a rambling statement in reply that the sympathetic old magistrate, completely losing sight of all else, broke in with—" Well, and what is your application here to-day ?" Before the astonished prisoner had time to express a wish to be allowed to say" good morning " and be gone, the police superintendent interposed with—" My applica- tion, your worship, is that the accused be sent to gaol." And it was even so. THE G RM THEORY. "No medical authority has ever yet proved that smoke killed any human being." This, according to a special contribution to last week's " Examiner," represents the attitude of the worthy Mayor of War rington in relation to the smoke nuisance which most of us have been accustomed to regard as at or.ce inimical to the public health and largely preventible. Let me give what I consider a precise parallel. A wealthy old lawyer, who at his death was said to be the senior magistrates clerk in the country, was induced to present a silver communion service to a Congregational Church on the ground that his mother was • formerly a • member of that body. Some years afterwards, the recipients of his County sought that donor's consent to the substitution of individual communion cups. Ile refused it and vigorously denounced the modern medical hypotheses respecting infection. "It's all nonsense," he exclaluied "it's led to the abolition of the old form of administering the oath. Stuff and nonsense, I say again. I've been clerk to the justices here for more than 50 years, and I've never yet known a ca,e of consumption traced to the practice about which so much fuss has been made." What he really meant, of course, was that " no medical authority has ever yet proved that kissing the book at the police court killed any human being. " Nevertheless, as I frequently took care to note in print during my residence in that district, the indescribably filthy volume in question bad been in use for the greater part of the period reviewed by the fossilised functionary referred to, and had been handled and k;ssed by one generation of dirty and diseased persons after another.</p>
