      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>WESLEYAN CONFERENCE AND LABOUR</head>
        <p>WESLEYAN CONFERENCE AND LABOUR</p>
        <p>UNREST.</p>
        <p>I At Monday's sitting of the Wesleyan Conference at Liverpool, Mr Arthur Henderson, M.P., moved and Mr CIV Slater (Swansea) seconded: "This Conference, in view of the present labour unrest, is deeply impressed with the duty of the Church to expound the evangelical and social teaching of the Bible as to the brotherhood of humanity and the responsibilities and perils attaching to the scuLisition sod possession of wealth. It re-affirms its conviction, expressed in its resolution of 1907, that the adequate supply of the physical needs of the workers must be the first charge upon every industrycand it believes that such provision is necessary if the moral responsibilities of the workers, men and women, are to be adequately discharged cud the spiritual possibilit'es of their lives realised." Mr. Henderson held that the labour unrest was indica- tive of evils, both social and economic, which called loudly for redress. Wage-earners were determined to secure a larger share of the product of labour. The awful conditions of life were due to the increased cost of living, the stationary character of wage income and the development of national wealth. The increased cost of living, amounting to 25 per cent.pressed most heavily on the poor. The Board of Trade's" Labour Gazette' hid declared that there bad been in ten years an actual drop in wages amounting now to £57,000 a week, along with the development of national wealth. In 1895 the gross assetsment for income tax was £678,000,000. In 1410 it was £1,050,000,000. Last year 700,000 persons died.Of these 660,000 left nothing,32,ooo left £49,000,000 end 5,000 left £216,600,000. He asked whether the Methodist Church had nothing to do with these awful extremes. There was no affinity between the love of God and the deplorable social onditions which we had to faoe in this country. Sir Tudor Walters, M.P., considered that the unrest was due to the Gospel, for it led men to become dis- satisfied with conditions which rendered religious life almost impossible. Miss Wallis (Lincoln) spoke of over a thoussnd women in Liverpool who earned only 55. 6d. per week, and to whom she said, only two courses were possible physical death or moral death. From these underfed, ill-clad, underpaid girls were recruited the class of unfortunates wno were the despair and the disgrace of civilization.</p>
        <p>Dr. Haigh said Methodism could not divorce herself from the great need and struggles of our time. They could not refuse to say (bat, God helping them, they would do everything the Gospel empowered them to do ; otherwise they would be recreant to their highest duty. The motion was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>DOCTORS AND THE INSURANCE ACT.</head>
        <p>DOCTORS AND THE INSURANCE ACT.</p>
        <p>By one hundred and seventeen to twenty-two votes the British Medical Association at Liverpool on Wednesday passed the following resolution : " That the British Medical Association calls on all practitioners to refrain from applying for, or accepting, any post or office of any kind in connection with the National Insurance Act, except in regard to sanatorium benefit, provided it is carried on in accordance with the wishes of the association, until such time as the Government has satitfied the as:ociation that its demands will be met."</p>
