      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>OUTRAGE ON PROTESTANT SCHOOL</head>
        <p>OUTRAGE ON PROTESTANT SCHOOL</p>
        <p>CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>A TRUE STORY FROM IRELAND. [By Stephen Gwynn, M P.]</p>
        <p>In calumniating a nation or religion to danger of penalties is incurred ; the only risk is that of exposure. Those who circulate the ordinary fabrications about persecution of Protestants in Ireland, which are the chief stotk-in-trade of Unionist advocate' . gene:ally obviate this risk by a careful avoidance of any detail which opens the way to verification of facts. But on the 12th July, the " • orkshire Hf raid" and other Unionist newspapers, tempted by the associations of the date, plunged boldly into circumstantial state- ment.</p>
        <p>Near lullucan, in Westmeath, two Protestant children attending a school, where all the other scholars were Catholics, were declared by the Tory Press to have been subjected to "outrageous insults and systematic ill-treatment. Their paretrts, poor and hard working, had been hooted and maligned. The boy had been so savagely assaulted that he had been laid up for a month. His sister bad been forced on her knees in school and been compelled to say Roman Catholic prayers and bless herself. Both, during religious exercises, had been obliged to remain in the open air in bad weather. The parents had tried to renardy matters by sending the children to a school three miles away, but the children had broken down through the long exhausting walk. The mother's time had been largely taken up in protecting the children going to and from school." So much for the story as told in the Tory papers. Now for the facts.</p>
        <p>A letter was written to the Protestant Rector of the parish where all these abominations are supposed to have taken place, asking him what had happened. The Rector (the Rev. B. 8. Radcliffe, D.D.) replied as follows :</p>
        <p>" I heard nothing of the story referred to until two days ago, when I heard it was rep3rted in a newspaper, and I have since seen the copy of the " Yorabhirellerald " referred to. I have made full enquiries in respect of children attending Roman Catholic schools in outlying parts of this district, and especially children who for some time attended a school which would best answer the description of the one mentioned. I fild that, if the story is meant to refer to any of there schools, part, of it is a perversion of facts' and the r chief part aLsolutely untrue. There has been no interference with the religious convictions ef any children attending schools in this parish, and no i Itreatment because of their religion. One of the children who seem to be alluded to in the state- ment, the girl (sot the boy), was roughly treated by a Loy in the school on one occasion but not on account of her religion' and was not laid up after it ; nod this boy was a bully, who ill-treated other children in the school, and I believe even beat the school mistress on one occasion. The priest spoke to the boy, and the girl went back to school. The g;rl was never forced to repeat prayers or to bless herself. The parents were never hooted end maligned.' Party expressions were occasionally used in disputes between the children uhich will always happen in a mixed school ; but complaint was ever made by the parents to me, and I only found out these facts to-day. (19th July). The children have now left the above school, not to attend Killucan Protestant School. The father baying recently died, the widow and children have changed their residence to Mullmgar. I have only to add that the children were put out of the school, not in ill-treatment as the statement seems to imply, but only during (Roman Catholic) religious instruction, which is strictly in accordance with the rules of the National Board. I regret that such a story should have been circulated, and if you think it necessary, you are at liberty to give my authority for your contradiction of it as reported. Youis faithfully, B. S. RADCLIFFE." The candour of this testimony from a Protestant clergyman who is probably not in sympathy with It eland's political demand, is none the less refreshing because it is only what would be expected of any honest gentleman confronted with a string of malignant • -</p>
        <p>Tlie truth of he whole matter isciven in a letter from Mr Briscoe, C,unty Councillor for the district, and himself a Pi otestant, who writes:—" There is no such thing as religious persecution or intolerance in West- meath," or, indeed, anywhere in Ireland outside cf the Unionist districts in Ulster.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>ELBOWS TO WRISTS ONE MASS.</head>
        <p>ELBOWS TO WRISTS ONE MASS.</p>
        <p>6, Perty's Close, Poplar, London, E., Eng.—" My ailment started about five years ago. It came out in the form of little watery heads which irritated tremen- dously, especially when I seemed to get warmer such las when I got into bed. It gradually got worse extend- ing from my elbows to my wrists on both arms, being one big mass of scab. In the shop I had to lean on the counter a good deal during the day which used to set it off itching. Then I commenced to rub it. After getting warm in bed I would wake up and find my- self tubbing my arms for all I was worth. I was treated twice a month but my arms were about the same, also I got one or two places on my knees. "Then I tried Cuticura Soap and Cuticura Ointment. The first thing I noticed, the Cuticura Soap and Ointment E tqp rod the itching and gradu- ally took all the ecabs away. The Cuticura Ointment cooled any places where I had rubbed them too hard. I kept on using Cuticura Soap and Ointment until my arms also my legs were quite well." t Signed) Sydney Towner Jan. 17, 1012. Cuticara Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists everywhere. A sample of each with 32 p. book post-free from nearest depot: Newberry, 27, Charterhouse Sq., London; It. Towne &amp; Co, Sydney, N. 8. W.; Lennon, I Ltd., Cape Town; Muller, Maclean &amp; Co., Calcutta and Bombay; "Cuticura," Boston, U. S. A. Tender- faced men should shave with Cuticura Soap Shaving Stick. Sample free.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>CORONER'S PLAIN TALK TO THE WEALTHY.</head>
        <p>THE CORONER'S PLAIN TALK TO THE WEALTHY.</p>
        <p>At an inquest at Southport on Monday, in which it was shown that unemployment and straitened circum- stances had brought about suicide, Mr. S. Brighouse, the Coroner, said that although he was coroner in the midst of the tich district of South-west Lancashire,the money standing to the credit of the coroner's poor-box at that time was the magnificent sum of 2s. Ifts did not want the money of the rich unless they were willing to give it. No one, bethought, saw more of the acute poverty, sorrow and distress than he did in his journeys in South-West Lancashire. Ile always felt desirous of as,isting those poor people. They had just heard of a very deserving case indeed, and he would give half a sovereign out of his own pocket. The jury made a collection, which increased the sum handed to the widow to 255.</p>
        <p>The Coroner said the amount was not much, but it was a practical way of showing sympathy with the widow. If he bad as much in the poor-box he would willingly give the widow £5. Ile suggested that some of the wealthy people who had half a million to their credit and grumbled because they received four per cent. interest instead of four and a quarter might be able to disgorge some of their money for such a case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>MR. GLADSTONE, M.P., ON THE BROTHERHOOD</head>
        <p>MR. GLADSTONE, M.P., ON THE BROTHERHOOD</p>
        <p>MOVEMENT.</p>
        <p>Over 1,000 persons attended the Shotton Picture Palace on Sunday afternoon in connection with the Connah's Quay and Shotton Brotherhood. Mr. W. G. C. Gladstone, M.P., who presided, said P.S.A.'s had a vast and powerful influence for good in the country, and he was glad to fluid that they were exercising that good. The movement did not depend upon clergy, chapel or church, but all their members were respon- sible. Martin Luther once said that every man was a priest, and that was applicable to every member of that brotherhood, and be appealed to them to be careful to first establish direct communication between God and themselves, otherwise they would not achieve their object.</p>
        <p>D.0....4.„,r-v.",..-•..."&amp;w,,,‘,...."......e.iv",,41"..‘,- iCHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB. ....,-,‘",..".....",,,,•\"‘"„...—..,--.‘"...rx",..</p>
        <p>A PEEP INTO AN INDIAN SCHOOL. A Missionary friend of mine who is at work in India sends me an interesting little description of an Indian school which I am sure you will like to read.</p>
        <p>"I don't know whether I ought to have said a peep, for we can easily take a good look at a school in India without prying into other people's affairs. The school to which I refer is held in the verandah of an ordinary house, which is just a raised mud platforrri on the side of the house next the street, with a roof to keep off the sun and rain. Any passer-by can see all that is going on in the school. What are the scholars doing? Well, that is not very easy to make out, as they all seem to be bawling out almost as loudly as they can, meanwhile swaying backwards and forwards like the pendulum of a clock! Every now and then the teacher, who may be engaged smoking or chatting with a friend, hits one or two of the boys on the head with his stick, or twists an ear or two in a way which looks as if it would be rather painful. Whether those punished have been in fault, or whether he does it to keep them in order, is not quite clear. Some of the boys are reading, others only reciting, but all are repeating portions of the Quran, the writings of the prophet Mohamed. Few of these boys understand more than a few words of Arabic, in which the book is written, and but a small propor- tion ever learn the meaning of what they have committed to memory. In the kind of school which I have described, nothing else. but the Quran, such as arithmetic or geography, is taught, so there is not much variety in the work of the school, and I think the boys are glad to go for a drink of water at short intervals for a little change from the weary repetition which goes on from about nine o'clock until three without stopping. Perhaps if you saw them at lessons you would wonder why they kept swinging the body all the time. The teacher would tell you that this was very necessary. He would say that as the information was put in through the ears it needed to be shaken down and that they kept the scholars' heads shaking and swinging so that the knowledge would settle down nicely and firmly to the bottom of the brain and not get loose again."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>WAS IT SO?</head>
        <p>WAS IT SO?</p>
        <p>When mother was a little girl (Or so they Fay to me), She never used to romp and run, Nor shout and scream with noisy fun, Nor climb an apple tree; She always kept her hair in curl— When mother was a little girl.</p>
        <p>When mother was a little git 1 (It seemed to her, you see), She never uced to tumble-down,</p>
        <p>Nor break her doll, nor tear her gown, Nor drink her father's tea ; She learned to knit, "plain," "ream," and" pearl '— When mother was a little girl. But grandma says it must be true, "How fast the seas( ns o'er us whirl ; Your mother, dear, W3B just like you, When she was grandma's little girl." DOES IT? IS IT? WHAT? It always has been a puzzle to me What sailors sow when they plough the sea. When a storm is brewing, what does it brew ? Does the foot of a mountain wear a shoe?</p>
        <p>Have you ever perused a volume of smoke ? Can butter be made from the cream of a joke? Can you fasten a dow with a lock of hair ? Did a biting wind ever bite you, and where ?</p>
        <p>Who is it that paints the signs of the times? Dues the moon change her quarters for nickels and dimes? What tune do you play on the feelings pray? And who is it mends the break of day ?</p>
        <p>And say—l'll admit this is quite absurd— When you drop a remark, do you break your word? Can a rope be made out of ocean strands? Have the silent midnight watches hands?</p>
        <p>Can money be tight when change is loose? Now what in all the world is the use _ _ . . .</p>
        <p>Of going through college and taking degrees When we're posed by such little problems as these?</p>
        <p>THIS WEEK'S STORY.</p>
        <p>A few commercial travellers were sitting round the hotel fire and were boasting—as travellers often do—of the bigness their firms. One said "My firm has more travellers pushing its goods than any firm in the world."</p>
        <p>"Oh no," said a quiet man who hadn't spoken before. "It hasn't as many people pushing its goods as mine. Can't have." The other turned round with a frown and a look of contempt.</p>
        <p>"And what do you sell ? " "Baby carriages," was the reply.</p>
        <p>BLASPHEMER SENT TO GAOL</p>
        <p>At Leeds Assizes, on Wednesday, before Mr Justice Banks,Edward Stevens, alias Bullooi.i (19),ttft9 sentenced to three months in the second division for blasphemy in Collega Square, Rotherham.</p>
        <p>SWIMMING MISTRESS'S MAD FREAK. A swimming mistress went suddenly mad while instructing a class of girls in the sea at Helsingfors, in Sweden, and ordered all her pupils to swim with her to Elsinore, five miles away. One girl swam back and called for help, and the whole party was rescued by several motor boats far out at sea.</p>
