      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>• WARRINGTON PHOTOGRAPHIC SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>• WARRINGTON PHOTOGRAPHIC SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>A Visit to Holland.</p>
        <p>Warrington Photographic Society was represented by three of its members, at the Photographic Con- vention of the United Kingdom, held in Holland last week.</p>
        <p>Arriving on the Sunday in Amsterdam, the party was soon out to see the many wondetful sights of this Venice of the North. Following one of the many beautiful canals, the Jewish quarter was reached, and one immediately forgot it was Sunday. Warrington market at its busiest time, was nothing compared with this animated scene on tie banks of these Amster:</p>
        <p>dam canals. Leaving the markets.l;;lii4the beautiful Vondelpark was visited and admired, but the greatest treat was seeing the magnificent pictures in the Rijks Museum. Here one could only sand, almost breathless, and admire he beautiful works of aye by such great Dutch masters as liembrant or Franz Hats. The figures on these canvases eeomed lifelike enough to be able to speak, and tell ciLe of the emit* to be seen when they weie painted, far lack in the 17th century. This gallery contains DO less than 1700 pictm es, the Rembrant collection I eing housed in ,a 81)1681 annexe, and cobtains the b_st of this great master's SVOIks.</p>
        <p>Monday opened the conventhn, the headquarters being at the Odeon, Amstet dam Het e members were welcomed by members of the " Motherland Amateur Phitographic ciety," who give valuable information as to places worth visiting round Amsterdam, and also personally conducted parti'e in the city. Bur ng the day Z iandam was visited, the journey occupying half-an-hour, and costing only 51. return first class. Zaandam is the centre of the timber and grain trade. Here were found plenty of pictures along the river, or on the many canals and amongst the hundreds of windmills used chiefly for sawing timber or grinding corn. In the afternoon the Annual General Meeting was held, the President, Sir Cecil Het tslet, F.8.P.5., Consul General for Belgium, being in the chair. North Wales was chosen for nrxt years convention with headquarters at Bangor. Bangor being so near to Warrington, there should be no excuse for a larger attendance from Warrington next July. After the meeting Sir Cecil and Lady Ilertslet invited the mem- bers present to afterno-n tea with them. In the even- ing a series of lantern slides by the members of the Netherland Photogi aphic Society was shown, but with a few fine exceptions, they were i ot up to the standard we expect at home. On Tuesday was the first convention excursion. The excursion was to the dead cities on or near the Zuider- Zee. The party numbering over 170 left Amsterdam at 0.30 by two special steamers for Monnikendarn, a short stop being 'made at Brock. Monnikendarn is an old and very interesting town, dreaming if its former greatness, for it was here that the famous Dutch fleets, which not infrequently beat England, ware built. From here the boats entered the Znider-Zee and beaded toward Volendarma town much frequented by artists. Here a mcst magnificent sight met our eyes, as the whole fishing fleet was just putting out to sea. Ship followed ship, each with its large red sails, its turned up prow, and its fisher crew in fur bats, red shirts, and enormously wide trousns. The whole was again duplicated by almost perfect reflections on a calm summer sea. The whole sight is one never to be forgotten by those who witnessed it. The scene ashore was not less interesting, for the fiaherwives were there in their white caps and their striped petticoats, so many of the latter being worn as to give one the idea that the crinoline was being revived. Lunch was here served, the waitresses all being in the naive costume except that they wore slippers instead of wooden shoes, and spoke fascinating broken English. The voyage being resumed, the Isle of Marken was reached. The costume of the inhabitants here is dis- tinctly different from that of Volenclam. The women wear a tight fitting cap and have their hair down over their shoulders. The men wear enormous knicker- bockers and are of enormous stature. The children are dressed FO much alike and, the boys having long hair, that it is impossible to tell whether a child is a boy or a girl. The only disappointing feature was that 6CII cely any- b ,dy had any plates left, so interesting were the places visited. The return journey was made by the Zuider- Zee to Amsterdam, which was reached by 630 p m As the steamers pas•ed through the locks into the river at Amstei dare, we were met by trumpeter play- ing "God save the King." This was followed by the Dutch National Anthem, which was heartily sung by the Dutch people present, all the English standing with hats off—a compliment much appreciated by the Dutchmen present. During Tuesday evening the visitors had the navel experience of listenitig to a lecture by a Dutchman delivered in most excellent English. This was followed by a selection of lantern slides of English scenery which were greatly admired by the Dutch members. Wednesday was spent at the Hague, where members if the Dutch society kindly acted as guiles round the city. Here we were shown in the old prison the various instruments of torture used by the Spaniards in Holland, in what our guide in his Dutch English described as the " goat olt tays." Later Scheveningen, the popular bathing resort, was visited, but unfortunately it was very much the same as many of our own seaside resorts. Here much I difficulty was found in trying to photograph the fishergirls. In the evening the annual dinner was held at the American Hotel. On Thursday was the official reception by the IBurgomaster of elmsterdam, followed by a drive by electric railway to Haarlem. After lunch there was a special recital on the famous organ in the Groot° Kerk. Later the official group was taken at BI iemen- daal, 200 members being present. In Amsterdam Mr. F. J. Mortimer gave a lecture on Marine Photographs, which, though given in a very quiet sty le, described how he obtained his results in the Scilly Isles, in winter months, with a rope connected with the shore round his waist, and dressed in pyjamas and oilskins. He thrilled his audience by the description of a wreck which he was fo-tunately, or unfortunately, able to witness, and chowei slides of it from the time the veKel was sighted, to the time it sank with its crew safely aboard the lifeboats. The photographs were obtained by no small risk, as he himself was in an open boat on the open sea himself. Friday was spent by a visit to the famous cheese market at Alkmaar. The visitors artived early and were immediately conducted to the market, where tons upon tons of red or yellow cheeses, glistening in the sun, were exposed for sale. On a busy day no less than 5,000 tons of cheese ii disposed of in the course of a morning. Words are not expressive encugh to describe the scene. There were the eager sellers, the hesitating buyers, and every bargain must be cm- chided by a handshake at least three times. Then there were the cheese porters dressed in the' white trousers and shirts, and large red hats. The cheese was carried on a kind of stretcher slung by ropes from the shoulders, the men staggering under a load of 3I hundredweights 1 etween them. The cheese all had to be officially weighed in the weigh bout e—a very fine old building used for the same pur- pose for hundreds of years. Above the weigh house is a fire tower containing a beautiful set of chimes, which can be played by hand on a keyboard as on a piano, except It requires a much stronger artiste. At 12 o'clock the chimes were played starting off with " God save the King," and finishing up with the Dutch National anthem and rot the least " lime sweet Home." The return journey was made at Amsterdam by boat on the river %man. Oa Friday evening the party was invited to a Bohemian club evening by members of the Nether- lands Photographic Society.. The evening opened with a shadow show describing the history of photo- graphy. The first scene was laid in the Garden of Eden, where Eve presents Adam not with the-pro- verbial apple but with a hand camera. Other humoratia versions of history followed, all relating to photograhy, and named great amusement. After music and refreshments each member was presented with a souvenir in the shape of a piece of modern china suitable inscribed with the date &amp;c. The President of the Dutch society also presented the Convention with two large portraits of the. English King . and Queen, executed in the same material. The meeting concluded by the singing of the English and Dutch n?tional antlumq, followed by singing " lle's a jolly good fellow " and three such hearty British cheers for Mr J Bispinck, the president of the Netherland Society, the like of which had never before been heard in Amsterdam. The official programme for Saturday was a carriage drive round Amsterdam but many, including the Warrington party, preferred to go by by steam tram to Edam and going on later to beautiful Volendam again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>CHESHIRE AGRICULTURAL SCHOOL EXTENSION.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE AGRICULTURAL SCHOOL EXTENSION.</p>
        <p>The party left Amsterdam on Sunday evening fJr home. Nes-dr was a week spent amoog4 more hospitable pe pie. The Netherldnd Photcgraphic Society deserve all praise, as it was their co-operation which went so far to make the week the success it was.</p>
        <p>OFFICIALS.</p>
        <p>The officials who must he complimented on the excellent w.y every thing was carried out were : Chairman, Mr Fr ink Houghton ; hon. sec., Miss A Bcswick ; hon. treas., Nlr Lambert,assisted by a willing body of ladies, who decorated the tables in an enticing manner, and a ctrong committee of gentlemen, who rendered invaluable help in the arrange- mei ts. The officials of the sports were ; Judges, Messrs William Barber, James Britch, Lambert and Marsh; Starters : Messrs E Britch Hindley, 3 Jones and T Taylor. Stewards: Messrs W Clayton, Walter Clayton, W Henkel, J Knowles, A Preston, Tomkins, E Taylor, aul Ii Taylor. Catering stewards : Messrs Joseph Barber, G Hardy, Pimlott and Silvester. The day and Sunday school teachers also assisted in the children's races and a large amount of the success of this branch of the fete is attributable to the energies extended ray Miss Beau ick and her hand of ,willing workers. The caterer was Mrs Joynson, Hall Cafe, Stockton Heath, and A stall of sweets, mineral waters ko. was in charge of Mrs Gleave. The round- abouts eco. were provided by Mr. Plinston. Mr and Mrs Palmer of Lymm sent a contribution of £llB. for the benefit of the children.</p>
        <p>They went out of their way to help uq, no service was too great or too small for them to perform. All we can do in return, if it is evar in our power, is to make the Du'ch poople as comfortable in England, as they made us in Holland, so that the memory of their visit would be an everlasting memory as ours will be. W.R J.</p>
        <p>THANKS. At an interval in the afternoon's proceedings Mr E Marsh said It was a most regrettable fact that Mr Houghton their host was not with them. (Hear, hoar). His one pleasure would, te felt sure, have been to come down amongst his guests that afternoon and it wouli also have been a very great üblasure to them to have had him with them. (Hear, hear). They all felt very much for him in his sic' ness—(hear, hear)—and he felt sure he bad had many a thought of them that day. (Applause). They hoped he would have a speedy recovery and that he would be given the best of health and strength to be with them on some future occasion. (Applause). Mr. E. Brach, in seconding, said Mr. and Mrs. Houghton's generosity to the parish was unlimited. (Hear, bear) Although Mr. Houghton was unfortu- nately absent, he felt certain his one wish was that they should all have a most enjoyable tirne—(hear, hear) and nothing would give him greater relief in his suffer- ings than to know that wish had been gratified. (Hear, hear and applause.) He called for three hearty cheers for the generous family, which were vallingly and loudly given. The Rev. Hon. C. F. Cross said that when they had a gathering of that character it was imperative to have a good centre in which to meet, and none could have been more suitable and hospitable than the Luilding they were assembled in, uhich had been so kindly placed at their disposal by Mr. and Mrs. 'hitch, to vs hom their best thanks were due. (Hear' hear ) The preparation of that building had involved a considerable amount of energy and time, and the labour bad teen done willingly. (Applause). Mr. Marsh se,:,onded aad said that Mr and Mrs Britch</p>
        <p>were always willing to help the parish in any way they could. They were always came to the helm at the right moment. (Applause.) Mr. Butch, in reply, raid nothing would give himself and his wife greater pleasure than to know they had contributed some iittle bit, towards the happiness of the villagers. They would ever be willing to help them in any way they could. (Applauv.) THE PRIZE WINNERS.</p>
        <p>The prize sinners in the children's sports were : - Flat race for boys under 7, 50 yards handicap : 1, C Ditchfleld ; 2, II England ; 3, J Woodward. Flat race for girls under 7, 50 yards handicap : 1, H Mills ; 2, D Moulsdale ; 3, E Brocklehurst. Flat race for boys from 7 to 9, GO yards handicap : 1, F Taylor ; 2, It Brock ;3, E Batch. Flat race for girls from 7t09, 60 yards handicap : 1, M hiley ; 2,W Brock ; 3,M Clarke. Flat TACO for boys from 9 to 11; 75 yards handicap : 1, 1' Garner ;2, J Garner ; 3, H Johnson. Similar race for girls : I, N Wright ; 2, J Brock ; 3, E Taylor. Flat race for schoolboys, 11 to 11, 100 yards handicap 1, A Bennion ;2, A Masterton ; 3, S Turner. Similar race for schoolgirls : 1, E Monis- dale ; 2, E Brock ; 3, E Roughsedge. Flat race for boys 12 to 16, 150 yards handicap : 1, A Gleave ; 2, E Tomkinson ; 3, A Bennion. Similar race for girls : 1, E Warburton ;2, F. Brock ; 3, E Roughsedge. Sack race for boys over 10, 50 yards handicap : 1, A Masterton ; 2, A Gleave ; 3, Jones. Skipping contest for girls under 10, 3 minutes : 1, D Mills ; 2, Jane Brook; 3, P Wright. Skipping contest for girls over 10, 5 minutes : 1, E Roughsedge ; 2, E Warburton; 3, S Jones. Three-legged race for boys over 9, 75 yards : 1, J Moulsdale and P Gleave ; 2, 1.1 J Johnson and James Gleave ; 3, J and P Garner. Egg and spoon race for girls over 9, GO yards : 1, N Wright ; 2, C Warburton ; 3, M Blain. Wheelbarrow race for boys over 0, GO yards : 1, II J an 4 J Moore ; 2,1 and P Garner ; 3, J Moulsdale and 1' Gleave. Boot- lacing contest for girls : 1, E Brock ; 2, E Rough- sedge; 3, E Moulsdale and J Brock. The prize winners in the adults sports held at night were :-Flat race for moo, 150 yards handicap: 1, W Tomkinson ; 2, L Roughsedge ; 3, W Clayton (2nd and 3rd divided). Flat race, 75 yards handicap for women : 1, V Evans ; 2, H Warburton; 3, M Clark. Flat scratch race for men, 200 yards : 1, W romkinson ; 2, W Clayton ; 3, L Rough- sedge. Egg and spoon race for women 50 yards : 1, A Moulsdale ; 2, Mrs England; 3, Miss Besw!ck. Hop skip and jump for men-: 3, L Roughsedge ; 2, W Tomkinson; 3, 0 Tomkinson. Ladies tug of war : Mrs Britch, Mrs Turner, Mrs J Barber, Miss Parnell, Miss Hardy and Miss A Beswick. Bandsmen's 100 yards race : 1, T Litherland ; 2, Ashworth ; 3, Head. Tug of war for youths ! James Lee, J Garner, H Garner, J Lee, W Moulsdale, NV Lunt, E Tomkinson. High jump for men: 1, W Tomkinson; 2,0 Tonikinson; 3, Ii L Roughsedge. Sack race for men, 75 yards: 1, A Gleave; 2, W Clayton; 3, F Jones. Three legged race for men, 100 yards: I, J and 11 Garner; 2, W Moulsdale and L Roughsedge; 3, E Tomkinson and F Jones. Tng-o-war for genta committee: .1 Britch, E Britch, J Mouladale, J Jones, T Taylor, 11 Barber, E Marsh, A Preston and G Tomkinson. Slow bicycle race for ladies: I, Miss Evans, 2, Miss E Beswick, 3, Miss V Bate. Skov bicycle race for men: 1, 1.1 Brocklehurst ; 2, L Roughsedge; 3, T Bate. Tug of war Married v. single; Single men won. Obstacle race open): I, E Tomkinson; 2, W Betties, 3, H Brocklehurst ; and climbing the soapy pole: H Brockleburst.</p>
        <p>At the close of the fete it was plain to be seen that everyone had enjoyed themselves to the fullest extent Prior to the fair being closed Mr Britch proposed and Mr Marsh seconded a hearty vote of thanks to Mrs Houghton who had rendered such valuable assistance during the day. Mr Msrsh said it was solely for the love of parish that they acted in such a generous manner. (Applause.) There were many visitors present from Warrington and the immediate neighbourhood.</p>
        <p>In the absence of Mr. Runciman, Lord Lucas, the Under-Secretary to the Board of Agriculture, on Monday opened the extension of the Cheshire County Courncr ' Agricultural School at Holmes Chap?" The photagraph (reading from left to right) shows Mr. Jersey de Knoop, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Mr. G:Norris Midwood the Rev. J. Grant Bird, Lord Lucas, Colonel Dickson, Colonel Alan Sykes, M.P.. and Mr. T. J. Young (prircipal of the school). "Daily News &amp; Leader" Piwf</p>
