      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>APPLETON FETE.</head>
        <p>APPLETON FETE.</p>
        <p>A SUCCESSFUL VILLAGE FESTIVAL.</p>
        <p>The villager, with his life of freedom and compara- tive happiness amidst all the glories of natural surroundings, bas oft-times been envied by many a weary town dweller vi hose existence in a thickly populated borough clouded and " perfumed " by the results of progressive industrialism and modern ingenuity, is a very unnatural and uninspiring one. Yet the mill must grind and someone has to provide the grist for it, and that function is invariably per- formed by the man whose hum-drum surroundings from birth make him long for the green fields, clear skies and the natutal life led by his brother in the village. But there is another advantage besides that of healthier surroundings which the villager has over the townsman—an advantage perhaps not el important but nevertheless it is a gain. That is that the son of the soil enjoys his leisure hours better and derives more benefit from them than does the man about town. A visit te one or more places of amusement is always part of the weekly programme of "the busy man,' and his visits are apt to be 63 frequent that they become monotonous, Ithereaa to tho man who lives miles away from these " houses of enjoyment " a visit to a theatre occasion- ally is a rare treat, and he appreciates and enjoys to the full what he witness s, because he does not see much the same thing each week. The man of the town gets settled down to his life, and it is very seldom that anything unusual in the way of enjoy- ment enters his stereotyped form of living. It is because of this fixed existence and the ftequency and similarity of entertainments that he cannot enter into anything ben dering on the unti-ual, such as a thorough going fair in the same whole-hearted manner as the Mager to whom such a thing is a rarity and in the most favoured country places only an annual event If the villager has not enjoyed the thrills and bustle of a fair from boyhood days,and if perchance one year lie is favoured, be is so captivated by the alluring attrac- tions, that he wishes with all his heart the affair was an annual event. The villagers of Appleton Thorn are similarly p'aced, for last year, in common with every city, town, vilage and hamlet in the British Empire, they celebrated the Coronation of the King and Queen and the idea of a fete with a fair and other auxiliaries was heartily welcomed by all. Such was the success of the venture that a village fete similar in character will no doubt be an annual function. Indeed the idea has materialised and this year the fete was held on Saturday last and was so enjoyable that the wish of the villagers to make it an annual event will no doubt be gratified. The date having been fixed, the calendar has been very much used for weeks past by tne children, and if one had strolled into the sleepy little village and had asked how many weeks, days, and probably hours, it was before t!se fete took place the answer would have been readily forthcoming. The arrangements were excellent and from the commence- ment to the finish, late at night; there was not a Biagio bitch in the programme. Shortly after noon over 110 children assembled at the school, and headed by the Warrington Pioneer Prize Band marched through the village to the historic: "thorn" where the quaint cermony of" bawming the thorn " was delightfully peformed by the youngsters in charge of the teachers. They joined in the song specially composed for the old custom—which by the way was revived on Saturday—by Mr. It. E. Egerton-Warburton and which concludes :</p>
        <p>"So long as this thorn tree o'ershadows the ground, May sweethearts to bawl.° it in plenty be found ; And a thousand years hence, when 'tis withered and dead, May there stand here a thlrn to be bawmed in its stead."</p>
        <p>Choice bunches of flowers were placed on the tree and around the base within the railings. Prizes were given to those who ' possessed the choicest flowers as follows :—Clarden !towels : 1, Emily Taylor; 2, J Clark; 3, Nellie Taylor. Wild flowers : 1, Elizabeth Keen ; 2, Eva floughsedge ;3, Jane Brocklehurat. The whole party then proceeded to an adjoining field kindly lent by Mr. Edwin Briteh, Thorn Brow Farm, where the child' en's sports were held and these were highly successful. The youngsters entered into the various contests in a determined manner and their delight and enthusiasm were plainly evident. Meanwhile the field was prtsenting a busier appearance and various selections were played by the band—a feature which was thoroughly enjoyed by all. after over two hours of the sports over 300 parishioners and friends were entertained to tea in an adjoining barn through the generosity of Mr. and Mrs. Frank Houghton who were prominently connected with the fete. The repaat over, is. Houghton gracefully presented the prizes to the i children who were successful n the sports. At a later stage the sports for men and women were also held on the field and proved most exciting and in some cases, such as obstacle races, very al:prising. By seven o'clock the fair was almost at its height and the ground was filled with merry people who gave the stallholders a very busy time, the swing 'boats also being exceedingly well patronised. To the strains of the Pioneer Band happy couples tripped the "light fantastic" and over all there was such an air of jllification that one's spirits became very exhubrant and the !sterner realities of life were for the nonce laid on one side and the keenness for enjoyment and merrymaking predominated. This atmosphere of gaiety was maintained until the band struck up the National Anthem. The children were each presented with a bag of sweets, given by Mrs. Boughton. The last item in the sports was a very humorous one for it was climbing the greasy pole, and much interest was centred in the event, won after a keen struggle by H Roughsedge who was presented with a leg of mutton given by Mr. Marsh. At a late hour the prizes for the winners in the evening contests were presented by Mrs. Boughton who had a word of congratulation for the successful competit</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>WINMARLEIGH ESTATE SALE.</head>
        <p>WINMARLEIGH ESTATE SALE.</p>
        <p>OVER 3,00) ACRES AND RENT ROLL OF £3,775. A remukahly representative company assembled at the Ashton Town Hall, Lancaster, on Wednesday, when Messrs E J Castiglione and Sons, of Carlisle and Edin- burgh, offered by auction the Winmarleigh estate, in the townships of Nateby, Winmarleigh, Porton, and Catterall, for 40 years the pride of Lord Winmarleigh and his successors. Messrs H B White and Sons, War- rington, were the solicitors, and Messrs John White and Co., Warrington, estate agents The estate comprised 3,050 acres and 3 rent roll of £3,775, and was exclusive of Winmarleigh Hall and park (106 sores and £202 10a rental), which were withdrawn at £6,000, the lots all sold separately realleing £80,435.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>WOMEN'S GHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S GHAT.</p>
        <p>I Goodwood and Cowcs.—Rovalty and fashionable folk , will make Sussex and Hampshire their headquarters until the opening of the shooting sce2son. Gocdwcod is the last, of the fashionable summer race rueetings,and its advent means the end of the London season, with its round of gaieties, the opera, receptions, balls, dinner parties and kindred diversion', of the leisured classes. From Seises, s miety flits to the Solent, for the Cowes regatta 'reek, and the King's sejourn on the Coast, will extend to August 12th, e hen many people will have left the South for Scotland. The Royal yacht Squadron, which hes its headquarters at Cowes, received its title from King William IV., and among the privileges enjoyed by its members, is the right to fly the St. George's ensign. Cowes is 'nowadays generally known as the Mecca of pleasure yachting, but it was famous for its dockyards and shipbuilding industry, long before the regatta was thought of. Many a gallant ship came off the slips on the Medina, including Nelson's famous Vanguard, and it has in these days, large engine works, shipbuilding yards,rope works, and sail factories. Doing Without Servants.—Some houswives dismissed their servants rather than " lick stamps," but they were very few, and most of them have since discovered that there is, after all, not much difference between stamping cards and paying for ii firmaries, the education and rchool meals of other people's children, public parks, and so on, for all of which there is compulsion, although oily a part of the community may benefit from such institutions. At the same time the Insurance Act will undoubtedly cperate as an antidote to the keeping up of large household establishments. In Victorian times the posit ion of a family in the social scale was mainly determined by the size of the erosab- lishment that was kept up, but there has long teen a tendency to break away from such traditions, and to escape from the discomforts and drudgery of running a house when competent servants are difficult to obtain. Some practical people have goze to the leng h of selling off their superfluous furuiture and ormaments, which may have been carefully preserved for generations but have never been put to any practical use in the dady life of the household. There are hundreds of thousands of homes, in which a large part IA the domestic work is directed towards keeping up Moms that are seldom if ever used, whilst other apartments are overcrowded, with purely work-making encumbrances. People do not like parting with long-treasured possessions' but those housewives will probably live longest who can make up their minds to sacrifice the unnecoessary work. making furniture anti exchange it for work saving devices such as vacuum cleaners, " washers-tip " and other modern appliances, which render Horn less dependent upon servants. hot Weather Attire.—Whilst many women suffer greatly from the heat during a spell of hot weather, others succeed in looking cool, even in is blistering temperature, and such folk have a refreshing soothing isfluence on all around them. Possibly they have acquired the art of assuming coolness, whatever their feelings may be, but the average woman usually dis- plays evidence of distress which adds considerably to the discomfort of others who are brought in contact with her. Much of this might he avoided by the pro- vision of a proper outfit, such as is prepared for British residents in the topics. Undergarments, preferably all in one piece, should be of the lightest nn I thinnest of , wool, or a very thin mixture of wool and silk. The corsets should be very light, made of a transparent ' white material, and not tightly laced. The slip, of the thinnest silk, or the lightest nainsook. Delightful cool I summer dresses can be bought BO cheaply nowadays that two or three may be kept in reserve, and, if not con- spicuous in style, they could be worn for two or more summers. Needless to say, light, easy-thting shoes, with the coolest and thinnest of stockings are desirable and the bat must be light and afford shade. Thus equipped, every woman would be able to endure a heat I wave with comparative equanimity, or at any rate, ' without adding to the discomfort of others. The Pinafore.—The new pinafore for day and evening , wear is deservedly popular, for it has much to recom- mend it. It lends itself admirably to the bringing ' up-to-date of a dress, that is still in good condition, but somewhat old-fashioned. It CBD be built from short lengths bought at the sales, and last but not least it is so very simple in make and cut, being in one piece , and requiring no fitting, that can quite sassily be I fashioned at home, by the least experienced of amateur , dressmakers. In white net, ninon, or lace, the pinafore , may be cot fidently be relied upon to smarten up a plain gown,for garden party wear. it is usually trimmed with lace, embroidery, or fringe, the latter being weighter, keeps it well in place. A Useful Coat and Skirt—All-black afternoon and evening dresses still maintain their popularity, and are worn with equal success by old and young. An invest- ment that will remain a source of congratulation to its purchaser, is the black dress, and coat of satin. But the quality must be good, the fabric soft, supple and yield- ing. The coat and skirt has the advantage of being , suitable for occasions of ceremony, or for general best wear, and if only one good dress of the tailor-made ' variety can be afforded, it is the most inexpensive and serviceable that could be chosen.</p>
        <p>Hat Pins and Neck Cheing.—Very varied and novel are the n west hat pins, which in some instances have large jewelled heads, nod are of course, expensive— quite simple ones however are often ffective. For instance, the heads of some pins consist of ribbon r. settes, or ruffles of valenciennes ; others again are composed of small silk flowers or fruit. A pretty novelty is the bouquet of tiny silk blossom., that are much worn in the bodices of dresses. Inexpensive imitations of precious and semi-precious stones forming long chains,:are in great request for wearing round the neck, and over the dress. Pink end red coral, green malachite, and all kinds of white beads, also chains of pearls—a hen one is fortunate enough to possess them— are all very fashionable. "Button me up the Back."—ln these days of indepen- dence and refusal to obey bluntly the dictates of fashion It is surprising that we tolerate so calmly the blouse or bodice which fastens at the back. To the woman with a maid, or servants at her beck and call, it matters little he), her gowns are put on, or t iken off, but to the great majority of girls and women, the present mode is the cause of considerable inconvenience, loss of temper</p>
      </div>
