      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>r r :4',47 – ' ":', I</head>
        <p>r r :4',47 - ' ":', I</p>
        <p>E • 1 • -..•••• Sc„,</p>
        <p>LI ,</p>
        <p>Winwick. thellolß Excunsiox.—The Winwick Parish Church titc)lf had their annual excursion on Saturday, when Paid a visit to Rhyl. They commenced their EttPleY in a 'bus from Winwick to Bank Quay Station tOQ ' .13 a.m. then by saloon to lthyl. Visits were paid pa,t„be Marble Church and St. Asaph Cathedral, the eB';',V being conveyed in a stage coach. Hopwood's Was patronized, and provided a very good dinner tea• Wiuwica was reached at about 10.30 p.m.</p>
        <p>Orford. CHOIR TRIP.—The members of the choir, Sunday teachers and other church workers had their a outing on Saturday, the place visited being Port. The party, numbering about 45, left thgton at 7.Z0 a.m., arriving at Southport at about Here the party broke up to enjoy the various 'actions which this popular resort has to offer. They 'het again at Lloyd's Restaurant, Coronation Walk, a good tea was provided. The return train left LhPort at 9 p.m., arriving in Warrington just before °'hlock.</p>
        <p>Walton. JR AccinENr.—A singular motor car accident 'red at Walton on Sunday evening. A car was pro- along Chester-road, and when passing three Who were walking along on the footpath one of named Thomas Roe, an Irish farm labourer, of 4tlGrange, was seen to slip off the kerbstone against c°t. He was knocked down and cut and bruised on „ toreheid and face, but, fortunately, was not °lisly injured. 4.III,EsTowN CONSERVATIVES AT WALTON HALL— /it two hundred members of the Eariestown Con- ative Club, Working Alen's Conservative Associa- i and Junior Unionist Association held their annual ic at Walton Hall, on Saturday afternoon. They conducted over the well-kept estate and beautiful 'Pa by Mr James Bainbridge. Tea was afterwarde tt4'cen of at the Stag Hotel, Lower Walton. A vote "allas was accorded to Sir Gilbert for his kindness. Croft.</p>
        <p>, rIOLARs' ANNUAL TREAT.—The annual treat for hildren attending the various schools in the parish, 'neld on Saturday and, favoured with fine weather, .'ved as successful as ever. Shortly before three 6, a picturesque procession was formed, headed by mounted characters. The Warrington Blue Coat ;°Ol Band came next, followed by a decorated lorry, Character of Britannia being well sustained by Maggie Taylor, who was accompanied by the Takee Gertrude and Elizabeth Daintith, and the Misses rtt.:-,otusson and Bertha Dodd, as flower girls. Two foirated lurries filled with the younger children were teholwed by they elder girls and the remainder of the thoi ars. The original Croft Burlesque Band with 4,1 Weird "instruments," crested much merriment, hro„cnnnibus for the conveyance of the older people soat'ght up the rear. Among the adults' fancy tionrnes Were the Misses Annie Taylor as Turkish Ada Taylor as Merry Widow, Elizabeth Taylor as arn Dancing Girl, Grace Coates as Tobacco, Lizzie tide.to,a7 as Colleen Hawn, Nellie Shaw as Italian 54nily Houghton as Spanish Girl, Mildred Coates 13.0 Girl, Alice Taylor as Queen of the Night, Leah liltton as Spanish Dancing Girl, Emily Houghton as Toreador, E Monks as Cigarette,Miss Speakman as rb and Custard, and 11 Ira Dickenson as Cowgirl. Linfi costumes were worn by Mr J A Smith as Field -11, Mr .1 Cawthorne as Admiral of the Fleet, Mr as a Roman Gladiator and Master Baden Wright olt Turpin. A number of young men also wore - representing policemen, cowboys, Bluebeard, ,b Beefeater, clowns, sailors, oostermonger, .I,"'nef, Zulu and niggere. On arrival at school the -en were presented with tea, the adults being ,toed afterwards. After tea sports took place, e Blue Coat School band played selections for lg. Toe arrangements were in the hands of Mr orne, Miss Cawthorne and Mr J Quaine. Stockton Heath, PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE. monthly meeting of the Stpokton Heath Gal Committee was bald on Tuesday evening in Cafe Mr 0 Pilling presiding. There were also mr"*.7bi:"t :—Messrs J H Clarke, .1 Hallows, F Pearson, J J MoDermid, C W Hallows, Mr Warburton It, clerk), J Farrington (inspector),and S Wright Wright. and Hamlyn, architects). At THE DANGERous (IoARDINGS. Aitt sz.e last meeting of the Committee it was decided Sy"" attention of the Blanchester Ship Canal Corn- he 'Vcalled to the dangerous position of the adver- t, 41 hoardings on their land at the entrance to the thzitall Steps Bridge.lt was pointed out to the corn an Nll,'„l'ivers of motor cars and other vehicles coming tty- 'Carrington could not see what was coming round tl?:Oreer of the cross roads, that drivers coming 444naeither of the cross roads experienced the same !tato-114Y, and that there bad been many narrow escapee ussc,Zneidents. The Committee were of the opinion (1,4 f... 4 danger ought to be removed. At the meeting qop, “resday the assistant clerk read correspondence ; ttiatn,e Company, and it was reported that the matter oe considered by the directors of the tilllposting ;11, tlir.alq at their next meeting, which would be held ' n,t sla,enond week in September.—The Secretary of `4lt e"'a Canal Company promised to communicate with kit46°,..tnnatttee as soon as the directors' decision was „anown. • ,aktis ,lewer to a member the Chairman said that in the an appeal it would have to be made to the 're County Council. It was decided to await the 1 ally it'a of the Billpostiog Company's directors before Irther movement was made. LIG4ITING COMMITTEE: ELECTRICITY SUPPLY. I )4, "TWO STRINGS TO THEIR ROW." kjob,'• whitley in presenting the report of the Lighting ttoraittee, said that the cost of 90 lamps for six Srg,,thn Was £127 16s lid which he thought was a very goy- arnount and far too much for Stockton Heath. atitii,Paid a charge of £2 15 6d per lamp whereas in ' Parishes they got the same advantage and the "umber of hours of light for £2 121. '.earsion : Is that the contract price ?—Yes. !arson: Then any discussion about being (trar- ia out of the question. (Hear, hear.) If the tee wanted any alteration they would have to Ito negotiations. Clarke calledl attentionl to the circular had been issued by the clerk to the Runooria bistrict Council in which he pointed out cognising the advantages of a public supply tricity in the district the Runcorn Rural Council ih the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee) aroached the Mersey Power Co. Ltd., who had Mtary Powers authorising them to supply irt the rural district of Runcorn under the ; Lighting Acts, to extend their main.; into the inrhood at an early date. The extension was lonsideration and should a sufficient demand be d the mains would be laid. :larks said that if the people ot Stockton Heath Company and if it was their good fortune to ")InPatly to bring the current to Stockton Heath nrnmittee would be prepared to deal with the 6tOu Corporation about fair prices for lighting Ilk. They would than have two strings to their t Wite, however, a matter which should not be cotaned to Stockton Heath but should be uto rill places between Weston and Stockton ” had spoken to a gentleman at Moore who</p>
