        <p>had said that if the mains went througn that district most people in Moore would take the current. (Hear, hear). It was decided to send a copy of the circular to every ratepayer in Stockton Heath. It was suggested that the Runcorn Rural District Council be invited to distribute copies to the ratepayers living between Weston and Stockton Heath. VISIT TO APPLETON HALL GARDENS.—By the kind ' permission of Mr Lyon, to the members and friends of St. Thomas's Class 03) the pretty gardens and grounds at Appleton Hall were visited on Sunday afternoon. The 'party after Ards met on the lawn, where a service was held. The service was conducted bythe rural dean,the Rev and Hon C F Cross, and the the address was given by the Rev Guy Warman, principal of St. Aidan's College, Birkenhead, the subject being "Approved in Christ." Several hymns were sling, end at the close of the service, the teacher, Mr Hallows, thanked Mr and Mrs j Lyon for the privilege which had been granted to the 'members of the class and the rev gentlemen for their services. ST. THOMAS'S MEN'S CLASS (B) —Thanks to the kindness of Mr l' E Warburton, a large number of members and friends of St Thome.s's Men's Class (B) spent a very enjoyable afternoon at Arley Hall on Saturday. Most of the party made the journey in brakes. The bicycle club in connection with the class also turned out in full farce. Over 60 members, together with the officers and three buglers, met in the Square. On arrival at Arley, where every preparation had been made for the comfort of the visitors, the bowling green, croquet grounds, swings and other amusements were taken full advantage of, while others rambled through the beautiful gardens and grounds. During this time the following workers were preparing tea : Mesdames Cleaton, C Hallows, Noden, Jepson, Drury, Stannard, Lookhard, Holmes, Stevens, Misses E and A Astol and Hallows, assisted by Messrs Cleaton Hatton, Maddook, Jepson and Fairbrother. After tea, which was served in the riding school, votes of thanks were passed to Mr Warburton for his kindness, to Mr Geo Warburton and the ladies and gentlemen who had worked so hard to make the visit a success. At seven o'clock a start was made by way of Budworth for home, which was reached in good time. HILL CLIFFE AND STOCKTON HEATH UNITED BAND OF Hop.—The annual trip arranged by this society to give the members an opportunity of visiting the seakiee took place to Southport on Thursday. The party left Victoria-square by car at 8 o'clock for Central Station Warrington, where they were joined by the members of Bold-street Baud of Hope, arrangements having been made to spend the day together. Special accommoda- tion had been provided in the 8.35 a.m. train to South- port, which was reached shortly after 10 o'clock. The party at once proceeded to their head quarters, The Bath's Cafe, where lunch was served. Atterwards the beach was the great attraction for wading and sand digging, whilst others preferred boating on tke marine lake or an hour's sail out on the open sea. At 4 o'clock the party reassembled at the Bath's Cafe, where a sub- stantial tea was served by Mrs Baker, the proprietress. After tea the various arcades and markets were visited to make purchases for home, prior to their departure at 7.15 Stockton Heath was reached about 9 o'clock, all thoroughly satisfied with the day's enjoyment. The arrangemeuts were admirably carried out by the Secretary, Mr G Whitfield, who, together with his workers of Bold-street society, bad charge of the children.</p>
        <p>Penketh.</p>
        <p>W. GOULDEN, OF PENK- , who won the Greenall Cup at the Druids' Bowling Main at Sankey Chapel House on Saturday last.</p>
        <p>TEMPERANCE MEETING.—An open-air meeting in connection with the United 'I emperance Campaign was held at Penketh last Friday evening, under the chair- manship of the Rev H Middlewood. Mr T D Leslie was the speaker. and devoted an interesting address to "Temperance and statistics." PENKETH LABOUR PARTY...4n connection with the above a garden party was held at Willow Bank,Penketb, ,by kind permission of Mr. W. W. Shaw, on Saturday afternoon. About 50 members and friends of the Local Labour Party put in appearance. They were also favoured with the presence of Mr. W. Forshaw, agent for the Newton Division Latour party, and Miss Forshaw. The arrangements were in the hands of the Women's Labour League and much praise is due to them for their untiring efforts to make the afternoon a success and pleasure to all. Refreshments were par- taken of on the lawn. After tea Mr Forshaw spoke a few words of encouragement to thz company, and then games were indulged in till 8.30 p.m., when a vote of thanks was proposed by Mr. Marahall, seconded by Mr. Gill and supported by the Chairman Mr. Faulkner and other members of the party to Mr. Shaw and Family for their kindness. Sympathetic reference was made to the loss Mr. Shaw had sustained in the death of his dear wife since last year. She was the one who suggested the Garden Party. Refreshments were carried round at the close and all went home feeling the better for such an enjoyable afternoon and evening. PARISH COUNCIL. A meeting of the above was held on Monday evening when the following Councillors were present :Dr Murray (chairman), Miss Tinsley, Mr J Woodward and Mr E Foster, clerk to the Council. FIDDLERS FERRY FOOTPATH. Mr Foster read a letter which he had sent to the London and North-Western Railway Company, Liver- pool in connection with above on March 26th and also their reply. The letter sent was as follows : At the annual parish meeting held last night a resolution was passed instructing the clerk to forward copies of correspon- dence that has pissed between this Council and Captain Michael Hughes with a view to ascertaining if the London and North-Western Railway Company, when they erected the fence along the c mal bank from Fiddlers Ferry to the locks, recognised the right of way or not. I may point out that your company left an opening whims has lately been closed by a swing gate. Are you aware of this? Tne reply from the company was as follows:—" Referring t your letter of the 26 th ult. I have inquired into this matter and so far as can now be ascertained the opening left by the company in the uuclimbable iron fence was for the purpose of giving access to Captain Hughes cottage situate on the west side of the Ferry Lock. I cannot say anything in regard to the matter of footpath. COUNCIL SCHOOL AGE-LIMIT.</p>
        <p>The Clerk wrote the following to Mr George F Clarke (clerk to the local sub-committee, Earlestown) :—"The Penketh Parish Council impress upon the Penketh School Managers the necessity of reducing the age at which children may commence attendance at Penketh Council school from five years to four. Will you please bring this resolution up at the next meetmg of the managers ? "—The Clerk received the following reply : Your letter with reference to the admission of children to the Peaked) Council Infants School at the age of four years was duly considered by the managers at a meeting held last evening, and lam instructed to inform you that in consequence of an application made by a resident of Penketh (igr H J Mainley) to the local sub-committee at Earlestown, and such committee having decided that no recommendation of any change can be made, the managers regret they are unable to take any action in the matter."</p>
