      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Lymm.</head>
        <p>Lymm.</p>
        <p>GRAMMAR SCHOOL GOVERNORS.--At the monthly meeting of the Lymm Council, on Thursday evening, it was announced that Mr G L Welford and Miss WeHord had been appointed governors of the Lymm Grammar School as representing the Council. LYMM ROYAL LADIES' TENNIS CLUB.—Mrs Ridgway, wife of Mr Thomas Ridgway, Higher-lane,gave a tennis party to the Lymm Royal Ladies, on Wednesday last.' Tea was provided by Mrs Ridgway and though the afternoon was a little showery it did not in any way interfere with the games. There was a large number present and a pleasant afternoon was spent. 1 CoNGREGAIIONAL SUNDAY SCHOOL—The Congrega- tional Sunday ,School held their annual excursion on Wednesday. Scholars assembled at the church by nine' o'clock and journeyed by 'bus to Frodsham which was reached shortly before noon. Most of the day was spent I by the majority on the hill where they could participate In various pleasures of a more or less exciting nature. All assembled for tea together, shortly alter which the return journey was commencen and about 9.30 the party arrived borne safe and sound. Tne weather ass all that could be d. sired and a most enjoyable outing was experienced. ANOTHER CYCLING ACCIDENT.—We regret to have to record another cycling accident, the unfortunate rider in this case being Mr John Warham, one of our leading shopkeepers. Mr Warham was proceeding froMtne Cross in the direction of his shop in Eagle Brow, but as he was in the narrowest part of the street and opposite the Millstone Inn, an empty waggonette came round the corner at a brisk pace. Owing to the recent heavy rains the street here was in a very slippery condition, and as Mr Warbam attempted a sharp turn to avoid the vehicle his machine skidded and he was thrown beneath the wheels of the waggonette which passed over both his legs. Fortunately the limbs were not fractured, though badly crushed; his head was also severely bruised by the step of the con- veyance. Mr Warham was conveyed to his home, and is, we understand, making satisfactory progress. FARM FlRE.—During the early hours of Saturday morning last the Partrugton policeman while passing Warburton Church noticed something unusual burning over Oughtrington way. He discovered the outbreak to be at Mr. Robert Oldfield's farm in Burford-lane where a corn stack was burning furiously. A man was dispatched to Lymm Fire Station on horseback where he rang the alarm shortly before five o'clock. With commendable promptness the firemen were at the station, the horses attached to the engine, and the whole party on the way to Burford-lane where they reached the farm before 5.20 a.m. As engineer H. Simpson had got steam up while on the way the pumps were quickly connected with the pit close by and the work of extinction begun. For several hours Super- intendent Kirkpatrick and his willing brigade made determined attacks on the conflagration and were at last ?awarded by seeing the fire corquered. The amount of damage done by the fire is estimated at £7O to £BO and the cause is at present wrapped in obscurity. One lesson gathered from the out-break is the necessity of having a more effective alarm at our fire station, as had it not been for the alertness of the men, the signal which was limited to six strokes ot the bell might not have been heard.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. E. IlEwurr.—We regret to announce the death of Mr. Enoch Hewitt, farmer, Lytum, from heart failure, after a short but painful illness, at the age of 61 years. The deceased, who was a retired police inspector, of " B" class of the Manchester Force, took up farming in Lymm some years ago, and with the help of his wife and family made it a successful venture. He was much respected by all who came in contact with him, and showed his good disposition by letting one of his fields to a few local young fellows for foot- ball games at a low price. It was on Tuesday, July 9th, while working in the hayfield, apparently in his usual health, that the first attack seized him, and which rendered him speechless. He lay for some time unable to move until his son found him in ast ite of collapse. He was carried home and a specialist was called in, cut he gave no hope of recovery. Mr. Hewitt lingered on in great pain until Sunday, July 21st, when he passed away during the early hours of the morning in his sleep Much sympathy is felt for the bereaved family. The interment took place on Thursday last at the local. Parish Church. There was a large attendance of friends and relatives, and on the collin were a large number of wreaths.</p>
        <p>HIGHER LINE BAPTISTS' ANNIVERSARY.—The Higher-lane Baptist Sunday school celebrated ita anniversPry on Sunday last. The Rev William Scragg, of Brighton Grove College, Manchester, was the preacher for the day and gave helpful sermons at both the morning and evening services. At the latter service he took for his text " Wist ye not that 1 must be about my Father's business ? " and proceeded with an able discourse on the child in the home, in the Sunday school and in the Church. Both the morning and even- ing services were enriched by the singing of special hymns by the scholars who had been successfully trained by Mies A L Brundrett. Miss Tanner was the soloist at the former service, whilst Miss M. E. Brundrett sang at the evening gathering. In 'be afternoon a chilaren's service was held. Miss Tanner gave a sweet rendering of " The glorious city." The scholars contributed two pieces called " Little modest violet blue " and " I'll hie me down to yonder bank."--1 he Rev. W. Scragg gave a short address on the text " Supposing him to be the gardener." Taking the Sunday school as the garden and Jesus as the Gardener, he rev, gentleman spoke about the variety and usefulness of the flowers. He said that their gardener not only knew each one of them by name, but , loved each one, and th• t He wished His flowers to be beautiful. Speaking of flower pests he showed how the pests of sin spoiled the flowers in Jesus' garden. Though the unsettled weather prevented a record attendance at these services, it is pleasing to note that the collections exceeded any previous total.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL. LIVELY DISCUSSION.</head>
        <p>URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL. LIVELY DISCUSSION.</p>
        <p>The moythly meeting of the Lymm Urban District ,Council was held at Lymm on Thursday evening, Mr W G Groves presiding. There were also present :—Mr T H Warburton (vice-chairman). the Rev G B Thurston, and Messrs G L Welford, J H Holt, R H Gotobed, 0 G Smith, S Davies, W Gould and F Lcckyer, W Mullard (clerk), Dr E Meyer de Jonge (medical officer), D Mort (surveyor, &amp;c.), and W L Donaldson (gas manager). THE GAS WORKS WALL.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee the gas manager reported that some portions of the wall on the West side had collapsed, and the manager was instructed to obtain tenders for re-building and give the work to the sender of the lowest tender.</p>
        <p>_ CARTING PRICES. At a meeting of the Roads Committee a letter was read from Mr W Allman asking the Council to meet a deputation re contract carting prices. It was decided to comply with the request. LYMM ESTATES AND DEVELOPMENT COMPANY. PROPOSED NEW ROAD. At a meeting of the Roads Committee plan and section of the proposed new road on the westerly side of the lake was submitted by Messrs W and S Owen on behalf of the Lymm Estates and Development Co. It was agreed that it be a recommendation to the Council to instruct the Clerk to write to Messrs Owen to the effect that the Council would raiss no objection to the pro- posed width and mode of construction shown on the plan, but it must be clearly understood that the Council did not in any way cor:amit itself to take over the road- way when completed as shown on the plan; also that the whole of the cost of providing an efficient sewer for the property to be built alongside the road must be borne by Sir William Lever. IIEATLEY GAS SUPPLY. From the minutes of the Gas Committee it appeared that a 6 inch main to replace the existing 3 inch pipe is to be laid from Oughtrington Bridge via. Sandy-lane to Lvnim-lane crossings. The Clerk was instruc'ed to advertize for tenders for the necessary cast iron pipes and connections for the carrying out of the work.</p>
        <p>SEWAGE IN'TNE DANE. At a meeting of the Sanitary Committee the Inspee- tor was instructed to obtain information as to the alleged sewage finding its way into the Dane, and that he report further on the matter to the next meeting of the Sanitary Committee. The minutes of the various committees were con- firmed.</p>
        <p>NEW POST BOX. The Postmaster of Warrington wrote that in accord- ance with the Council's request fur a larger box to be erected on the Lymm Railway Station, a larger box had been provided, and it was hoped would prove satisfac- tory. THE RAILWAY SERVICE. Mr Gotobed asked if the Clerk had heard anything from the railway company in regard to the resolution passed at the last meeting of the Council on the ques- tion of railway service. The Clerk : Merely an acknowledgment. CLERK OF WORKS. A HEATED DISCUSSION. The Chairman moved that the resolution passed at a previous meeting re advertising for a clerk of works for the new sewage scheme be rescinded. Mr Welford seconded. Mr Gould said he had always advocated the appoint- ment of local men but he felt that in this case it was too important a post to have one man decided upon without competition. He felt that they ought to advertise and get qualifications and testimonials and judge which was the best man for the situation. If the local man was the best by all means let him have it. Mr Gotobed agreed with the views of Mr Gould. Mr Smith thought ins matter of that kind,which was so important, that they should advertise for the beat man and if the local man was better than the others. no one would be more pleased than himself. (Hear, hear). Air Welford said he was not prepared to spend the ratepayers' money on unnecessary advertising and it was in the interests of the ratepayers that they should take the course proposed. There was no need to go to the trouble of advertising. Mr Gould : If you are going to spend about £7,000 on a scheme you want to know the quality of the man who is going to take charge. Mr Welford : We have fully discussed the matter and the qualifications of the man proposed are the best. Mr Gould: " Hearsay." Mr Welford: No not hearsay. Mr Gould: Undoubtedly. Mr Welford: No. •</p>
        <p>Mr Davies said that at the General Purposes Com- mittee they had unanimously decided to appoint Mr W Mort, clerk of the works. He himself thought he would be a good man for the post. (Hear, hear). Mr. Gould : I object to anything suggestive of the cut and dried business.</p>
        <p>--„ Mr. Warburton pointed out that they had had testi- monials of the highest standard. They knew the quali- cations of Mr. Mort, which were very high. They had no need to go out of Lymm when they had as good a man in the village. (Hear, hear). The resolution, on being put to the meeting, was carried. .</p>
        <p>Mr. Welford then formally proposed that Mr William Mort be appointed clerk or tbe se‘Nage works. His mind was not made up until be had heard the qualifica- tions of the man. He thought most or those present knew him and knew his excellent qualities. Mr Holt in seconding said he thought Mr Mort was the right roan tor the job. Mr Gotobed and Mr Gould •both expressed their opinion and said they were not against Air Mort but against the principle of the thing. The Chairman then read Mr Mort's application and it 11- decided thAt he be appointed tJ the position at tt sala4 of „C 3 3s per week. TENDERS FOR SEWERING. It was decided that a sub-committee consisting of Messrs Groves, Holt and Davies and the Rev G B Thurston, be appointed to confer with reference to six selected tenders for carrying out the sewerage scheme and " report to the Council. Sixteen tenders were received. It WbS decided on Thursday that the tender of Messrs Bennie and Thompson, Warrington, of .£6,917 14s 7d be 'accepted subject to modifications and certain omissions a ad variatiuns suggested by the sub-committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Hatton.</head>
        <p>Hatton.</p>
        <p>SEWING CLASS PICNIC.—The mothers of the Hatton Sewing Class on Thursday journe3ed to Southport. Tney left Hatton in a brake at half-past eight in the morning, and proceedel o the Central Station, War- rington, and thence to Southport. Mrs. Seeker had charge of the party, and was ably assisted by Mrs. Blackly, of Thelwall, and Mrs. Lee Jones, who did all in their power to give the mothers a most enjoyable day. An excellent dinner was provided by Rowntree'e, Lord-street. Afterwards many of the visitors had a sail on the lake, and disported themselves on the water chute and various other kinds of amusements. At four o'clock the party partook of a good tea. The return journey was commenced at 7.15. arriving at Warrington an hour late-, thence to Hatton by brake, leaching home about 9 O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Grappenhall.</head>
        <p>Grappenhall.</p>
        <p>TANNERY TRIP.—The employees of the Grappenhall Tannery Company held their annual works trip on Saturday last. The place selected was Southport. Leaving Warrington at 9.40 they arrived in Southport at 10.55. An excellent repast was provided for the party at Fletcher's Cafe in Lord-street. Mr. W. J. Walker (managing director), who was detained at Colwyn Bay, sent a telegram stating his regret at not being present and wishing them all an enjoyable day. Mr. S. Metcalfe (secretary) was in charge. Everybody regretted that Mr. Stanley (works foreman) was not present. Mr. Stanley has been suffering for a consider- able time and the staff sent him a telegram expressing their sympathy and wishing him a speedy recovety. After dinner a few speeches were made. Mr. E. Sutton moved, and F. Leach seconded, a vote of thanks to Mr. W. J. Walker and the company for the interest they took in their employees. Mr. Metcalfe suitably replied. The places of amusement were visited and the party left by the 9.5 train for home, after a most enjoyable day. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>EMPEROR OF JAPAN</head>
        <p>THE EMPEROR OF JAPAN</p>
        <p>The Emperor of Japan is seriously ill at his palace in Tokio. His illness began on the 14th, and on Friday last he became delirious. It is known that he has suffered from an internal complaint for some years. The Emperor was born in 1852, and ascended the „throne in 1867.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>"PRAYED FO R DIVORCE." WIDNES WOMAN'S LETTER TO HUSBAND'S SOLICITOR.</p>
        <p>In the Divorce Court on Monday, a case was heard in which Mr Ernest Antrobus. a wheelwright, formerly of Chester, now of Sydney, New South Wales, sued for a divorce from Annie Antrobus, stated to be living at Widnes, by reason of misconduct. The suit was undefended. Mr Middleton appeared for the petitioner. It was stated that the marriage took place on July sth, 1905. Some time prior to August, 19011, the petitioner suffered from ill-health, and went to reside' with his mother, and at that time was unable to suppoi his wife. She summoned him for desertion, and emeriti: ally he was ordered to pay ss. a week to her. The wife got employment, and the allowance was paid her until it was discovered she had a child. Counsel said that a letter had been received by the solicitor from the wife, in which she said, We are very pleased that divorce proceedings are taken. It is what we have long prayed for.--Yonre faithfully, Annie Antrobus." His Lordship granted a decree nisi.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF DR CARRUTHERS. A gloom has been cast over the Runcorn district by the aeath, which took place with tragic suddenness on Monde y,kif Dr. William Hodson Carruthers, of Halton, Runcorn. He had been making his daily calls in his motor-car, and instructed his chauffeur on the way home to stop at the shop of Mr JH Westan, chemist, Victoria-road. On arrival there, the chauffeur opened the car door, and, as the doctor did not immediately step out, glanced at him, and observed that his head bad fallen on to his chest. Assistance was at once summoned, and the doctor was carried into a room adjoining the shop, but death had probably taken place before he was lifted from the car. Dr Carruthers was well known all over the countryside, in which his life was spent, and had the entire confidence of the people of Runcorn and the country gentry of the surrounding district. He was a son of the late Dr. Hodgson Carruthers, of Runcorn, formerly of Carlisle, and had practised Ideally for forty-five years. At one time he was house surgeon at Manchester Royal Infirmary. He was an M.R.C.S.Eng. (1867), and M.D. Edinburgh (1870). A man of great kindness of heart, he was beloved deeply by all sections of the community. The cause of death is assumed to be heart failure.</p>
        <p>At the inquest at HAlton on Wednesday the jury returned a verdict of "Death from acute heart failure.' Coroner Ridgway, in referring to the lovable character of the deceased, said that, sad though the end was, it was fitting that a man of such noble character should die in harness.</p>
      </div>
