      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Ltd.,</head>
        <p>Ltd.,</p>
        <p>Jubilee Buildings, Horsemarket St, Warrington.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>BAD EYESIGHT AND DEFECTIVE TEETH.</p>
        <p>High Infantile Mortality</p>
        <p>I)'. Meredith Young, Chief Medical Officer of the Con,uire County Council, has issued his third annual report on the system of medical inspection of school chilaren. He says that a considerable number of . !hcols, despite tl.e fact that they are under the super- \ .9iOLl of educated men and women and are supplied with an assumedly competent caretaker, leave a good deal to be desired in the matter of elementary sanita- tior. The number of communications he has had to issue calling attention to want of cleanliness, leilure to use ventilating appliances, improper storage and casual removal of refuse, defects in cloakrooms and lavatories, &amp;c., has been greater than should have been the case in well-ordered schools Teachers complain that their numerous cuties prevent attention to such matters and that they are, further, doubtful as to tee extent of their authority in dealing with them. Both of these objections are, it is submitted, amply met by the regulations of the Education Committee. He hopss tbst teachers will strain every endeavour to secure proper attention to the fist canon of hygiene—cleanli- ness everywhere.</p>
        <p>RESULTS OF SCHOOL MEDICAL INSPECTION. The records of definite treatment obtained for children discovered to be suffering from som* defect are distinctly gratifying, says Dr. Young. Out of every 100 children found to be the subject of some defect in 1911 there have been 81 adequately treated, while the majority of the balance are well on their way to receiving treatment. With the present staff Dr. Young points out that it is only possible to arrange for the re-examination at reasonable periods of comparatively few of the children discovered tl be the subject of some defect. Such re-examination is exceedingly desirable if the impaired child is to be placed and kept on the narrow path of health. At present the Assistant Medical Officers of the Inspection Staff are: East Cheshire, Dr Reginald Lawrence ; West Cheshire, Dr George Jobb ; and Mid. Cheshire, Dr Cameron Gibson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>PREVALENT AFFECTIONS.</head>
        <p>PREVALENT AFFECTIONS.</p>
        <p>Each of the Medical Officers pays a tribute to the assistance given them during their inspections and at other times during their observations by the teachers in the schools. Affections of the eyes, the mouth and 11 throat, and of the skin—including parasitical cases— I appear to be the chief affections to which the child flesh is heir. Of 16 J 73 children examined in 1911,0n1y 45 wore returned as suffering from tuberculosis of the longs. In 743 per cent, of the children examined— roughly one out of 14—some external disease of the eye or defect of vision existed. Caries of the teeth and affections of the tonsils and adenoids are the most frequent complaints. Among the County Medical Officer's recommenda- tions are : That welshing machines, height standards and tape measures for tatting chest measurements be provided for the u, eof the Public Elementary Schools in the County Education Area. That head teachers should be encouraged to carry on open-air classes in suitable weather. That in all cases children who are about to receive swimming instruction should, prior to commencing such instruction, be medically examined. That it is eminently desiiable that children attending secondary schools should be medically examined on the same lines as those attending public elementary schools</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>DEATH-RATE OF INFANTS.</head>
        <p>DEATH-RATE OF INFANTS.</p>
        <p>In his annual report on the health of the county in 1911 Dr. Young analyses the figures of infant mortality. Taking the administrative county as a whole, the deaths of infants under one year of age numbered 1,836, cone' 13 a rate per 1,000 registered births of 121. In engtand a-id Wales the comparative figure was 130, in the 77 treat towns 140, in the 136 smaller towns 133, and in •Ile rural districts 118. The figure for the administra- tive county of Cheshire is thus a distinctly favourable one. But there are lights and shades in the picture, as may be seen from the fact that in the six municipal h‘noughs of Cheshire the rates recorded averaged 154, as compared with 118 in the 36 other urban districts, and 79 in the 12 rural districts. HIGH RATES OF INFANTILE MORTALITY.</p>
        <p>In the following districts infantile mortality ruled high: Dukinfield M.B. 206,41ddlewich U.D.2o4,Cougle- ton M.B. 195, Hyde M.B. 189, Hollingworth U.D. 189, Northwich U.D. 180, Buglaw ton U.D. 178, Altrincham U.D. 173, Stalybridge M.B, 172, Sandbach 166, Nautwich U.D• 165, and Crewe M.B. 162. - -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>LOW RATES OF -MORTALITY.</head>
        <p>LOW RATES OF -MORTALITY.</p>
        <p>In the under-mentioned districts the rates have been very low ones: Compstall U.D nil, Runcorn R.D. 22, Disley R.D. 26, Lymm 36, end liandforth 50. Some of the above figures, relating to both high and low mortality rates, are no doubt accidental, and are due to the smallness of the statistics concerned. A consideration of the statistics over a period of five or ten years would show different results and he would be grateful if medical otlicers of health of small areas would in subsequent reports present important statistics in this form. The extraordinary rate of 204 per I,ooh registered births in Middlewich Urban District is the result of working on small figures, as there were only 29 deaths of infants under one year of age, and only 112 births registered. The height of this figure has undoubtedly been due to the extraordinary drought and heat which prevailed. PULMONARY PriTHIiIS.</p>
        <p>This disease claimed no fewer than 589 victims during 1911 in the administr Live county, a number equal to a death-rate of 085 per LOCO persons living. This is not such a favourable rate as that recorded for the past few years, and may be due to the greater facilities for diagnosis which are now available. For some years two opposing factors will influence the death-rate from phthisis, the improvement of methods of diagnosis both clinical and batetiological and the increasing attention to preventive and curative methods. The death-rate from phthisis shows a decided decrease during the past 18 years. The decrease has been fairly gradual and steady throughout.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>LOCAL MILITARY CHANGE.</head>
        <p>LOCAL MILITARY CHANGE.</p>
        <p>Major H L Tennant, of the West Lancashire Terri- torial Force, a popular and well-known figure in Liver- pool, has just been gazetted lieutenant-colonel, and will probably proceed by the first troopship to South Africa to take up military duties there. As chief assistant to General Bethune in Liverpool, Lieutenant. Colonel Tennant has done much to put the Territorial movement on a sound focting.</p>
