      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO A RAILWAY OFFICIAL. An interesting presentation has been made in the offices of the G..ocis Department of the London and North-Western Railway, St. Helens, to Mr. W. H. Hunt, who for over eight years acted as goods agent, and has been promoted to another position at Garston Dock. The gift took the form of a case of fish knives and forks and servers, with a suitable inscription on the case, and a silver-mounted briar pipe. Mr. T. Glover, who succeeded Mr. W. H. Hunt, made the presentation, and referred to the good feeling that existed between the recipient and the staff, as was evidenced by the gathering there that day. In the name of the employes, he wished Mr.llnnt long life and happiness in his new sphere. Mr J Lunt, chief clerk, and Mr J Middlehurst, chief foreman, also spoke in gratifying terms, and assured Mr Hunt of the esteem in which he was held._</p>
        <p>Mr but, on rising to respond, was received with acclamation. He had at all times had the interests of the staff at heart, and always endeavoured to do for them what lay in his power, and would continue to do what he possibly could. If at any time he had seemed. to be a martinet, he asked them to forgive him by virtue of the call of duty. HER POOR HEART BROKEN." PITIPUL STORY AT A ST. HELENS INQUEST' What Coroner Brighouse described as a very sad case was the subject of an inquest on Monday at Eccleston. Tue deceased was Janet Bridge, a married woman, aged forty-two years, of 102, Raglan-street, St Helens, whose body was found last Friday morning in the Yaddock Dam, Eccleston. Richard Bridge, her husband, said they had been parted for seven years. He had only seen his wife once during that time. Alice Lawrenson said the deceased, her sister, had been a domestic servant for eighteen months in New Brighton, and came to her house in Raglan-street last Tuesday midnight. She seemed very low-spirited and depress( i, but witness did not ask what was the matter, as she thought it was through their mother having been taken to the imbecile ward of the workhouse. Witness last saw the deceased alive on Thursday night, for when she got up the following morning the deceased had left the house.</p>
        <p>Police Constable Conliffe said the body had no marks of violence. Pinned to the woman's hat was a note to the effect that she could not stand the strain any longer, as her poor heart was broken and she wanted to go to little Willie and Elsie. Witness added that to get to the dam the woman would have to climb a four- foot wall or get over iron railings about five feet high. The jury returned a verdict of " Suicide while of unsound mind."</p>
        <p>Opening the new golf house at Prestatyn, on Satur- day, Lord Abercouway said it was intended to develop the large estate on the seaside of the town on the model of a garden city.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Disordered Blood</head>
        <p>Disordered Blood</p>
        <p>the Cause of Eczema.</p>
        <p>In Eczema and Skin Disorders the disease must be mastered by treatment of the cause, viz., Vitiated Blood. Ointments can only re- lieve the irritation for a time, but Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People enrich and build up bad blood so thoroughly that they have cured many cases of Eczema and Skin Disorders in both sexes.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Annie Butterworth, 26 Penny- foot Street, Nottingham, states :</p>
        <p>" When pimples formed on my neck I applied ointments, but the pimples broke and spread. My hair came out and an angry rash appeared on my ankles. A doctor told me that impure blood was the cause of the Eczema. In spite of all my efforts I grew no better ; my skin was never free from burning irritation, and my ankles became covered with sores. I became so ill that I spent most of mytime in bed. " I was advised to try Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, and after taking a supply I grew stronger. Soon the Eczema showed signs of leaving me and the itching was not so maddening. In time every trace of the disease died away and my skin became, clear and healthy. I was cured of Eczema, and ArlDettlia, and my thanks are due to Dr. Williams' Pink Pills."</p>
      </div>
