      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>r – 4fligfrr."7"7</head>
        <p>r - 4fligfrr."7"7</p>
        <p>Ho-seruarket Chambers, Ilorsemarket-street, Warr:ngton.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>Yo! ig Liberals and Lodger Votes. Sir,—Kindly allow me through your cqlumns to make an urgent appeal to all young Liherald to assist the party by securing as many Liberal lodger voters as possible. Yersistent inquiries amongst friends and acquaint- ances would result in much good being done, and cur organisation will gladly investigate the claims of any names sent to the address befow,or will put applicants in touch with the Liberal agents. We also give all young Liberals a cordial invitation to join the Junior Liberal Association. Application forms can be bad from the caretaker or from the Hon. Secretary, Mr. Peter Delaney, junior. , E. LLOYD JONES, Chairman. Junior Liberal Association,</p>
        <p>The " Scarcity" of Teachers.</p>
        <p>Sr,— In your columns on Saturday you made mention of the remarks of our Director of Education upon this subject, when he referred to the bright prospects which were unfolding themselves to the teaching pro- fession. Some few weeks back the Principal of our local Training College also painted a glowing picture of the future ; and urged, nay pleaded, with parents, to encourage their sons and daughters to adopt teach- ing as their life's work. These advertisements" are getting quite a cotn- mon occurrence and parents may be led to accept the advice given without reflection upon the other side of this question. To state the case briefly :—before a boy or girl becomes a certificated and trained teacher the age of twenty-one has been reached ; and during this time the only income has been between £2O and £4O, while the expenditnre totals easily £l5O. During the seven years (14 21) the student has of aecessity applied himself diligently to study and sacrificed pleasure, and in to many cases, health to prepare himself for his future work. The parents, unless they have had money by them, have also denied themselves of many luxuries, and have suffered a great amount of nervous strain as they have awaited the various examination results which are announced periodically and which mark the sages of progress in the teacher's career. Now to come to the close of the scholastic portion of the career the leaving of the Training College. Probably this is the most critical portion of the whole period. With practically no knowledge of his fitness for the teaching profession—for it is not all "chalk and talk" as some of our town councillors think—the teacher begins to apply for a position in a school. If he is fortunate he gets a post very soon ; but if he is like the majority, one, two or even six months are spent in anxiously writing for vacancies and here again the nervous strain is really terrible. But granted he is successful, and also a good teacher—what then ? "; The prospects of a headship are very remote—at the best one in nine ; and the salary of a head seldom reaches £3OO per annum. These positions, as we have just seen in Warrington, don't always come as rewards for services well and faithfully rendered, and then the 04ss you know of an applicant the better for the applicant. "Too old at 40" is a reality. So the majority of teachers are doomed to permanent assistantship and the maximum salary is £l4s—not the wages of a decent clerk who has never worried his head about any examinations nor wasted any of his substance in mental exertion orworry.Moreover, to keep -up to the daily increasing requirements of the Board's Inspectorate, the assistant must spend his evenings and his holidays even, in working at some new scheme or system of schemes cunningly and curiously devised • in the brain of a University Blue. So we could go on ad lib and ad infinitum. With one-quarter of the mental energy and one-fifth of the financial outlay a boy can take up a Civil Service appointment with a certainty of a maximum salary of .£3OO a year and a possibility of more if merit is behind him. Parents, panne ere you decide to place your scns in a position which will give them cause to upbraid you when it is tco late to take any other step.—Yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>ONE WHO KNOWS.</p>
