      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>STOCKTON LODGE. -- Annual Prize-Glving.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON LODGE. -- Annual Prize-Glving.</p>
        <p>The students at Stockton Lodge, Stockton Heath, eagerly look forward to the annual speech day cn which the prize distribution akes place. This year's ceremony was held in the Hall Cafe yesterday after- noon, the Principal (Miss Part ingron), presiding over a large gathering of parents and children. In presenting her annual report the Principal made special mention of the excellence of the students in Latin, domestic work and gardening. The students were more success- fill in music than had ever been the case before and in all subjects there was a marked improvement (Applause.) • Mrs Rylands, of Thelwall, in presenting the prizes made an interesting speech in the course of which she said she was pleased to see that household work was taught in the school, besides the ordinary subjects. (Hear, bear and applause.) She complimented each of the successful students and had a word of encourage- ment for all. THE PRIZE LIST.</p>
        <p>CERTIFICATES. OXFORD LOCAL; JULY 1911.-8011 Plant and Gladys Hughes, Junior. INCORPORATED SOCIETY OF MUSICIANS, DEC. 1911.— Dolly Hammen, Grade 11. INCORPORATED SOCIETY OF MUSICIANS, JULY 1912. Grade IV. (honours) : Gladys Whitlow. Grade 111. (honours): Sybil Plant and Doris Butterworth. Grade 111. (pass) : Gladys Hughes and Dolly Hammen. Grade 11. (pass) : Elsie Booth, Sybil Gunner, Irene Bennett and Phyllis Hammen. Grade I. (honours) Beatrice Trampleasure. Grade I. (pass) : Nora Knowles, Alice Wilson and Mary Bennie. THE ROYAL DRAWING SOClETY.—Division 11. (honours) : Rozabel White, Hilda Geary, Doris Gunner, Joyce Warburton, Mary Mclntyre, Pauline, Johnson. Division 11. (pass) : Doris Butterworth' , Mina Johnson, Sybil Plant, Phyllis Hammon Sybil Gunner, Gladys Hughes, Louie Bennie,lthona Flamman. Division I. (honours) : Rozabel White, Hilda Geary, Doris Butterworth, Rhona Hammon, Doris Gunner, Paulin% Johnson, Phyllis Hammen, Louie Bennie, Mina Johnson, Joyce Warburton, Gladys Hughes. Division I. (pass) : Sybil Plant, Mary Mclntyre, Sybil Gunner. ENGLISH.—Form V., Rozabel White • Form IV., Dorothy Griffiths ' • Form 111., Beatrice Trampleasure ; Form IL, Barbara Johnson. ARITHMETIC.—Form V., Kathleen Cooplr; Form IV., Dorothy Grifaths ; Form 111., Rhona Hammen ; Form IL. Claudia Griffith 1. FRENCH.—Form V., Gwen Thomas; Form IV., Joyce Warburton ; Form 111. Beatrice Trampleasure. LATIN.—Form V., Gwen 'Armes Form IV., Doris Gunner and Hilda Geary; Form 111. (Upper), Gladys Naylor; Form 111. (Lower), Dorothy Halliwoll. BOTANY.—SybiI Gunner. GARDENING.—Doris Butterworth. Music.—Sybil Plant. SEWING.—Muriel Trampleasure (Upper), Mary Gandy (Lower), Ellie Crawford (Kindergarten). The prize distribution over, the usual excellent concert was held. It was opened by a pianoforte solo skilfully played by Mr W Darlington (visiting professor at Stockton Lodge). A number of the girls gave a pretty action song, " Nellie's Dolly " in it charming manner, which received the approbation of all. Miss K Grocott and Miss Edith B Partington rendered the duet " The Princess of Kensington " in a finished style, and Miss Dorothy Halliwell delighted the company with her excellent dancing. "Merry Little Milkmaids" was the title of the next action song taste- fully given by the scholars. Miss Paulina Johnson played "Lee Sylvains " on the piano, and her efforts proved highly successful. Three of the students presented " The Banishment of Rosalind," a scene from • As you like it," in effective style, and the characters were Duke Frederick, Mina Johnson ; Rosalind, Phyllis Hammen; Celia, Doris Butterworth ; the song "Caller Herrin" was characteristically given by the students, and Miss Gladys Whitlow greatly pleased with her pianoforte solo "Nocturne" (Dohler)lMlss Edith B Partington rendered the song " Serenade" (Strauss), in a feeling manner and the song and drill "Poppies" was cleverly given by a number of the girls. Miss Alice Wilson did a scarf dance in a graceful manner and Miss Doris Gandy pleased the audience with a pianoforte solo. The concluding item was a song and coon dance creditably given by the Misses Sybil Plant, Gladys Hughes, Gwen Thomas and Alice Wilson. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded Mrs Rylands on the proposition of Mrslßalph Johnson seconded by Mrs Plant,of Crewe,and supported bylMr.Aubrey White. Tea was afterwards partaken of at Stockton Lodge and subsequently the exhibition of painting, carving, needlework, &amp;0., was inspected, the parents and students having had a most enjoyable time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>ORONATION MEDALS FOR POLIC</head>
        <p>ORONATION MEDALS FOR POLIC</p>
        <p>oto Burrell. EF CONSTABLE (MR. M. NICHOLT,S.)</p>
        <p>cm:</p>
        <p>applause). The police force of this country hid most important work to do in the executive administration of the law and ii) maintaining order. The public did not always feel so grateful to the force as thoy ught to do, but be was certain that the citizens oF \Warring- ton bad ciituplete confidence in and esteem and respect , for every member of the force, from the Chief down- wards. Although there were only five King'. medals, the recipients had been so carefully chosen a- to repre- sent every section of the force. Of course, Royalty had to cmsider economy like other people and no doubt on that ground alone, as the whole country had to be taken into account, the presentations were some- what limited. The M2O-,.;1 then made the presentitions, beginning with the King'a med.ds. The Chief Constable was the first reilpient Then the roll of the free ws called and each man came forward to receive hie medal at the hands of the Mayor. Additiolal interest ' vir,s given to the proceedings by the announcement of sevliral roinotions. At the close of the ceremony The CIIIEF CoNsTABLE briefly voiced the thanks of the force to the Ma; or and members of the c minittee fr their presence The large ai tend ,nce was an ev:dence that the force was appreciated. Toe medals would he prized as heirlooms ii common life just as highly as notable posse-sions were treasured and handed down by the I mit; of the laud. (A.op'ause). Tire DF.PUTI" MAYou then presented to the Mayor a duplicate C ,ronatian medal as a memento of the occasion. He also s,conded the vote of thanks to his Worshio who, he ssid, performed the high duties of his office in a very excellent and admirable manner. The vote was carried with loud cheers. The Ilivort, in reply, said he had always regarded himself as one of the force but now that he had received the medal he felt he was an actual member—though physically a very insignificant force. (Laughter).</p>
