      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>Buckley —Davenport. A pretty wedding took place at Our Lady's Church, Latchford, on Thursday when Mr. John Anthony Buckley, son cf Mr. P. Buckley, Chelsea Perk-road, Beckenham, was married to Miss Katie Davenport, daughter of Mr. C. W. Davenport, Holly Cottage, Stockton Heath The Rev. Father Tallon officiated. The bride, who was given away by her father, was charmingly attired in Duchesse satin, trimmed with Honiton lace and orange blossom. with Limerick lace veil. She carried a shower bouquet of lilies, white roes and white heather and were a gold locket and chain, the gift of the bridegroom. The bridesmaids were Miss Susie Davenport (sister of the bride) and Miss Mabel Buckley (sister of the bridegroom). Miss Daveuiort w ore a pretty dress of pale blue Duchesse satin, with gold trimmings, and Italian straw hat trimmed with pale bffie. Mies Buckley was becomingly attired in pale pink satin with hat to match. Mr. K. Buckley (brother of the bridegroom) discharged the duties of best man. Mr. R. 6hacklady was at the ergan and played choice seleetions. After the cerswohy a reception was held at the Hall Cafe. Later the happy couple left for their honeymoon tour in Jeisey and France, the bride travelling in a tailor- made costume, witn hat to match. Many nandsome presents were received.</p>
        <p>Dwerryhouse--Davies. St. Mary's Church, Great Sat,key, was the scene of a wedding in which great interest was taken on Wednesday when Mr. Wilfred Dwerryhouse, second son of Mr. Samuel Drverrybouse, U'arrington-road, Penketh, was married to Miss Isabella Davies, daughter of Mr. George Davies, Belmont, Chapel-road, Penketh. The bride and bridegroom are well known in the villages of Panketh and Sankey, and there was a large congregation to witness the cere- mony. The Vicar of Sankey (Rev. J. R. Jones) officiated. The service was fully choral, Miss Standish being at the organ. The bride, who was given away by her father, looked charming in a gown of ivory satin charmeuse,the corsage being com- posed of draped ninon and silver net. The trained skirt was embroidered with true lovers' knots and seed pearls. She wore a chaplet of orange blossom with Mechlin veil. The bridesmaid was Miss Annie Davies (cousin of the bridegroom), who was attired in Rose du Barry silk cord with chemisette of aluminium net, and a large black picture hat trimmed with willow plumes to match the gown. Her bouquet was composed of pink carnations and her ornament was a gold pendant, the gift of the bridegroom. Mr. Arthur Dwerryhouse t(brother of the 'bride- groom) acted as best man. The reception was held in the Wesleyan Schoolroom, Penketh, a large number of guests being present. Amid the hearty congratulations of their friends the happy couple left for Port Erin, Isle of Man, where the honeymoon is being spent. The bride travelled in a smart navy suiting with tegal hat to match, with a chic mount of white wings. Many beautiful presents were received.</p>
        <p>• PALACE AND HIPPODROME. The famous Craggs are a big attraction at the Palace and Hippodrome this week, and their latest , comedy sketch "Billy," the new steward of the Channel Queen, is screamingly funny. There are very few troupes of acrobats aud tumblers who are on the same plane as the Craggs, while " Billy " stands in a class by himself. He acts the simpleton with rare skill, and introduces the drollest knock-about business imaginable. We had never laughed more at a turn of this type. Humour is the predominant note this week, and the return visit of Lillie Soutter ensures more mirth. The buxom Lillie has a style of burlesque quite her own, and few artistes get more out of their songs than she does. There are no dull moments while Miss Soutter occupies the boards. Arthur Astill earns hearty applause as the whistling ploughboy, while his farmyard imitations are realistic. A bright show is that produced by "Haley's Garden of Girls" in their vocal and dancing Bona. These ten pretty maidens ace generously endowed with talent of ' a varied order, and whether performing singly, in quartettes, or groups they never fail to entertain. The" scene "is well mounted and well-lit electrically. Quite the oddest thing in the way of comedians is S W Wyndham, whose voice may best he described as a " skrike." The very absurdity of his vocal efforts compels a laugh, but a little more contrast between his various numbers would be an improvement. Three songs in succession pitched in the steam-whistle key are a bit trying for the ear-drums. Molly Sayers, comedienne, and Phil Kauffman the popular coon, contribute to the harmony of the evening with tuneful songs. " NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. The top place on a good bill for next week is astrethdebysePwalomaanode, theSel p l e ryo in "A clumsy acrobat pular comedians Other es , turns to appear are James Hares Co., in a humorous '!L Marco Belli andwe Walker, dramaticwo od wooden episodeehoe " W ro o tins"; Riche and wizards; French comedy jugglers; Sisters Royce and Sid. vocalists and dancers; Andie Burns, comedian and dancer; Sam Kelly, a Scotch comedian—only different; and Fred Camplin, comeiian.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>MAYOR DECORATIYG THE CORONATION MEDALS. Interesting Presentations to Warrington POIIC3.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR DECORATIYG THE CORONATION MEDALS. Interesting Presentations to Warrington POIIC3.</p>
        <p>There was an echo of the Coronation celebrations on Thursday afternoon, when the Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph) presented commemoratory medals to the members of the Borough Police Force. Every policeman who was on duty at the tiuue of the Corona- tion was the recipient of a bronze medal, suitably inscribed, the gift of the Corporation. Five racers, representative of the various ranks in the force, were selected for special recognition, the King's silver police Corouation medal being (-inferred uiann them Thesis officersare :—The Chief Co.,siab(e (Mr. M. Nicholls), Chief Inspector Williamson. Sergeant Frank Cooper who has just retired on pension), Detective S.las Clough and Constable J Millington. The ceremony took place in front of the Town Hall at the close of the usual meeting of the Watch Com- mittee. Amongst those present were: Toe Mayor (Councillor Dr Joseph), thus Deputy-mayor (Alderman Forshaw), Aldermen Bolton. :lames Evans. Ttnnion and Burton ; Councillors Birtles. Craik, Wilkinson, Sankey, Dickinson, Cunitington and Ilalliwell, Messrs J Lyon Whittle (town cieik), J Fairhurst (borough surveyor), M Nicholls (chief constable), G F F White (magistrates' clerk). and Segar Owen. The Maton characterised the presentation of the King's medals as a very auspicious event. When the medals were forwarded to the Cnief Constable, the Secretary of State requested that they should be presented in a manner befitting the occasion and he (the Mayor) considered it appropriate that the duty should devolve upon him as chief citizen—he might almost say as chief policeman. (Laughter and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>GOLDEN WEDDING.</head>
        <p>GOLDEN WEDDING.</p>
        <p>On Saturday last a larg•A number of members of the Bewsey Adult School gathered together to do honour to two of the most energetic and willing mem- bers, namely Mr and Mrs Healey, upon the r ccasion of their golden wedding. Tea was provided for about 60 members at 6 p.m , and although the company were somewhat handicapped as regards space, the gathering proved a huge succ-as.Mr. W Webster kindly to ,k the chair, and in opening the meeting spoke for some time upon the Adult School work in general. The ww thy President, Mr. Harold Gray, then addressed the meeting, after which he presented to Mr. and MN. Healey a handsome present iii the form of a marble clock. In accepting the presentation Mr. Healey went on to say how much he appreciated the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>MR. AND MRS. HEALEY.</head>
        <p>MR. AND MRS. HEALEY.</p>
        <p>good feelings that had been shown to him upon this occasion, and that he valued those feelings far more than the clock. Mrs. Healey also made a few suitable remarks. Mr. Prince also r•poke and hoped that they tvsuld all have the pleasure of commemorating Mr and Mrs Healey's diamond wedding. The programmeof the evening commenced at 3.30. Each itcm brought forth much applause. Below we give the programme : Song, "Simon the Cellarer." by Mr A Pearse ; song, "Locks, Bolts and Bars," Mr A Pearse (encored) ; quartette, " The Love of Man for Man," Bewsey Male Voice Quartette ; song, "I love the dear old home songs,' Mrs W H Parkes ; recitation, The French Horn," Mr Harry Wright ; song, "Into Thy Hands," Mr. Walter Bennett, jun. ; recitation, " Staffordshire Dialect," Mr 'l' Whittle ; quartette, " Spin, Spin," Bewsey Male Voice Quartette ; song, "The Broken Bow," Mr. Holyhead ; humorous song, " Shirts," Mr I Frank Bibby; pianist, Miss G Atherton.</p>
