      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>MOTOR TRAGEDY. WARRINGTON MOTORIST IN FATAL ACCIDENT.</head>
        <p>MOTOR TRAGEDY. WARRINGTON MOTORIST IN FATAL ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>A fatal motor car accident occurred at Blackpool on Thursday night, the victim being a Heckmoudwike lady, who was knocked down by a motor car driven by Mr. Thomas Outwin, of "Thurston," Latch- ford Without, Warrington. The scene of the fatality was the central portion of the promenade, and the tragedy deeply affected the huge crowds of holiday makers who were leavina the places of amusement. The victim, Miss Ethel Atkinson, 25 years of age, was accompanied by a distant cousin, named Emily Ann Atkinson, also from Hecknaondwike, both of whom were staying at Blackpool, and a gentleman friend named Herbert Walters, at' present on a visit from South Africa, whom they had met earlier in the even- ing. They were crossing the promenade from Victoria-street, when a motor car approached from a northerly direction. The three visitors were in the act of crossing the promenade when the driver of the car sounded his horn. Mr. Walters and Miss Emily Atkinson drew back, but the deceased ran forward. The driver of the car strove to avoid the impending accident, but Miss Ethel Atkinson was knocked down, and the wheels passed over her, rolling her over several times. The driver of the car pulled up in about ten or fifteen yards, and upon learning of the serious character of the accident drove off to the Police Station. The young lady, whose condition was obviously critical, was carried into the Tower Cafe, adjoining, and Dr. Richardson, who lives near, was summoned, but despite his attention the young lady expired within a few minutes.</p>
        <p>The inquest was proceeding at the Blackpool Police Court at a late hour last night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>SPEECH DAY.</head>
        <p>SPEECH DAY.</p>
        <p>GRAMMAR SCHOOL PRIZE DISTRIBtiTION.</p>
        <p>BISHOP ON " PLAYING THE GAME."</p>
        <p>Speech Day—the great day of the year to students of the Boteler Grammar School—was observed on Thursday when the Bishop of Liverpool (Right Rev. Dr. Chavasee) distributed the plizes in the Parr Hall. Parents and friends of the pupils assembled in large numbers and the boys themselves added much to the zest of the proceedings by their pleasing songs and lusty cheers. The Rector (Rev. Canon Willis) presided and was accompanied on the platform by the Bishop of Liverpool, the Revs. Canon Penrhyn and E. C. E. Carleton, Alderman Bennett, Messrs. Vera B. Davies, W. Peter Rylands and C. S. Burgess, the Rev. Horace Gray (headmaster) and members of the staff.</p>
        <p>THE RECTOR ON RELIGIOUS INSTRUCTION. The RECTOR devoted a short introductory address to the subject of religious instruotion. Dealing with the elementary schools, he pointed out that the population of Warrington was over 72,000, of whom 12,000 were scholars in the elementary school. Of those, 8,000 were receiving denombuational religious instruction-6,000 in the Church schools and 2,000 in the Roman Catholic schools. Four thousand were receiving undenomina- tional religious instruction-2,000 in the British and Wesleyan schools and 2,000 in the Council schools. He had nothing but praise for the syllabus of religious instruction in the Council scbools, so far ar. it went. The committee could not do more than they were doing. In addition to the 12,060 scholars in the elementary schools, there were 300 in the two secondary schools- -100 in the Boteler Grammar School and 200 in the Municipal Secondary School. In the Grammar School religious instruction and Church teaching were given but he deeply regretted to say that in the Council School there was no religious instruction whatever. What he wanted to punt out was that the matter rested entirely first of all with the parents and then with the committee. If tha parents made an applicatior, it rested with the committee to grant it or nit, as they thought well, but hi would very earnestly impress upimn all parents and the children attehditig that school to make application and he sincerely trusted that the comintweee would giant it. 3 heir outy also was to urge upon members of Paniament that whenever a new Act was passed the " right of entry " should be given for the religious instruction of children according to 'the faith of the parents. In the meantime they must make the very most of their Sunday 6chuols. What he earnestly felt was that Church parents ought never to be satisfied with a lything less th in the Bible, of which we ought at to know the history and the contents, the three Cr,ieds and the Sacraments. BOTELER SCHOLARShIPS.</p>
        <p>The RECTOR then announced that St a meeting of the Go‘ertiois n Wtcli e diy the following boys were elected to Beteler scholarships :—C Hayes, E Jeffery, W Aiagins It A Bunch and A Ferguson. The toll 'wing seiolarships viele renewed :—J C F Fern, W Jennings, If T .I.flues,J Jenks, C W Healey, W R Wiltiis. L J Vite. E Johnson, H Booth, C F R.Aerts, .1 WHeock, 11. R Hope, J R Griffiths, J Stialieshaft.</p>
        <p>The I,dlowing were elected to entrance scholarships, on the result seceut eisadusti.Jus :—P Haze .lad W H t"..yton. HEADMASTER'S RI, PORT.</p>
        <p>The HEADMASThR. in submitting his fifth annual report, said the traditiLms for successtul work at the school bad been fully maintained during the past year. Twelve menthe ago he dwelt upon the criticisms and recommendatioci. of H.M.'s Inspectors as contained in their report of the full inspection of the school He anticipated that the recognition which it was possible for him to give to the same would prove ben.ficial to the general efficiency of the school. The chief sugges- tion was that the teachiog of each subjeut should be as much as possinte in the hands of the master who had made such his sptcial study. That naturally had been the keneral practice with them, but they had found it possible in one or two instances to extend it. The teaching of English had been considerably strengthened. On the departure of Mr. Greer for Cheltenham, they secured the services of fur. Dalby, who had passed through the English Literature School at Oxford. Some two or three years ago in his report he made mention of the tact that some of the boys showed evident mathematical ability, and that if they elected to specialize in that branch of study they would be likely to meet with success. His prediction had, he was glad 'to say, been amply justified. After a lapse of nineteen years THE SCHOOL COULD ONCE AGAIN PRIDE TIERSELF on having obtained a mathematical exhibition at Cam- bridge. (Applause.) Their congratulations went out to Woodcock, the head boy, who had brcught that dis- tinction to the school. (Applause.) Such an achieve- ment was all the more meritorious when one bore in mired the strictly limited time which he (the Head- master) had been able to devote to his (Woodcock's) special work. It seemed to augur well for his future that the trustees of the Willcocks Memorial Fund I should have found themselves abea to grant a sum of £35 from their trust in aid of an exhibitioner who went trom the school to that college of which Mr. Willcocks was in his time a schelar. (Hear, hear.) As Woodcock I had made that good beginritig, he trusted teat his University career would bring him much credit. (Applause.) Another boy of the same period who passed on from the sixth form to the Manchester School of Technology had just completed his course at that institution and had graduated in honours. They were gratified at his success, and had marked the number of special distinctions be had gained. (Applause.) It would be recollected that he (the Headmaster) also mentioned chemistry and physics, which were to receive fuller attention. Their environment naturally demanded that, and be felt hound on that occasion to recognize the excellent work which Mr. Pearson had accomplished. He would have the pleasant recollection that one of his pupils succaeaed in obtaining for the school</p>
        <p>FOR THE FIRST TIME IN ITS HISTORY an exhibition of £5O tinder the Archbishop of Canter- bury's Oxford and Cambridge Fund for Chemistry and Physics at Selwyn College, Cambridge. He felt sure that the governing body would wish him to convey their sincere regret that owing to medical reasons the successful boy, Banks, had to forego the advantage offered by that distinction. (Hear, hear.) The Civil Service had continued to attract the attention of the older boys. Three had already presented themselves for examination, and others were reading with that end in view. In December last Thomas gained the 11th place out of 260 candidates in the open competitive examina- tion for junior appointments in the Admiralty, whilst Weaver was placed 78th out of over 2,000 candidates in the second division clerkships. These results were, of course, obtained direct from the school, which tclearly demonstrated the extremely sound nature of the teach- ing given and the wide curriculum covered. In the case of the third candidate, they were awaiting the publica- tion of the list. The details of the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>OXFORD LOCAL EXAMINATIONS</head>
        <p>OXFORD LOCAL EXAMINATIONS</p>
        <p>had been laced in their hands. Eleven candidates were placed in the honours division, six securing first- class honours. There were also eleven special distinc- tions. Considering that those boys who had previously gained the Oxford certificate did not enter for this examination, these results must be held to be eminently satisfactory. (Applause.) The upper school had this year been examined by the Oxford Local Delegacy, and the report, which was only received a few hours ago, would be considered at a later date. Having read one or two extracts from it, the Headmaster, turning to the SUCCESS OF OLD 11)TELERIANS said they had a much more prominent list this year than last year. At Cambridge H Hands had graduated in honours in the law tripos, whilst B Browne, their exhibitioner, had strengthened his position at St. John's College by obtainining a first class in classics in his college examination. It was hoped he might have the gratification of securing a similar position in his tripos next year. (Hear, hear, and applause.) At Oxford R Wilcox and 3 H B Dawson had completed their course, but the concluding viva examination remained to be taken. J Bunch, to whom be had previously referred, had passed with honours the final examination for ILSc Technical in mechanical engineering at Manchester University, and had been awarded the Schuster Research Scholarship as also the Michael Longridge engineering prize. A Wright had proceeded to the M.A. degree at the same university. F A Ruddock had obtained the Associate. ship in metallurgy at Sheffield University. At Liver- pool University R Kennon bed been elected to the Holt Fellowship in physiology of the value of £3OO, and R Lea had passed the examination for the final M.B. They noticed with pleasure, the appointment of another "old boy,' G W N Joseph, as deputy medical officer of health for the borough of Warrington. To all these " old boys' the school offered her sincere con- gratulations. (Applause). The successes which had fallen to their share during the past year should aniline to convince parents that when a boy was entrusted to their care he had every opportunity of preparing him- self for either a commercial or a professional career : but he would point out that competent as the staff undoubtedly was, in order to obtain a result honourable to the school, and pleasing to the parents, the pupils themselves must make on their part, the requisite effort. Unfortunately, one had noticed during the past year that several bad preferred to MUDDLE THROUGH. THE SLOUGH OF INDOLENCE rather than to ride on the crest of the wave of energy. He felt that the attention and power of the staff had been taxed to too great an extent by one or two such cases. He trusted that that public reference would bring about the desired improvement, and he would impress upon the parents the importance of extending to the school their co-operation. The governing body were always desirous of promoting the best interests of the scholass. As an instance of that he would mention the free provision by them during the year of tuition in a subject which to-day was of vital importance in commercial life, viz., shorthand. He was glad to say that the majority of the boys in the upper school had</p>
        <p>availed themselves of that opportunity and were doing good work. He would remind parents of the existence of the preparatory class for young boys in connection with the school. It was fulfilling its purpose, and he hoped the school year, which began with next term, would find several new names on the roll.</p>
        <p>He had not up to that point madeany special referenca to either classics or modern languages. It was evident from what he had read from the examiner's report, that those subjects, in spite of the extra attention they had given to science, had not suffered in the least degree. They were still in the capable hands of Mr. Rossiter and Mr. Herzog. The Warrington Chamber of Com- merce had again that year presented prizes for the best essays on " The Duties of Citizenship." The</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>SCHOOI, GAMES</head>
        <p>SCHOOI, GAMES</p>
        <p>_ had continued much the SAllle, the respective teams in cricket and foothall having met with very fair success. They would be pleased to hear that last season an " Old Boys' Football Club was formed. That was able to place two teams in the field, end they certainly did well. He urged those boys who remained In that neighbourhood, after leaving school, to give that club the support it thoroughly deserved. The annual " old bays"' dinner was again held in the big schoolroom, and was a most enjo3able !Unction. A pleasing feature was the presence of a large number of younger" old boys." He had to express his indebted- ness to all the members of the staff for their hearty co-operation. That, he need hardly say, had ever been at his command, and h-+ trusted the same</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>GENEROUS STI FLIT</head>
        <p>GENEROUS STI FLIT</p>
        <p>would continue to preveil,-1717- could assure his colleagues—if they needed any such assurance—that their interests had always been studied by him, and he trusted would continue so to be. He could not con- clude his report without alluding to the confidence which the governing body lar.d placed in his administra- tion of the school. So far as his memory served him, everything had passed off very happily during the past twelve months. He thought he could say his best services had been at the disposal of the foundation, and he could assure them they always would be. (Applause ) It but remained for him, on behalf of the school, to express their appreciation of the kindness of the Lord Bishop of Liverpool in coming there to address them and to present the prizes. His Lordship had previously shown his interest in more ways than one. fie rnight refer to his recognition of a pupil some three years ago, who received most generous assistance from the mad which he (the Bishop) administered. He was sure the boys would realize the sacrifice of time which their Bishop had made, and would follow closely what he said to them, and lay such to heart. (Applause). The RECTOR congratulated the headmaster on the results of the year's work and assured him that the governors had the most implicit confidence in him and in the staff. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>which the school had passed. It was evident that the hsadruaster and his assistants did not throw their , strensth into a few clever boys, but soug4t to educate the whole body of boys committed to their care, and they did it with such thoroughness and conscientious- mess that the school was taking a high place among the secondary schools in Lancashire. He congratulated Warrington on having that excellent school in its midst. (Applause.) The Bishop next recalled the fact that 80111 A of our greatest men ill Church and State came from schools such as theirs. Some of the ablest Bishops on toe Bench to-day received their education in country grammar schools. Addressing himself directly to the boys, his lordship made the basis of his remarks the familiar phrase " Play the game." By playing the game they meant that they played fairly, that they never cheated, that they kept the rules and played with their whole heart. They did not play the game selfishly for themselves, but forget themselves in their side. One whom he knew very well was running aome years ago for Oxford against Cambridge. He was the University of Oxford 'second string and when the race was over he received the highest praise that could be ' bestowed upon him. He ran, it was said, so that the first string might win, lie did not attempt self; he was not sent by the University t? win him- self; to win : he was sent to make the running arid he so made it that Oxford carried off the race. Again, playing the game meant honouring their opponents and never speaking evil of them, hut speaking well of them. The true sportsman was also modest if he won. He thought it bac form to boast and he was good humoured,instead ' of sulky, if he lost. " Now boys " continued the Bishop : " What I am asking you to do is to carry those principles into your lives. All of you here—and it is a good thing—wilt have to make your way in the world, some of you in the civil service,some in business, some in great professions, some perhaps in manual labour—ever to be honoured.' But in whatever busi- ness or calling you may he, play the game. Be fair, honest, truthful and whole-hearted Do whatever you have to do as well as it can he done. John Ruskin says somewhere that honesty and truthfulness, like good writing, come by practice. You are learning in your playground and in your playing fields to be honest and truthful and whole-hearted men and to do whatever your hand finds to do with all your might. Then PLAT TIIE GAME 7N YOUR POLITICAL LIFB. You are going to be citizens of the greatest Empire in the world. Don't be sleeping citizens; be active citizens. Take some part in the politics of your country. Think your position out for yourself and whether you are a Liberal or a Conservative, a Radical or a supporter of the Labour party, throw your whole stvength iuto the cause that you take up. Sink your</p>
        <p>In lovii who died</p>
        <p>IUSINESS HOURS 9 to 8 DAIL'</p>
        <p>oerl ; self in your country. Never try to grind yonr.oi iv axe at the expense of your fatherland. May it,tr of you, as it was said of a grest statesman death, that he was a man who Down to death from youth Steered for the living star of truth Made his own country's cause his own, „se), And loved her for herself alone. (APP- As politicians, as citizens, take an interest country. Play the game. Put your country firsovioe yourself and your class second. And lastly in re v.; play the game. Put God first. All of you doubt, belong to some Christian Church. 'Re",s,4"spiss the most important part of all. Don't be si,ri members of the Church, but active members. ilof do something for the spiritual state of your `" be chinas. Think your own position carefully out ; fast to it and „ t RE TOLERANT sre to other people. The intolerant are those who 600: quite sure whether they are right or not, who are wbat uneasy about their own position. The to; bier man is the man who has carefully thought out f° told' '• self what he believes and who, having a firm is°. for the grace of God bears tolerantly with thOs`tiest differ from him. Have the courage of your coovlG,", hold them firmly and seek to spread them. the game at school, than you will play the business, in politics, and in your religious life, edto, ie eLI, result will be, you will not gain merely yonr:-„rl°.' colours or, it may be, a blue at the liniversith will gain hereafter a Crown of Life." (ApPiso" THANKING THE BISHOP. .slis ' Mr. W. P. RYLANDS, in proposing a vote of the s the Bishop, likened Speech Day to the school esoir Year's Day when the scholars should make good r tions as to the coming year's work. It was tbe a irilr syr.en those who were perhaps a little slack 6„rid fe,e should be fired with energy and enthusiasru 1,17,000; that they had examples worth following. Ocoee ' roes" Lions, if roundel on tie rock of determinatis°' ourt lead to a successful goal. The boys could, be cc,,e600: • look forward with