        <p>SANKEY V WALKER'S BREWERY.—PIayed at Orford on Saturday. Scores : b Sankey.—E Whitfield lbw b Roylance 8, Mel4rugeld Roylance 13, H Porter c and b Roylanoe 18, T W""„ b b Roylance 12, C Forster b Roylance 0, 0 Dickens(/' 0, Roylance 0, E Bullen b Roylance 4, Plant b Mettler 0, B,amhall not out 0, Daintith c Turner b Boylan° Millington not out 0, extras 8, total 63. , Walker's Brewery,—Lomas o Bramhall b Bulien„ it Roylance b Bullen I. Woods c Sullen b Millingos"ter Mather c T Whitfield b Millington 14. Leigh b 6, Coyne c and b Forster 3, Clare 11 Forster not out 3, Turner c Bramhall b Forster 0, Clarke Forster 0, extras 2, total 39.</p>
        <p>T Ellison (Whitecross) was in his usual good for .1 and easily disposed of C Smith (Cemetei y) by 21-7 in 19 ends. -- A Warburton (Whitecrms) was always a winner against A Rosterne(Cemetery)for after having led 18-10 at the 19th end he ran his woods in fine style to win 21-13 at the 25th end. -- Jack Gleave (Cemetery) pl4yed a hard game against J Haworth (Whitecross) but although he played well and on his own territory he was no match for the wire- worker, who scored well all through the game to finally run out the winner by four challis. -- T Quarrie (Cemetery) played a similar game against A Ford (Jun. Conservatives) although the junior stuck it well and it was only the advantage of the green that gave the Cemetery man a win of 21-17. The Cemetery men know every corner of the green and strangers are handicapped to a slight extent. Quarrie, however, is a good player on almost any green in the town and he fully deserved his victory, which was well earned. -- NV Simister (Cemetery) wea in a had way against W Daniels another Cemetery player when he lost 4-21 in 19 ends. -- E Taylor (Sen. Conservatives) 'ray just managed to scramble horny against J Armitage (Jun. Conservatives) by two points in a game extending over 32 ends. Taylor established a good lead early on and at the 10th end led 11-3. Armitage played the jack and bowling well peeled with 14 at the 21st end. Play was then even and at the 30th end the score was 19 each. The senior got o neat singlss on the n-xt two ends to run out 21-19. -- G Ball, juo, the Cemetery player, played a winning game against J Higham (Whitecross) and finally won 21-13, having had a good advantage from the first. The game was finished in 21 ends and the wireworker had only lauded three doubles and seven singles. -- W Bridge (Junior Conservatives) and J E Harrison (Senior Conservatives) proved a good match for they kept, close together and peeled at 5, 10, 13. 14, and at the 28th end with 18 each. A double and a single on the next ends nut Harrison in the second race where he will meet It Holbrook (Junior Conservatives). --- There are some good games to be decided to-day and , no doubt one of the most intarestiug games will be between Joe Gleave (Bulls Head) and G Warburton (Whitecross). Tie whole draw for to-day will be found in another column. GOLF. WARRING TON CLUB. The fourth round for the club prize against boyey was played on Saturday, Mr C J Smita in the first division, returning a card all square and qualifying for the final, and Mr H Dolan, in the second division, qualifying with 3up on bogey, Scores : FIRST DIVISION. Charles J Smith T B Handley Herbert Woods._ 3 u F S Horner 4 „ T I Blacichurne Rev T T Lee-Jones B El Tilston 4 „ J Hepherd 5 it John Ellison 5 3, John Fairhurst 7 If P C Garnett 9 I I SELOND DIVISION. HDolan Albeit IVarbutton 4 down. Drßower C1arke................................ EA Wtite</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION. Appointment of the Referees. A meeting of the committee of the Northern Rugby League was held in Manchester on Tuesday evening, Mr J B Cooke, of Vtakefield, prksiding. The following referees were appointed :—Lancashire : G Moore (Runcorn), .1 A Barrow (Preston), J Winatrualey (War- rington), F Mills (Oldham), .1 H Crawshaw (Salford), J Jones (Wigan), F Crabtree (Rochdale), W Gabbutt (Barrow), H Ogden (Oldham), and A Webster (Wigan), and A Webster (Wigan). Yorkshire: A Brown (Wake- field), H D Redfern (Huddersfield). G Dollman (Leeds), T Johnson (Bramley), CC Bell (Bradford), CE Heyton (Halifax). H GI Roebuck (Huddersfield), P Hardy (Huddersfield), W 0 Stewart (Hunalet), and F R \‘Rson (York). ALLEGED FORGERY IN THE ISLE OF MAN. Jane Kelly, the wife el a farmer living near Laxey, was committed for trial at Douglas, on Wednesday, on charges of forging two cheques on the Isle of Man I Bank, one for £l5 and one for £lO 15s.</p>
